window.SOUBEL_SEARCH_INDEX=[{"url":"/","title":"SOUBEL | Commercial Growth & Operational Trust","h1":"Industrial leadership. Operational trust. Measurable impact.","description":"Commercial growth, strategic accounts, industrial intelligence, integrity, asset performance, risk, and digital transformation grounded in Operational Trust.","headings":"Three ways into the work. | Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts | Asset Performance & Integrity | Digital Transformation & Industrial Intelligence | Trust has to survive execution. | Where the SOUBEL point of view meets live industrial questions. | You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. | Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard | When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines “Proven”?","text":"THE ARCHITECTURE OF OPERATIONAL TRUST Industrial leadership. Operational trust. Measurable impact. SOUBEL connects commercial strategy, industrial asset integrity, regulatory requirements, and digital capability to the operating decisions and field execution that create measurable business value. Explore SOUBEL Current Thinking EXPLORE SOUBEL Three ways into the work. Start with the question closest to the work at hand. The deeper commercial, operational, technical, regulatory, and digital material lives beyond the homepage. Commercial Growth Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts Market development, strategic accounts, business cases, value quantification, channel strategy, and the architecture required to build durable growth. Explore commercial growth → Asset Performance Asset Performance & Integrity Pipeline integrity, corrosion, cathodic protection, reliability, operational risk, and lifecycle decisions focused on understanding asset conditions, supporting better business decisions around risk, safety, reliability, performance, and investment, and strengthening field execution. Explore asset performance & integrity → Digital & Intelligence Digital Transformation & Industrial Intelligence Digital twins, AI-enabled workflows, data confidence, technology adoption, research, and intelligence translated into operational relevance. Explore digital transformation → KNOWLEDGE CREATES ITS GREATEST VALUE WHEN IT IS SHARED WITH OTHERS. OPERATIONAL TRUST Trust has to survive execution. SOUBEL focuses on whether the right information, decisions, people, and field actions stay connected from plan to execution. CURRENT THINKING Where the SOUBEL point of view meets live industrial questions. Commercial growth, operational trust, regulation, integrity, and transformation are where those principles get tested. Explore Analysis & Perspectives → Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. Why growth strategy begins with understanding the business, testing market evidence, and an honest view of what the organization can actually deliver. Read insight → Operational Trust & Integrity Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Why industrial transformation has to move beyond visibility toward information, decisions, execution, evidence, and outcomes the organization can rely on. Read insight → Pipeline Integrity & Regulation When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines “Proven”? A current regulatory question that tests what trusted, defensible engineering decision-making looks like in practice. Read insight →","type":"SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/","title":"About SOUBEL | Helen M. Dupree","h1":"SOUBEL","description":"SOUBEL is the operational intelligence platform and digital repository for Helen M. Dupree","headings":"Helen M. Dupree | Building forward from experience. | Professional Foundation | Technology & Information Systems Current Education Track | Advanced Technology & Continuous Learning Current Education Track | Continue the conversation.","text":"About SOUBEL SOUBEL is an operational intelligence platform and digital repository for Helen M. Dupree’s professional body of work. It is not a consulting firm or advisory practice. SOUBEL gives corporate leaders direct access to the strategies, frameworks, regulatory analysis, economic models, case histories, and original thinking behind Helen’s work. Executive profile Helen’s current professional profile, experience, and industry background are available on LinkedIn. View LinkedIn Profile → Service to Others is the ethic. Operational Trust™ is the standard. SOUBEL is the expression of the work. FOUNDER Helen M. Dupree Oil & Gas · Commercial Growth · Strategic Accounts Helen M. Dupree is a Houston-based commercial leader whose background is rooted in oil & gas and energy markets, with adjacent experience across utilities, critical infrastructure, and industrial technology. Her work spans strategic accounts, market development, corrosion and cathodic protection, pipeline and asset integrity, asset performance, and digital transformation. SOUBEL documents how that experience is translated into commercial strategy, operational understanding, and measurable growth. View Helen's LinkedIn profile → Explore Experience → Reading & Influence → PROFESSIONAL DEVELOPMENT & CONTINUOUS LEARNING Building forward from experience. SOUBEL reflects an established career in oil & gas, energy, commercial leadership, pipeline and asset integrity, and digital transformation, supported by continued learning in AI, data, information systems, OT/ICS, and critical infrastructure. Professional Foundation Texas A&M Industrial Distribution NACE / AMPP Cathodic Protection Miller Heiman Strategic & Conceptual Selling MEDDICC Sales Methodology SLB Digital & Commercial Development U.S. Department of Energy Hydrogen Program AMPP Industry Leadership Technology & Information Systems Current Education Track Python SQL Information Systems AI & Data Industrial & Agentic AI Microsoft Azure AWS AI Governance OT / ICS Critical Infrastructure CISA ICS ISA / IEC 62443 Advanced Technology & Continuous Learning Current Education Track DevOps Site Reliability Engineering Cloud Deployment & Infrastructure CI/CD & Modern Software Delivery Containers Monitoring & Observability MLOps Production AI Operations Industrial Digital Twins Cyber-Physical Systems SOUBEL Oil & Gas AI Lab Learning continues as technology and industries continue to evolve. FOLLOW SOUBEL Continue the conversation. SOUBEL publishes oil & gas, commercial, integrity, and digital perspectives on LinkedIn and Instagram, with the website serving as the permanent editorial home. SOUBEL on LinkedIn → SOUBEL on Instagram →","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/career-in-motion/","title":"A Career in Motion | SOUBEL","h1":"The work leaves a trail of people, places, and moments.","description":"A visual record from across the career behind SOUBEL — field work, industry communities, customer environments, travel, colleagues, and the moments that accumulate into experience.","headings":"The work leaves a trail of people, places, and moments.","text":"About · A Career in Motion A CAREER IN MOTION The work leaves a trail of people, places, and moments. A visual record from across the career behind SOUBEL — field work, industry communities, customer environments, travel, colleagues, and the ordinary moments that accumulate into experience. Back to Experience A CAREER IN MOTION The work leaves a trail of people, places, and moments. A visual record from across the career behind SOUBEL — field work, industry communities, customer environments, travel, colleagues, and the ordinary moments that accumulate into experience.","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/experience/","title":"Experience | SOUBEL","h1":"Experience that informs the work.","description":"Selected industrial commercial, corrosion, integrity, asset performance, and digital transformation experience that informs SOUBEL","headings":"Industrial products, services, software, and field organizations. | The work also has a visual record. | Different chapters. A consistent operating thread. | Build markets, accounts, channels, and revenue. | Understand the operating problem behind the sale. | Translate technology into operational value. | Connect technical capability to customer priorities. | Experience also includes helping shape the industry conversation.","text":"About · Experience EXPERIENCE Experience that informs the work. SOUBEL draws on Helen M. Dupree's career across commercial growth, strategic accounts, pipeline and asset integrity, corrosion and cathodic protection, asset performance, and digital transformation, shaped by customers, field environments, industry relationships, and the work of connecting technical capability to commercial and operational value. Career context A Career in Motion CAREER CONTEXT Industrial products, services, software, and field organizations. SLB Asset Performance · Pipeline Integrity · Strategic Accounts · Digital Twin · Industrial Software · AI As Asset Performance Domain Champion, advised enterprise and account leaders across North America and Europe on strategic growth, customer positioning, pipeline and asset integrity, and the application of asset-performance technologies to complex operational and business challenges. The role connected technical domain expertise, commercial strategy, executive engagement, and account development across major industrial customers. De Nora Commercial Leadership · Channel Strategy · Cathodic Protection · MMO Anodes Led commercial growth across the Americas for corrosion-control technologies, revitalizing market development, strengthening distributor channels, expanding customer engagement, and helping translate technical innovation into revenue-producing market opportunities. Integrated Corrosion Companies Vice President · Organizational Change · Commercial Growth · Pipeline Integrity · Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Recruited as Vice President to help lead company-wide change, with responsibility spanning commercial strategy, operational improvement, market development, organizational alignment, pipeline integrity, and field execution, with a focus on increasing revenue, improving profitability, and strengthening the delivery of corrosion-control products and services. MATCOR and earlier industrial roles Corrosion & Cathodic Protection · Market Development · Strategic Accounts · Pipeline Integrity · Infrastructure Built foundational experience in corrosion and cathodic protection, market development, strategic account growth, pipeline integrity, infrastructure services, and field-oriented commercial leadership, including helping expand MATCOR during a significant period of company growth and developing long-term customer relationships across the energy and industrial sectors. A CAREER IN MOTION The work also has a visual record. Field work, industry communities, customer environments, colleagues, travel, and ordinary moments that accumulated into experience are collected separately so this page can stay focused on the professional through-line. Explore A Career in Motion WHAT THIS EXPERIENCE BRINGS FORWARD Different chapters. A consistent operating thread. COMMERCIAL GROWTH Build markets, accounts, channels, and revenue. Growth work begins with market evidence, customer reality, disciplined discovery, and a clear understanding of what the organization can actually deliver. CORROSION & INTEGRITY Understand the operating problem behind the sale. Commercial decisions are stronger when equipment, procedures, field execution, records, risk, and asset outcomes remain connected. DIGITAL & ASSET PERFORMANCE Translate technology into operational value. Digital capability matters when it improves decisions, execution, adoption, reliability, risk, or another measurable operating outcome. STRATEGIC ACCOUNTS Connect technical capability to customer priorities. Complex opportunities are built through relationships, technical discovery, internal alignment, business cases, and evidence gathered over time. INDUSTRY LEADERSHIP Experience also includes helping shape the industry conversation. AMPP Longstanding involvement in corrosion and materials-protection communities, including leadership within digital-transformation work. Continuing technical learning Ongoing study across AI, cloud, analytics, asset performance, risk, regulation, and industrial operations keeps commercial interpretation connected to changing technical capability. Public writing SOUBEL's Knowledge Library, Analysis & Perspectives, and The Integrity Shift turn accumulated experience into public, source-conscious industrial interpretation.","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/reading-influence/","title":"Reading & Influence | SOUBEL","h1":"What stays with us shapes how we think.","description":"Books, people, experiences, and earlier writing that shaped Helen M. Dupree’s thinking about leadership, growth, judgment, risk, technology, and people.","headings":"Two ways influence stays with us. | Earlier writing. Enduring lessons. | Ideas worth carrying forward. | Not every influence comes with a title or an ISBN.","text":"About · Reading & Influence What stays with us shapes how we think. Books, people, experience, and earlier writing that have shaped Helen M. Dupree’s thinking about leadership, growth, judgment, risk, technology, history, and human behavior. “And when I am forgotten, as I shall be ... say I taught thee.” William Shakespeare · Henry VIII , Act 3, Scene 2 The line appeared at the top of Helen’s 2018 thought piece The Inheritance . It captures the purpose of this collection: not simply what was read or experienced, but what remained and continued to shape judgment. Source: Folger Shakespeare Library → EXPLORE THE COLLECTION Two ways influence stays with us. Earlier writing captures lessons as they were lived. Books preserve ideas that continue to return long after the last page. THOUGHT PIECES Earlier writing. Enduring lessons. Gumbo? , The Inheritance , and Where’s My Bike? gathered together as one collection of earlier writing. Explore Thought Pieces → BOOKS THAT STAYED Ideas worth carrying forward. Books that continued to influence how Helen thinks about disciplined growth, scale, value, relationships, and leadership. Explore Books That Stayed → PEOPLE & IDEAS Not every influence comes with a title or an ISBN. Some lessons come from people, conversations, customers, mistakes, and moments that change how a person sees the work. This collection will grow over time as Helen records the people and ideas that stayed with her.","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/reading-influence/books/","title":"Books That Stayed | Reading & Influence | SOUBEL","h1":"Books that stayed.","description":"Books that have continued to shape Helen M. Dupree’s thinking about disciplined growth, scalable thinking, value, relationships, and legacy.","headings":"Create something extraordinary. Believe in incredible transformations. Never forget that business is ultimately between people. Build a legacy worth carrying forward. | Good to Great | The Science of Scaling | The Go-Giver | Build a Business You Love | Your Values-Based Legacy | Earlier writing. Enduring lessons.","text":"About · Reading & Influence · Books That Stayed Books that stayed. Books whose ideas continue to surface in the way Helen M. Dupree thinks about growth, leadership, value, relationships, scale, and legacy. BOOKS THAT STAYED → Create something extraordinary. Believe in incredible transformations. Never forget that business is ultimately between people. Build a legacy worth carrying forward. Some books are useful in the moment. Others keep showing up years later in the way a person thinks, leads, sells, builds, and makes decisions. BOOK COVER Good to Great Jim Collins DISCIPLINED GROWTH Good to Great Jim Collins In 2010, when Helen joined MATCOR, then-CEO Jeff Stello gave every employee a copy of Good to Great . It became more than a business book. It helped establish an enduring belief that sustainable growth is not created by chasing the next opportunity, but through disciplined choices about people, focus, execution, and what a company is capable of becoming. What stayed: Great companies are built deliberately. Growth without discipline is simply activity. BOOK COVER The Science of Scaling Dr. Benjamin Hardy & Blake Erickson SCALABLE THINKING The Science of Scaling Dr. Benjamin Hardy & Blake Erickson The Science of Scaling challenged Helen to think beyond incremental growth. Its lasting idea is that meaningful scale does not necessarily come from doing more of what already works. Sometimes it requires redesigning the model, eliminating what cannot scale, and setting a goal large enough to demand a fundamentally different way of thinking. What stayed: A bigger future often requires a different system, not simply more effort. BOOK COVER The Go-Giver Bob Burg & John David Mann VALUE & RELATIONSHIPS The Go-Giver Bob Burg & John David Mann Some business books teach strategy. The Go-Giver is a reminder of what business is ultimately for. Its principles of value, influence, and authenticity reinforce a belief Helen has carried throughout her career: some of the strongest commercial relationships begin when the objective stops being the transaction and becomes helping another person succeed. What stayed: Value comes before reward. Trust and influence are earned through what one contributes. BOOK COVER Build a Business You Love Dave Ramsey INSTITUTIONAL LEGACY Build a Business You Love Dave Ramsey Build a Business You Love maps five stages of business growth, culminating in Stage 5: the Legacy Builder. What makes that idea important here is the shift from a business sustained by daily hustle to one built around structure, people, systems, and leadership capable of carrying the work forward beyond the founder’s direct involvement. What stayed: A business becomes a legacy when its purpose and capability can continue without depending on one person to hold it together. BOOK COVER Your Values-Based Legacy Harry M. Jansen Kraemer, Jr. VALUES-BASED LEGACY Your Values-Based Legacy Harry M. Jansen Kraemer, Jr. Your Values-Based Legacy moves the idea of legacy beyond enterprise continuity. Kraemer focuses on the continuum of legacy and challenges leaders to consider how influence, resources, and responsibility can be used in service of something larger than personal success. That broader view gives the book its place here: what endures is not only what was built, but the values and impact carried forward through the people and institutions touched along the way. What stayed: Legacy is not only what remains. It is what others are able to carry forward because of the values, choices, and contribution that came before them. This collection is intentionally unfinished. Additional books will be added as Helen identifies the ideas that genuinely stayed with her. The collection does not need to be narrowly professional to belong here. CONTINUE EXPLORING Earlier writing. Enduring lessons. The same ideas also appear in Helen’s earlier writing, shaped through customers, family, setbacks, persistence, and experience. Thought Pieces → Reading & Influence →","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/reading-influence/gumbo/","title":"Gumbo? | Reading & Influence | SOUBEL","h1":"Gumbo?","description":"A story about persistence, trust, and learning that access to an account cannot always be won by a better pitch.","headings":"Continue exploring","text":"Ideas That Built a Career · Commercial Reflection Gumbo? A story about persistence, trust, and learning that access to an account cannot always be won by a better pitch. By Helen M. Dupree · Originally published February 3, 2018 Seafood gumbo accompanying Helen’s 2018 thought piece. “I don't need to meet you and I know all about the company you're working for.” How naive I was. How completely unprepared in my arrogance of success, was I for this answer. It was unequivocally “no”. As many times as I tried, the answer was always identical. And year after year, the answer remained. What I couldn't see on the other side of my pitch , was a company man completely satisfied with the products he was using every year and no desire for change. He didn't need a salesman or the person on the other end of the relentless phone calls. He didn't need new products or services. Many managers or sales experts would say I just hadn't found his pain threshold, or identified the value proposition which would move him off of “no” and allow me to garner a meeting and subsequent purchase order. And in a way, they would be almost correct. Years went by and I made the move to a new company. As I started making announcements and phone calls... there he was... staring back at me from my list... taunting me. “No, not interested, another time, maybe next year” , etc. I could only imagine how this call was going to go or how many more years he would refuse me! But this tête-à-tête had become a challenge... like going to New Orleans on a diet staring down a beautifully powdered pile of hot beignets... oh no... we're gonna jump right in this alligator infested river again Helen! And so the phone rings... “If you're down this way, sure. Stop by.” THUD! I think after I came to, I danced around my office waving my arms in the air, yelling “Yo Adrienne... I did it!!!” (obligatory Rocky reference). You see, what I had to learn outside of sales theories and strategies and everything I thought I knew, was that he had a loyal business network and base of which I was not a part. I had to earn my place. We discussed our mutual love of fishing and cooking great food. I told him about the big pot of étouffée I made every year and how, just recently, I had cooked the most delicious seafood gumbo this side of Louisiana (insert pain threshold here) . He smiled a big smile and looked at me sideways like I had just told a great big whopper of a fish tale. But I confirmed... there wasn't any finer gumbo to be had and promised...one day...to bring him a pot. (insert value proposition) (Insert here, a value higher than any purchase order.) Today, he is one of my most valuable and trusted mentors and, more importantly, someone I consider a wonderful friend and confidant. And yes, he utilizes all my products on his projects. These ten years later, I find myself with a big smile on my face at every chance communication, as the pat answer is always... “Gumbo?” (Insert here, a value higher than any purchase order.) -Helen Dupree THE SOUBEL LENS Gumbo? is a story about trust. In industrial markets, strong relationships are not built from sales plans alone. They are built by showing up, being useful, keeping your word, and taking the time to understand the people behind the organization. SOUBEL republication based on Helen’s original LinkedIn article, preserved from the supplied screenshots with web formatting normalized. Continue exploring Reading & Influence → About SOUBEL → Analysis & Perspectives →","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/reading-influence/the-inheritance/","title":"The Inheritance | Reading & Influence | SOUBEL","h1":"The Inheritance","description":"A reflection on the lessons a father leaves behind, not as possessions, but as habits of mind, standards, stories, and ways of seeing the world.","headings":"Continue exploring","text":"Ideas That Built a Career · Thought Piece The Inheritance A reflection on the lessons a father leaves behind, not as possessions, but as habits of mind, standards, stories, and ways of seeing the world. By Helen M. Dupree · Originally published December 29, 2018 Restored photograph accompanying Helen’s 2018 thought piece. “And when I am forgotten, as I shall be ... say I taught thee.” Henry VIII , William Shakespeare September 24, 2001... the front of the house was loud, some laughter, some crying... a young man, still in his sunglasses, sobbing, retelling the story about the time my father refused to take his money after betting on the Saints because it was his crop seed money... the crowds of mourners paying their respects to the family. I retreated to the master bedroom looking for some piece of him to carry with me through the coming days and weeks. On the bedside table was the deck of cards he mindlessly played solitaire with for hours - life's lesson about odds, certainty... clearing your mind. And as I reached into his pants pocket, it was still there. The treasure I had adored all my years as his daughter - a small stone of green jade, a worry stone, if you will, that he rubbed while most, unknowingly, would have just thought he was jingling coins... the lesson that things may not always be what they seem but it is critical to maintain your composure even if your world is falling apart. Next to his handkerchief lay a beautiful German coat brush he used to maintain such a coiffed appearance, it was all in the details; life too. The backgammon set - surely it was only luck which would determine the winner, by the simple roll of the dice? Not exactly. The repeated mathematical computations expected to be put to memory... I mean why not? Preparation, strategy and planning will always prevail. The poetry of Robert Service, Keats, Shelley, all handed to me to be recited was far greater than the mere printed words on paper. All of it was about emotion, love, loss. Being real. Being prepared to step outside my comfort zone in a moment's notice. The last bottle of wine he gave me so I could be a great hostess. All the lessons he lived and taught resound in my head most days and it was the greatest inheritance he could have ever given me. And the marble ball? “A sculptor wields the chisel, and the stricken marble grows... to beauty.” -Helen Merrifield Dupree THE SOUBEL LENS The Inheritance is about the habits that stay with us: preparation, composure, attention to detail, curiosity, and the way lessons learned early can shape judgment much later. SOUBEL republication based on Helen’s original LinkedIn article. The opening Shakespeare quotation is presented here using the verified wording from the Folger Shakespeare Library; the body has otherwise been preserved as supplied in Helen’s screenshots. The closing marble quotation is attributed to William Cullen Bryant, The Flood of Years . Continue exploring Reading & Influence → About SOUBEL → Analysis & Perspectives →","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/reading-influence/thought-pieces/","title":"Thought Pieces | Reading & Influence | SOUBEL","h1":"Earlier writing. Enduring lessons.","description":"Earlier writing by Helen M. Dupree on trust, persistence, preparation, judgment, relationships, and enduring lessons.","headings":"Ideas that built a career. → | The Inheritance | Gumbo? | Where’s My Bike? | Books that stayed.","text":"About · Reading & Influence · Thought Pieces Earlier writing. Enduring lessons. Three earlier pieces on trust, persistence, preparation, judgment, relationships, and the lessons people leave behind. THOUGHT PIECES Ideas that built a career. → Long before SOUBEL, Helen was writing about many of the ideas that continue to shape the work today. These pieces were written years apart and for very different reasons. Together, they show how experience accumulates and eventually becomes a way of thinking. WHY THESE PIECES ARE HERE These stories show the human side of professional judgment: how trust is earned, how people respond under pressure, and how lessons from work and life continue to shape decisions years later. DECEMBER 29, 2018 · THOUGHT PIECE The Inheritance On what people leave behind, and the lessons that outlast them. Playing cards. Jade. A coat brush. Backgammon. Poetry. Wine. A marble. Ordinary objects became reminders of preparation, probability, composure, hospitality, curiosity, attention to detail, and the ways one generation quietly shapes another. Read The Inheritance → FEBRUARY 3, 2018 · COMMERCIAL REFLECTION Gumbo? On trust, persistence, and earning a place in the relationship. A difficult account. A relationship that could not be forced. And a reminder that sometimes the most meaningful commercial progress is not a purchase order at all. Read Gumbo? → MAY 4, 2019 · PERSONAL REFLECTION Where’s My Bike? On perseverance when life refuses to cooperate with the plan. Work, family, uncertainty, exhaustion, broken plans, and one week when almost everything seemed to arrive at once. Sometimes resilience is not heroic. Sometimes it is simply deciding what has to be handled next and continuing forward. Read Where’s My Bike? → CONTINUE EXPLORING Books that stayed. Some influences arrive through experience. Others arrive through a book and keep resurfacing years later. Books That Stayed → Reading & Influence →","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/about/reading-influence/wheres-my-bike/","title":"Where’s My Bike? | Reading & Influence | SOUBEL","h1":"Where’s My Bike?","description":"A difficult week, an old bicycle, and a reflection on the need to keep moving when work, family, uncertainty, and ordinary life pile up at once.","headings":"Continue exploring","text":"Ideas That Built a Career · Personal Reflection Where’s My Bike? A difficult week, an old bicycle, and a reflection on the need to keep moving when work, family, uncertainty, and ordinary life pile up at once. By Helen M. Dupree · Originally published May 4, 2019 Vintage bicycle image accompanying Helen’s 2019 thought piece. “Life is like riding a bicycle. To keep your balance, you must keep moving.” Albert Einstein Six a.m., and I open the back door to see what kind of damage the storms left me with last night. But the morning breeze, the sunshine, the little squirrels out running the fence... all greeted me and it was just beautiful. It reminded me of home. It reminded me of being a kid and how on Saturday's, I absolutely could not wait to run outside, jump on my bike and be gone. My beautiful Schwinn Varsity 10 speed which was like riding on a cloud, in circles around our neighborhood, for hours on end. My bike was the one way I could escape Mother's laundry list of chores she had us tackle every day, but it was Saturday. And if I left the house early enough, well... guess who wouldn't be cleaning the pool or raking the yard or scrubbing the sidewalk with a toothbrush... not me! Not today. Today was about freedom from school, freedom from chores, freedom from my little brother. Peace was the goal. It was just me and my bike. See ya! Reminiscing over coffee brought me to a conversation I had last night. You see, it was a week...and I mean one of THOSE weeks. You know exactly what I'm talking about. The pressure from work, trade shows, spreadsheets, phone calls, etc., is overwhelming and you forget to order your plane ticket for next week. A “right out of the shop” repaired car gives a drivetrain malfunction code and there's fresh oil on the driveway. The divorce will hopefully and finally happen after almost three years of waiting. Both daughters headed to prom... dresses, shoes, flowers, tickets, prom t-shirts, makeup, jewelry... name it... you're going to buy it. And as the night wears on, you're alone and it brings you to the point of breaking. It is unbearable. “Honor and duty must always come first. Yes, unfortunately”. And I felt bad to have complained because my dear friend was having the exact same kind of week. It is 2 a.m. It is dark and the rain is so loud you can't hear yourself think. The tornado warnings and the thought of pending destruction and doom make it hard to rest. The stresses and the anxiety of what's left to do to make your job, your children, your life... successful, it will break you if you're not careful. And it is during those times, you absolutely have to find the will to keep moving. What is the vehicle of perseverance to overcome all that you are faced with? Why should any one person have to deal with so much?? Because its life and life happens and this morning... as the breeze blew in the back door, I smiled and asked myself... “where's my bike?” -Helen Merrifield Dupree THE SOUBEL LENS Where’s My Bike? is about continuing when several things go wrong at once. Resilience is not always dramatic. Sometimes it is finding enough perspective to handle what comes next and keep moving. SOUBEL republication based on Helen’s original LinkedIn article. The familiar English bicycle line is a polished rendering of an idea Einstein wrote to his son Eduard in 1930; the thought piece is preserved here as originally framed by Helen, with web formatting normalized. Continue exploring Reading & Influence → About SOUBEL → Analysis & Perspectives →","type":"About SOUBEL"},{"url":"/analysis-perspectives/","title":"SOUBEL Analysis & Perspectives | Original Analysis & Commentary","h1":"Perspectives on Oil & Gas, Growth, Integrity, and Transformation","description":"Original SOUBEL analysis, commentary, and perspective across commercial growth, industrial operations, integrity, regulation, Operational Trust, and digital transformation.","headings":"AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. | Published analysis across commercial, operational, regulatory, and digital questions. | You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. | You Don’t Transform Revenue by Hiring for Yesterday’s Job | Don’t Confuse Altitude with Influence | When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines Proven? | PHMSA’s April 2026 — Operational Trust™ Is the Next Standard | There’s Something Interesting Happening Across Industrial Operations Right Now | The Integrity Shift | Seven connected pieces. One operating thesis.","text":"ANALYSIS & PERSPECTIVES Perspectives on Oil & Gas, Growth, Integrity, and Transformation Original analysis and commentary across oil & gas, commercial growth, pipeline and asset integrity, Operational Trust™, regulation, digital transformation, and adjacent energy infrastructure. Browse by subject Commercial Growth Operational Trust™ & Integrity Digital Transformation & AI Pipeline Integrity & Regulation FEATURED ARTICLE DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION & AI · SEPTEMBER 2, 2026 AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. Industrial AI can move faster than the operating systems, data structures, and decision processes underneath it. Faster analytics do not automatically create better decisions. Read the article → RECENT ARTICLES Published analysis across commercial, operational, regulatory, and digital questions. COMMERCIAL GROWTH You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. Growth strategy starts with listening, market validation, account reality, operating constraints, and what customers will actually fund. COMMERCIAL TRANSFORMATION You Don’t Transform Revenue by Hiring for Yesterday’s Job Future revenue requires roles, resources, expectations, and measures of success designed for the future revenue model. STRATEGIC ACCOUNTS Don’t Confuse Altitude with Influence The real question is not how high to sell. It is where the buying event begins and how the decision moves through the organization. PIPELINE REGULATION When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines Proven? A closer look at predictive methods, defensibility, evidence quality, and the practical meaning of “proven.” PIPELINE REGULATION · OPERATIONAL TRUST™ PHMSA’s April 2026 — Operational Trust™ Is the Next Standard Remote sensing, remote monitoring, integrity-management-based compliance, and traceability point toward a more operationally focused digital future. INDUSTRIAL OPERATIONS There’s Something Interesting Happening Across Industrial Operations Right Now The differentiator is shifting away from technology alone and toward operational understanding that people can trust and use. SIGNATURE SERIES The Integrity Shift A connected series examining asset life, Operational Trust™, trusted data, operational maturity, AI readiness, and what happens when transformation meets operating reality. Seven connected pieces. One operating thesis. The Integrity Shift brings together some of the clearest expressions of the SOUBEL point of view on evidence, confidence, asset condition, decision quality, and execution. Explore the full series → 01 The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life 02 Operational Trust™ Is Becoming the New Standard 03 Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions 04 The Confidence Gap 05 Enterprise Readiness for AI 06 When Transformation Meets Operational Reality Reflection The Integrity Shift: A Reflection on the Series Looking for source material? The Knowledge Library holds technical references, regulatory material, research, and supporting sources. Industry Intelligence tracks what is changing. Analysis & Perspectives is where SOUBEL publishes original interpretation and commentary. Explore the Knowledge Library →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/ask-soubel/","title":"Ask SOUBEL | Operational Trust","h1":"Ask SOUBEL","description":"Ask SOUBEL is the developing public question-and-answer environment for corrosion, pipeline integrity, and related industrial knowledge.","headings":"","text":"IN DEVELOPMENT Ask SOUBEL Ask SOUBEL is being developed as a public educational question-and-answer environment for corrosion, pipeline integrity, and related industrial knowledge. The experience will emphasize plain-language explanations, cited source trails, and clear separation between established information and material that still requires review. This page is intentionally being held as a working destination while the public experience is completed.","type":"SOUBEL"},{"url":"/expertise/asset-integrity-management/","title":"Asset Integrity Management | SOUBEL","h1":"Operational trust, applied across the asset lifecycle.","description":"SOUBEL connects asset condition, technical evidence, operating context, decisions, field execution, verification, digital capability and learning across the asset lifecycle.","headings":"Trust is built through continuity. | AIM extends across the asset system. | Long-life linear assets and associated facilities. | Storage, transfer, containment, and supporting systems. | Condition and operating context in one system. | Integrity across connected physical systems. | Every integrity decision should leave the organization knowing more. | Condition, threat, consequence, uncertainty. | Gather the evidence the question requires. | Make the decision basis visible. | Carry intent into the field. | Strengthen the next decision. | Connected information can strengthen every stage of AIM. | Go deeper where the asset or decision requires it. | Where AIM applies | How reasoning survives the handoff | The management system around integrity | Integrity knowledge across the life of the asset | Match the evidence to the integrity question | Technical pipeline depth | Corrosion-control context | SOUBEL Resources | Can the organization explain what it knew, why it decided, what it did, what the field confirmed, and what changed as a result?","text":"Expertise ASSET INTEGRITY MANAGEMENT Operational trust, applied across the asset lifecycle. SOUBEL views Asset Integrity Management as the discipline of keeping asset condition, technical evidence, operating context, decisions, field execution, verification, and learning connected over time. Explore the AIM framework Explore AIM Technical Knowledge AIM + OPERATIONAL TRUST Trust is built through continuity. Inspection, assessment, engineering judgment, operations, maintenance, remediation, and field execution each contribute essential knowledge about an asset. AIM connects those contributions so the reasoning behind a decision remains visible as work moves across teams, systems, contractors, and time. Condition → Evidence → Decision → Execution → Verification → Learning APPLICATION DOMAINS AIM extends across the asset system. The technical questions vary by asset class. The management discipline remains centered on condition, context, evidence, decision quality, execution, verification, and learning. PIPELINES & TRANSMISSION Long-life linear assets and associated facilities. Pipeline systems, compressor and pump infrastructure, metering, crossings, corrosion-control systems, and related assets. TERMINALS, TANK FARMS & STORAGE Storage, transfer, containment, and supporting systems. Tanks, terminal piping, loading and unloading infrastructure, pumps, containment, monitoring, and associated facilities. PROCESS FACILITIES & FIXED EQUIPMENT Condition and operating context in one system. Process piping, pressure-containing equipment, vessels, pumps, compressors, structures, and other critical facility assets. INDUSTRIAL & ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE Integrity across connected physical systems. Infrastructure supporting production, processing, transportation, storage, utilities, and energy delivery. The asset may change. The management discipline remains: understand condition, preserve context, make a defensible decision, execute it well, and carry the result forward. Explore Application Domains THE AIM DECISION CYCLE Every integrity decision should leave the organization knowing more. 01 · UNDERSTAND Condition, threat, consequence, uncertainty. Establish what is known about the asset and the operating context around it. 02 · ASSESS Gather the evidence the question requires. Use inspection, testing, monitoring, history, field evidence, and qualified analysis appropriately. 03 · DECIDE Make the decision basis visible. Determine what the evidence supports, why the response is appropriate, and who owns the action. 04 · EXECUTE & VERIFY Carry intent into the field. Confirm what was found, what was performed, and whether the intended result was achieved. 05 · LEARN & CARRY FORWARD Strengthen the next decision. Preserve findings, reasoning, outcomes, and lessons in the asset record. DIGITAL ENABLEMENT Connected information can strengthen every stage of AIM. Integrity software, connected asset data, condition monitoring, analytics, modeling, digital twins, and artificial intelligence can bring together information that historically lived across separate systems, teams, and records. Integrity Software · Connected Data · Digital Twins · Analytics & AI Their value depends on the quality, context, traceability, and qualified judgment behind the information they use. Explore Digital Transformation & AI EXPLORE DEEPER Go deeper where the asset or decision requires it. APPLICATION DOMAINS Where AIM applies Pipelines, terminals, tank farms, process facilities, fixed equipment, and industrial infrastructure. EVIDENCE TO EXECUTION How reasoning survives the handoff Follow technical meaning from evidence and decision through field work, verification, and learning. GOVERNANCE & ASSURANCE The management system around integrity Explore competency, decision authority, procedures, traceability, verification, assurance, and learning. ASSET LIFECYCLE Integrity knowledge across the life of the asset Follow design intent, commissioning, operation, assessment, modification, life extension, and decommissioning. INTEGRITY METHODS & DECISION INPUTS Match the evidence to the integrity question See how inspection, NDE, corrosion management, risk, engineering assessment, metallurgy, monitoring, and digital systems contribute to decisions. PIPELINE INTEGRITY KNOWLEDGE Technical pipeline depth ILI, pressure testing, direct assessment, field validation, corrosion, data, risk, and remediation. CORROSION & CP KNOWLEDGE Corrosion-control context Explore corrosion mechanisms, cathodic protection, field evidence, monitoring, and decision context. DECISION TOOLS & GUIDES SOUBEL Resources Practical tools for asset-condition, lifecycle, reliability, maintenance, metallurgy, and integrity decisions. THE DECISION TEST Can the organization explain what it knew, why it decided, what it did, what the field confirmed, and what changed as a result? Asset Integrity Management strengthens Operational Trust when technical evidence remains connected to decisions, execution, verification, and the knowledge carried forward. Explore the Knowledge Library Open SOUBEL Resources","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/asset-integrity-management/application-domains/","title":"AIM Application Domains | SOUBEL","h1":"Asset Integrity Management across the industrial asset system.","description":"SOUBEL applies Asset Integrity Management thinking across pipelines, terminals, tank farms, process facilities, fixed equipment and industrial infrastructure.","headings":"Integrity decisions begin with an understanding of the threat. | Pipelines and associated facilities | Explore Pipeline Integrity | Storage and transfer assets create a connected integrity context. | Storage, transfer, and containment | The facility operates as a system. | Condition, process, and operating context belong in the same conversation. | Fixed and supporting equipment | Multiple inputs shape the decision. | Integrity extends across connected physical systems. | Infrastructure that supports operations | Asset Performance | The asset may change. The management discipline remains. | Connected information can strengthen integrity decisions across every domain. | Individual assets belong to larger operating systems.","text":"Asset Integrity Management APPLICATION DOMAINS Asset Integrity Management across the industrial asset system. SOUBEL applies AIM thinking across multiple industrial environments, recognizing that asset types, degradation mechanisms, inspection methods, standards, operating conditions, and technical authorities vary while the need for decision continuity remains. Explore the domains Return to AIM PIPELINES & TRANSMISSION SYSTEMS Integrity decisions begin with an understanding of the threat. Pipeline systems combine long service lives, changing operating histories, varied environments, associated facilities, and distributed condition information. Integrity decisions may draw on inspection and assessment results, corrosion-control history, operating conditions, material properties, previous repairs, direct examination, testing, monitoring, and consequence information. REPRESENTATIVE ASSETS Pipelines and associated facilities Gathering and transmission pipelines, compressor and pump stations, metering and regulation facilities, crossings, corrosion-control systems, and related infrastructure. TECHNICAL KNOWLEDGE Explore Pipeline Integrity Assessment, ILI, pressure testing, direct assessment, field validation, corrosion, metallurgy, remediation, data, risk, and regulatory context. TERMINALS, TANK FARMS & STORAGE Storage and transfer assets create a connected integrity context. Terminals and tank farms bring together storage, piping, containment, transfer equipment, loading systems, corrosion-control systems, instrumentation, operations, inspection, maintenance, and environmental responsibilities. Condition in one part of the facility can shape the integrity decision elsewhere. REPRESENTATIVE ASSETS Storage, transfer, and containment Aboveground storage tanks, terminal and transfer piping, loading and unloading systems, pumps, secondary containment, corrosion-control and monitoring systems, and supporting infrastructure. DECISION CONTEXT The facility operates as a system. Inspection findings, corrosion history, settlement, operating service, repairs, coatings, material condition, maintenance history, and changing requirements can all influence the response. PROCESS FACILITIES & FIXED EQUIPMENT Condition, process, and operating context belong in the same conversation. Process facilities contain interconnected assets whose integrity can depend on materials, pressure, temperature, process chemistry, degradation mechanisms, operating history, inspection findings, maintenance, and changes in service. REPRESENTATIVE ASSETS Fixed and supporting equipment Process piping, pressure-containing equipment, vessels, heat-transfer equipment, pumps, compressors, structural systems, and other critical facility assets. CONNECTED DISCIPLINES Multiple inputs shape the decision. Inspection and NDE, corrosion management, materials and metallurgy, mechanical integrity, fitness for service, risk-based inspection, operating limits, maintenance history, and engineering assessment may all contribute. INDUSTRIAL & ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE Integrity extends across connected physical systems. Industrial operations depend on networks of physical assets supporting production, processing, transportation, storage, utilities, and energy delivery. The integrity question can involve dependencies between assets, changing operating requirements, maintenance priorities, reliability, consequence, and aging infrastructure. REPRESENTATIVE SYSTEMS Infrastructure that supports operations Utility and energy systems, industrial piping and distribution systems, supporting structures and facilities, mechanical equipment, monitoring and control infrastructure, and other critical supporting assets. CONNECTED EXPERTISE Asset Performance Explore the relationship between condition, reliability, maintenance, operating effectiveness, and lifecycle value. ONE MANAGEMENT DISCIPLINE The asset may change. The management discipline remains. Understand condition. Preserve context. Gather the right evidence. Make a defensible decision. Execute it well. Carry the result forward. Condition → Context → Evidence → Decision → Execution → Learning DIGITAL ENABLEMENT Connected information can strengthen integrity decisions across every domain. Integrity software, connected asset data, monitoring systems, analytics, digital twins, modeling, and AI can help bring together information that historically lived across separate systems, teams, and records. Their value grows when asset identity, data quality, operating context, traceability, validation, and qualified judgment remain connected. Explore Digital Transformation & AI Explore Evidence to Execution ASSET INTEGRITY MANAGEMENT Individual assets belong to larger operating systems. SOUBEL connects asset-specific evidence to the wider operating context, decision process, execution, verification, and learning that support Operational Trust. Return to Asset Integrity Management Open the Knowledge Library","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/asset-integrity-management/asset-lifecycle/","title":"AIM Across the Asset Lifecycle | SOUBEL","h1":"Integrity knowledge should grow with the asset.","description":"SOUBEL explores how Asset Integrity Management preserves design intent, condition knowledge, operating history, decisions, verification and learning across the full asset lifecycle.","headings":"Every stage creates evidence for the next. | The integrity question changes as the asset changes. | What was the asset designed, built, and commissioned to do? | What has changed during service? | What evidence supports the next lifecycle decision? | Asset history is more useful when the reasoning stays attached. | Preserve the starting assumptions. | Keep physical condition connected to service. | Carry the basis for major changes forward. | Let completed work improve future decisions. | Lifecycle thinking connects directly to execution and governance. | Carry technical meaning into the work | Keep ownership and decision authority visible | Compare credible paths forward | Can today’s decision be understood in the context of how the asset was designed, operated, changed, and previously assessed?","text":"Asset Integrity Management AIM ACROSS THE ASSET LIFECYCLE Integrity knowledge should grow with the asset. From design intent and commissioning through operation, maintenance, modification, life extension, and decommissioning, Asset Integrity Management depends on carrying forward what the organization has learned about the asset. Explore the lifecycle Return to AIM LIFECYCLE CONTINUITY Every stage creates evidence for the next. Design basis, materials, construction records, commissioning data, operating history, inspection findings, repairs, modifications, and field verification each add to the organization’s understanding of the asset. Their value increases when that history remains connected over time. Design Intent → Build & Commission → Operate & Maintain → Inspect & Assess → Modify or Extend Life → Decommission THREE LIFECYCLE QUESTIONS The integrity question changes as the asset changes. ESTABLISH THE BASELINE What was the asset designed, built, and commissioned to do? Design intent, materials, fabrication and construction history, baseline condition, testing, commissioning records, and early operating information establish the starting point for future integrity decisions. OPERATE, ASSESS & ADAPT What has changed during service? Operating conditions, maintenance, inspection, monitoring, degradation, repairs, field findings, and changing service demands build the condition history used to assess risk and determine action. MODIFY, EXTEND OR RETIRE What evidence supports the next lifecycle decision? Modifications, changes in service, life-extension decisions, major repairs, replacement, and decommissioning should reflect both current condition and the history that produced it. THE CONTINUITY RECORD Asset history is more useful when the reasoning stays attached. A lifecycle record gains value when it preserves more than dates and completed tasks. It should help future reviewers understand what changed, which evidence mattered, why decisions were made, what the field confirmed, and how the asset’s operating context evolved. DESIGN & MATERIAL BASIS Preserve the starting assumptions. Materials, configuration, design conditions, construction details, testing, and commissioning information establish the technical context inherited by later decisions. CONDITION & OPERATING HISTORY Keep physical condition connected to service. Inspection, monitoring, maintenance, process or operating changes, corrosion-control information, repairs, and field observations become more useful when viewed together. CHANGE & DECISION HISTORY Carry the basis for major changes forward. Modifications, altered service, risk decisions, deferred work, mitigation, and life-extension choices should remain understandable after the people who made them have moved on. VERIFICATION & LEARNING Let completed work improve future decisions. Field verification, closure evidence, lessons learned, and reassessment results should strengthen the next cycle rather than disappear into separate records. Explore Digital Transformation & AI CONNECTED AIM TOPICS Lifecycle thinking connects directly to execution and governance. EVIDENCE TO EXECUTION Carry technical meaning into the work See how evidence, decisions, field execution, verification, and learning remain connected. GOVERNANCE & ASSURANCE Keep ownership and decision authority visible Explore competency, procedures, traceability, assurance, and organizational learning. LIFECYCLE DECISION TOOL Compare credible paths forward Use the SOUBEL Lifecycle Decision Comparison resource to examine assumptions, consequences, and lifecycle implications. THE LIFECYCLE TEST Can today’s decision be understood in the context of how the asset was designed, operated, changed, and previously assessed? Lifecycle continuity strengthens Asset Integrity Management when each decision begins with the knowledge already earned by the asset. Return to Asset Integrity Management Open SOUBEL Resources","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/asset-integrity-management/evidence-to-execution/","title":"Evidence to Execution | SOUBEL","h1":"The reasoning behind a decision should survive the work.","description":"SOUBEL follows the decision chain from technical evidence through interpretation, handoff, field execution, verification and learning.","headings":"Technical evidence gains value when its meaning stays connected. | Understand what the information means. | Carry the reason with the work. | Let field reality strengthen the record. | A stronger handoff preserves the intent behind the work as well as the work itself. | What the next person needs to know | The field tests the organization’s understanding of the asset. | Resources that help carry reasoning through the work. | When Asset Condition Changes | Lifecycle Decision Comparison | Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook | Pipeline Integrity, Metallurgy & PHMSA | Technology can help preserve the decision chain. | Could the next person understand the decision without reconstructing it from fragments?","text":"Asset Integrity Management EVIDENCE TO EXECUTION The reasoning behind a decision should survive the work. Asset integrity decisions move through inspection, engineering, operations, maintenance, planning, contractors, field personnel, verification, and records. SOUBEL focuses on keeping the technical meaning connected as the work moves. Follow the decision chain Return to AIM THE DECISION CHAIN Technical evidence gains value when its meaning stays connected. Evidence → Interpretation → Decision → Handoff → Execution → Verification → Learning EVIDENCE & INTERPRETATION Understand what the information means. Inspection findings, monitoring data, operating history, laboratory results, field observations, assessments, and maintenance records become useful when qualified judgment places them in context. DECISION & HANDOFF Carry the reason with the work. The response, priority, ownership, assumptions, uncertainty, technical requirements, and verification expectations should remain visible as the work moves between people and systems. EXECUTION, VERIFICATION & LEARNING Let field reality strengthen the record. The field confirms, refines, or adds to the original understanding. What was found and performed should return to the asset record and improve the next decision. THE HANDOFF A stronger handoff preserves the intent behind the work as well as the work itself. Useful handoffs give the next person enough context to understand why the work exists, what evidence supports it, what remains uncertain, what the field needs to confirm, and what information must return when the work is complete. CARRY FORWARD What the next person needs to know What prompted the work, what is known, what is assumed, what authority applies, what evidence matters in the field, and what closes the decision. FIELD REALITY The field tests the organization’s understanding of the asset. Measurements, actual condition, configuration, access, materials, previous repairs, or operating constraints can add new information. A strong process carries that information back into the decision and record. PRACTICAL DECISION TOOLS Resources that help carry reasoning through the work. DECISION BASIS When Asset Condition Changes Questions for establishing what changed, what evidence supports the conclusion, what uncertainty remains, and what decision is being made. COMPARISON Lifecycle Decision Comparison A framework for comparing credible responses, assumptions, consequences, and lifecycle implications. WORKING RECORD Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook A practical record for carrying evidence, reasoning, ownership, field findings, verification, and learning through execution. TECHNICAL FLUENCY Pipeline Integrity, Metallurgy & PHMSA Technical context for understanding the evidence and conversations surrounding pipeline integrity decisions. Open SOUBEL Resources DIGITAL CONTINUITY Technology can help preserve the decision chain. Connected asset records, mobile field systems, integrity platforms, digital twins, analytics, workflow tools, and AI can help retrieve prior reasoning, integrate field evidence, compare history, and surface change. Digital continuity strengthens Operational Trust when asset identity, source, context, assumptions, ownership, and verification remain traceable. Explore Digital Transformation & AI THE CONTINUITY TEST Could the next person understand the decision without reconstructing it from fragments? When evidence, judgment, action, field findings, and verification remain connected, the organization preserves decision memory and strengthens the next integrity decision. Return to Asset Integrity Management Explore Technical Knowledge","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/asset-integrity-management/governance-assurance/","title":"AIM Governance & Assurance | SOUBEL","h1":"Integrity decisions need a system around them.","description":"SOUBEL explores the governance, competency, decision authority, traceability, assurance and learning that help Asset Integrity Management remain connected and defensible.","headings":"Good technical work gains strength when ownership is clear. | Qualified people understand the work and its limits. | Decision rights remain visible. | Repeatable pathways support consistent decisions. | The record carries context forward. | Verification shows whether the system is working. | New information should improve the system. | A decision should have an owner, a basis, and a path to verification. | Make the reasoning visible. | Define who can decide and when review is required. | Close the loop with evidence. | Assurance turns expectations into evidence. | Operational trust grows when responsibility and evidence remain connected. | Return to the AIM framework | SOUBEL Resources | Can the organization show who decided, on what basis, what controls applied, what was verified, and what changed afterward?","text":"Asset Integrity Management GOVERNANCE & ASSURANCE Integrity decisions need a system around them. Asset Integrity Management becomes stronger when technical work is supported by clear ownership, qualified people, defined authority, reliable procedures, traceable information, verification, and organizational learning. Explore the management system Return to AIM THE MANAGEMENT SYSTEM Good technical work gains strength when ownership is clear. Governance provides the structure that helps inspection, engineering, operations, maintenance, contractors, and leadership work from a shared understanding of responsibility, evidence, and decision authority. PEOPLE & COMPETENCY Qualified people understand the work and its limits. Roles, training, experience, technical qualifications, and access to the right expertise shape the quality of integrity decisions. ROLES & AUTHORITY Decision rights remain visible. Ownership, technical authority, approvals, escalation paths, and accountability should remain clear as work moves across functions. PROCESSES & METHODS Repeatable pathways support consistent decisions. Procedures, assessment methods, work processes, acceptance criteria, and change controls provide the framework for moving from evidence to action. DATA & TRACEABILITY The record carries context forward. Asset identity, source information, assumptions, findings, decisions, work performed, and verification remain connected over time. ASSURANCE & PERFORMANCE Verification shows whether the system is working. Reviews, audits, KPIs, completion evidence, recurring findings, and trend information can reveal where controls are strong and where attention is needed. LEARNING & CHANGE New information should improve the system. Field findings, incidents, changed conditions, regulatory developments, technology, and lessons learned should influence future decisions and practices. DECISION GOVERNANCE A decision should have an owner, a basis, and a path to verification. AIM governance helps preserve who is responsible for the decision, which evidence supports it, where uncertainty remains, what controls apply, and what evidence will demonstrate that the intended action was completed. DECISION BASIS Make the reasoning visible. Record the evidence considered, assumptions made, uncertainty recognized, alternatives evaluated, and rationale for the selected response. AUTHORITY & ESCALATION Define who can decide and when review is required. Clear thresholds and escalation paths help changed conditions or conflicting evidence reach the appropriate technical and operating authority. CLOSURE & VERIFICATION Close the loop with evidence. Completion should show what was performed, what the field confirmed, whether the intended result was achieved, and what changed in the asset record. Explore Evidence to Execution THE ASSURANCE LOOP Assurance turns expectations into evidence. A management system gains credibility when expectations can be traced through execution, verification, review, and improvement. Define Expectations Execute the Work Verify the Result Review Performance Improve the System The purpose is continuity: each cycle should leave the organization with a clearer understanding of the asset, the decision process, and the effectiveness of the controls around it. GOVERNANCE + OPERATIONAL TRUST Operational trust grows when responsibility and evidence remain connected. Governance gives Asset Integrity Management an organizational backbone. It connects technical authority to decision authority, procedures to field execution, verification to closure, and learning to the next cycle of work. ASSET INTEGRITY MANAGEMENT Return to the AIM framework See how governance fits with condition, evidence, decisions, execution, verification, and learning. DECISION TOOLS & GUIDES SOUBEL Resources Use practical tools that support clearer decision basis, handoff, verification, and learning. THE GOVERNANCE TEST Can the organization show who decided, on what basis, what controls applied, what was verified, and what changed afterward? When those connections remain visible, governance strengthens assurance, accountability, and Operational Trust. Return to Asset Integrity Management Explore AIM Technical Knowledge","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/asset-integrity-management/integrity-methods/","title":"Integrity Methods & Decision Inputs | SOUBEL","h1":"Different integrity questions require different evidence.","description":"SOUBEL connects integrity methods, technical evidence, operating context, limitations and qualified judgment to stronger Asset Integrity Management decisions.","headings":"Methods create evidence. Context gives that evidence meaning. | Observe, detect, measure, and characterize condition. | Connect mechanisms, environment, control, and field evidence. | Use likelihood and consequence to focus attention. | Evaluate whether observed condition can support continued service. | Understand what the material is telling the organization. | Extend visibility across time and across systems. | Start with the integrity question. | Define the condition, threat, or uncertainty. | Match the evidence to the decision. | Keep capability and uncertainty visible. | Technical inputs become useful when they remain connected to operating reality. | Go deeper where the technical question requires it. | Assessment, validation, and integrity decisions | Corrosion-control evidence in context | Data, digital twins, analytics, and AI | Consequence, uncertainty, and operating context | SOUBEL Resources | Does the method answer the integrity question, and are its limits understood?","text":"Asset Integrity Management INTEGRITY METHODS & DECISION INPUTS Different integrity questions require different evidence. Inspection, testing, corrosion control, risk analysis, engineering assessment, materials knowledge, monitoring, and digital systems each answer different questions. Asset Integrity Management connects those inputs to the decision they are meant to support. Explore the method families Return to AIM METHOD FAMILIES Methods create evidence. Context gives that evidence meaning. The method should fit the asset, the suspected degradation mechanism, the operating environment, the decision being made, and the confidence required. No single technique provides every answer. INSPECTION & NDE Observe, detect, measure, and characterize condition. Inspection and nondestructive examination provide evidence about physical condition, location, dimensions, morphology, or change. Capability, access, calibration, coverage, and detection limits shape what can reasonably be concluded. CORROSION MANAGEMENT Connect mechanisms, environment, control, and field evidence. Coatings, cathodic protection, monitoring, process conditions, materials, inspection, and historical performance contribute to understanding corrosion risk and control effectiveness. RISK & RBI Use likelihood and consequence to focus attention. Risk assessment and risk-based inspection can help prioritize where evidence, inspection, mitigation, or review is most valuable when assumptions and uncertainty remain visible. FITNESS FOR SERVICE & ENGINEERING ASSESSMENT Evaluate whether observed condition can support continued service. Engineering assessment brings material condition, geometry, loading, degradation, operating conditions, uncertainty, and applicable criteria together to support asset-specific decisions. MATERIALS & METALLURGY Understand what the material is telling the organization. Material properties, degradation mechanisms, fracture behavior, laboratory findings, failure analysis, and historical records can clarify why damage occurred and what it means for future decisions. MONITORING, DATA & DIGITAL Extend visibility across time and across systems. Condition monitoring, integrity software, connected records, analytics, digital twins, and AI can help organize and compare evidence when data quality, provenance, context, and validation remain intact. METHOD SELECTION Start with the integrity question. WHAT NEEDS TO BE UNDERSTOOD? Define the condition, threat, or uncertainty. The first question is what the organization needs to know about the asset, rather than which technology happens to be available. WHAT DECISION WILL FOLLOW? Match the evidence to the decision. Screening, prioritization, repair, continued operation, mitigation, reassessment, life extension, and replacement require different levels and types of evidence. WHAT ARE THE LIMITS? Keep capability and uncertainty visible. Every method has boundaries. Detection limits, assumptions, operating conditions, data quality, model confidence, and validation affect the strength of the conclusion. FROM METHOD TO DECISION Technical inputs become useful when they remain connected to operating reality. A result gains decision value when the organization can see where it came from, what question it answers, what assumptions or limitations apply, how it relates to asset history and operating conditions, and what qualified judgment was used to interpret it. Integrity Question → Method → Evidence → Interpretation → Decision → Verification CONNECTED KNOWLEDGE Go deeper where the technical question requires it. PIPELINE INTEGRITY Assessment, validation, and integrity decisions Explore ILI, pressure testing, direct assessment, field validation, remediation, data, risk, and pipeline-specific context. CORROSION & CP Corrosion-control evidence in context Explore corrosion mechanisms, cathodic protection, field measurements, monitoring, interference, and decision context. DIGITAL KNOWLEDGE Data, digital twins, analytics, and AI Explore provenance, data-to-decision continuity, model confidence, sampling bias, and trustworthy digital twins. OPERATIONAL RISK Consequence, uncertainty, and operating context See how technical evidence connects to exposure, controls, prioritization, and operating decisions. TOOLS & GUIDES SOUBEL Resources Use practical decision tools and technical guides for integrity, metallurgy, asset condition, lifecycle, reliability, and maintenance decisions. THE METHOD TEST Does the method answer the integrity question, and are its limits understood? Strong Asset Integrity Management connects technical capability to the decision being made, the operating context around it, and the evidence needed to verify what happens next. Return to Asset Integrity Management Explore the Knowledge Library","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/asset-performance/","title":"Asset Performance | SOUBEL","h1":"Running ≠ Performing","description":"SOUBEL connects condition, reliability, maintenance, operating context, risk, economics, and digital decision support to asset-performance outcomes.","headings":"Asset performance has no meaning without context. | What does the operation require from the asset? | What result has to be delivered? | What is changing, and does it threaten that result? | Why does the change matter now? | What response creates the strongest operating value? | When the question becomes a decision. | Technology should sharpen the decision, not become the story. | Technical depth lives in the Knowledge Library. | Does the information change what the organization should do?","text":"Expertise ASSET PERFORMANCE Running ≠ Performing Asset performance asks whether a physical asset is fulfilling its intended purpose safely, reliably, and economically. SOUBEL connects condition, integrity, reliability, operations, maintenance, data, risk, and economics to the decisions that determine whether an asset continues to create value. Explore the perspective Open the knowledge branch THE SOUBEL PERSPECTIVE Asset performance has no meaning without context. The same equipment can be considered successful or underperforming depending on what the operation requires from it. Throughput, reliability, integrity, energy use, maintenance burden, availability, remaining life, safety, and cost all shape the answer. Asset performance begins by understanding the intended function and operating objective before deciding what data, technology, maintenance, or intervention matters. THE PERFORMANCE QUESTION What does the operation require from the asset? Performance starts with the required operating outcome, not a generic benchmark. The question is whether the asset can continue to do the job the operation needs, under the conditions it actually faces, with an acceptable balance of reliability, risk, cost, and remaining life. OPERATING REQUIREMENT What result has to be delivered? Duty, throughput, quality, availability, operating envelope, safety, and service expectations define what good performance means for that asset. CONDITION & RELIABILITY What is changing, and does it threaten that result? Inspection, sensors, integrity evidence, process conditions, maintenance history, alarms, OEM information, and field observations help establish whether condition or reliability is moving away from what the operation requires. OPERATING CONSEQUENCE Why does the change matter now? Production, safety, energy use, maintenance burden, workforce capacity, spares, operating windows, customer commitments, and business continuity determine the practical consequence of waiting or acting. ECONOMICS & REMAINING LIFE What response creates the strongest operating value? Monitoring, maintenance, repair, derating, operating changes, additional evidence, replacement, and capital investment should be weighed against cost, risk, downtime, reversibility, and the useful life that remains. PRACTICAL TOOLS When the question becomes a decision. Two SOUBEL resources carry the same thinking into a working decision without turning this page into a technical manual. CONDITION CHANGE When Asset Condition Changes 10 questions for testing the signal, evidence, operating context, consequence, options, and follow-through. Open guide LIFECYCLE CHOICE Lifecycle Decision Comparison Compare cost, operating impact, consequence, uncertainty, remaining life, and the value of acting or waiting. Open tool DATA & DIGITAL SUPPORT Technology should sharpen the decision, not become the story. SCADA, historians, inspection systems, maintenance records, field data, OEM information, engineering models, digital twins, and AI can extend visibility and decision support. Their value depends on whether the information preserves operating context, can be trusted, and helps the organization make a better decision. SOUBEL view: more data is not the objective. Better understanding of asset condition, consequence, timing, and choice is. CONNECTED KNOWLEDGE Technical depth lives in the Knowledge Library. This page stays focused on the operating decision. The Knowledge Library carries the deeper technical context behind condition, reliability, maintenance strategy, and lifecycle value. ASSET HEALTH Condition & Context How evidence becomes a useful view of asset condition. RELIABILITY Reliability & Maintenance Strategy Connecting failure risk, criticality, work planning, and intervention timing. VALUE Lifecycle Economics Balancing performance, risk, cost, downtime, and remaining life. THE DECISION TEST Does the information change what the organization should do? That question keeps asset performance grounded in operating reality. Evidence matters when it helps the organization understand what is changing, why it matters, what options are credible, and which action best supports the required outcome. Explore the Asset Performance Knowledge Branch Operational Risk","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/commercial-growth-strategic-accounts/","title":"Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts | SOUBEL","h1":"Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts","description":"SOUBEL commercial growth strategy connects evidence, customer decisions, account strategy, channels, forecasting and execution to profitable growth.","headings":"Three companion playbooks. One operating philosophy. | The First 90 Days | From Market Signal to Revenue | SOUBEL SCALE™ | Go deeper where the commercial question requires it. | Revenue Strategy | Strategic Accounts | Channel Strategy & Partner Management | Revenue Forecasting | The Revenue Opportunity Map","text":"Expertise · Commercial Growth Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts SOUBEL connects market evidence, strategic accounts, channel design, forecast confidence, and execution capacity into a commercial system leadership can inspect, prioritize, and act on. Explore the Playbook System Explore Commercial Capabilities SOUBEL COMMERCIAL GROWTH PLAYBOOK SYSTEM Three companion playbooks. One operating philosophy. The system moves from establishing commercial reality, to proving opportunity, to building the enterprise capable of carrying greater ambition. Open the Playbook System Establish Reality → Prove the Opportunity → Earn the Right to Scale 01 The First 90 Days Establish the fact base. Test inherited assumptions, reporting quality, account reality, forecast integrity, and the constraints leadership needs to see before prescribing change. 02 From Market Signal to Revenue Prove the opportunity. Connect customer decision, value, pricing, account and channel strategy, forecasting, and execution evidence into a defensible path to revenue. 03 SOUBEL SCALE™ Earn the right to scale. Determine which opportunities justify greater commitment and what the enterprise must become capable of doing to carry the ambition. COMMERCIAL CAPABILITIES Go deeper where the commercial question requires it. Each capability has its own depth page for the underlying method, operating questions, and related playbook connections. Revenue Strategy Test market demand, margin, competitive position, commercial constraints, and delivery reality to define which growth paths have a defensible economic case. Explore → Strategic Accounts Connect customer priorities, decision pathways, budget ownership, existing footprint, credible whitespace, technical requirements, and commercial economics to executable account growth. Explore → Channel Strategy & Partner Management Design the route to market deliberately, then align partner roles, economics, territory ownership, activation, forecasting, inventory, performance data, and governance around measurable growth. Explore → Revenue Forecasting Build confidence from observable customer action, buying milestones, technical qualification, commercial agreement, timing, delivery capacity, and known dependencies rather than probability percentages alone. Explore → FROM DIAGNOSIS TO DECISION The Revenue Opportunity Map The objective is not to produce more ideas. It is to identify the few opportunities the evidence supports strongly enough to fund, staff, and measure. Opportunity → Evidence Evidence → Economic Range Economic Range → Barrier / Root Cause Barrier → Priority Priority → Recommended Play The Revenue Opportunity Map is the synthesis layer connecting commercial diagnosis to execution and, where warranted, larger-scale investment decisions.","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/commercial-growth-strategic-accounts/channel-strategy-partner-management/","title":"Channel Strategy & Partner Management | SOUBEL","h1":"The route to market should be as deliberate as the market strategy.","description":"SOUBEL channel strategy and partner management connects route-to-market design, partner economics, activation, governance, forecasting and performance to measurable growth.","headings":"Coverage alone is not channel strategy. | Design the channel. Then operate it. | Build the route to market. | Make the route perform. | What leadership needs to see. | Partner Strategy & Segmentation | Partner Activation | Territory & Account Governance | Partner Economics & Performance | Channel Operating Cadence | From Market Signal to Revenue","text":"Expertise · Commercial Growth CHANNEL STRATEGY & PARTNER MANAGEMENT The route to market should be as deliberate as the market strategy. Channel strategy determines where direct sales, distributors, representatives, integrators, or other partners create real market access, capability, or economic advantage. ← Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts ROUTE-TO-MARKET DISCIPLINE Design for advantage. Choose the role, economics, territory logic, and coverage model deliberately. Operate for performance. Make activation, pipeline, pricing, inventory, forecasting, and accountability visible. THE COMMERCIAL SYSTEM Coverage alone is not channel strategy. SOUBEL evaluates market coverage, partner roles, territory and account ownership, commercial agreements, pricing and margin structure, seller activation, technical capability, inventory requirements, customer access, pipeline visibility, and performance measurement as one connected commercial system. Effective partner management depends on clear expectations, aligned economics, reliable information, and an operating cadence that shows where the channel is producing value and where intervention is required. TWO DIFFERENT JOBS Design the channel. Then operate it. CHANNEL DESIGN Build the route to market. Market coverage, partner model, territory logic, account ownership, direct versus indirect motions, economics, commercial agreements, pricing architecture, technical capability, inventory requirements, and where the channel creates an advantage the company could not create as efficiently alone. PARTNER MANAGEMENT Make the route perform. Activation, enablement, pipeline visibility, pricing discipline, inventory performance, seller engagement, forecast accuracy, account planning, partner business reviews, and the operating cadence required to keep the channel commercially productive. A signed partner is not the same as an activated channel. OPERATING DISCIPLINE What leadership needs to see. The operating model should make market access, partner productivity, economics, ownership, and execution visible enough to manage. Partner Strategy & Segmentation Different partners require different levels of investment, support, and governance based on access, capability, engagement, data quality, and economic contribution. Partner Activation Move from agreement to execution through named sellers, target accounts, product knowledge, opportunity development, technical support, inventory alignment, and recurring joint activity. Territory & Account Governance Clarify account and project ownership, opportunity protection, pricing discipline, channel roles, and rapid conflict resolution. Partner Economics & Performance Track margin contribution, price realization, sell-through, inventory, rebates, productivity, forecast accuracy, and working-capital impact — not sales volume alone. Channel Operating Cadence Create a repeatable rhythm for opportunity review, forecasting, inventory, seller activation, account planning, business reviews, and executive decisions. CONNECTED PLAYBOOK From Market Signal to Revenue The companion playbook connects channel design to customer decision, commercial value, route-to-market evidence, forecasting, and the broader Revenue Opportunity Map. Explore the playbook Download PDF","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/commercial-growth-strategic-accounts/playbook-system/","title":"Commercial Growth Playbook System | SOUBEL","h1":"Three companion playbooks. One operating philosophy.","description":"Three SOUBEL companion playbooks for establishing commercial reality, proving revenue opportunity and earning the right to scale.","headings":"Operational Trust™ from decision to execution. | One system. Three decision points. | The First 90 Days | From Market Signal to Revenue | SOUBEL SCALE™","text":"Expertise · Commercial Growth · Playbook System SOUBEL COMMERCIAL GROWTH PLAYBOOK SYSTEM Three companion playbooks. One operating philosophy. The system moves from establishing commercial reality, to proving opportunity, to building the enterprise capable of carrying greater ambition. ← Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts 01 Establish Reality Build the fact base before prescribing change. 02 Prove the Opportunity Test customer decision, value and revenue evidence. 03 Earn the Right to Scale Confirm the enterprise can carry the ambition. OPERATING PHILOSOPHY Operational Trust™ from decision to execution. Operational Trust™ means the evidence, economics, decision logic, execution capability, and learning stay connected as an opportunity moves from idea to investment to execution. Ambition creates possibility. Evidence supports the decision. Execution creates proof. Repeatability enables scale. THE PLAYBOOKS One system. Three decision points. Each playbook is designed to answer the next commercial question without losing the evidence established in the stage before it. 01 Establish Reality The First 90 Days A practical system for entering a complex industrial business, testing inherited assumptions, establishing information trust, identifying commercial and operating constraints, and building a defensible fact base for the decisions that follow. Key tools Hypothesis & Contradiction Log Metric & Source Register Account Portfolio Map Opportunity Reality Gate Day-90 Executive Readout Download PDF → 02 Prove the Opportunity From Market Signal to Revenue A commercial operating method for converting market signals into qualified customer decisions, defensible value cases, strategic-account and channel priorities, forecast evidence, and durable revenue opportunities. Key tools Opportunity Reality Gate Customer Decision Map Value & Pricing Case Commercial Metric Dictionary Revenue Opportunity Map Download PDF → 03 Earn the Right to Scale SOUBEL SCALE™ An executive growth architecture for determining which opportunities have earned greater commitment and what the organization must become capable of doing across capital, people, commercial capacity, delivery, operating systems, and information infrastructure. Key tools Candidate Growth Brief Four-Test Decision Lens Scale Readiness Stress Test Portfolio Decision Record Stop Disposition Download PDF →","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/commercial-growth-strategic-accounts/revenue-forecasting/","title":"Revenue Forecasting | SOUBEL","h1":"A forecast should explain where the number comes from.","description":"SOUBEL revenue forecasting builds confidence from observable customer actions, buying milestones, qualification, commercial agreement, timing, delivery reality and information trust.","headings":"Make the assumptions visible. | Build confidence from what is actually known. | Evidence before probability | Separate what behaves differently | Name what the business actually knows | Trust the architecture before the report | Reality first. Opportunity proof next.","text":"Expertise · Commercial Growth REVENUE FORECASTING A forecast should explain where the number comes from. Forecast confidence should rise when observable customer action, commercial agreement, technical qualification, decision timing, and delivery reality become more visible. ← Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts FORECAST CONFIDENCE Committed Observable customer action and commercial agreement support the timing. Probable Material evidence exists, with identifiable steps still required. Developmental The opportunity is real, but customer commitment remains incomplete. Speculative Potential exists, but the evidence is not yet strong enough to carry the number. FORECAST ARCHITECTURE Make the assumptions visible. SOUBEL approaches forecasting through the evidence beneath the number: opportunity stage, customer commitment, buying process, technical qualification, competitive position, pricing, partner involvement, delivery capacity, historical conversion, timing, and known dependencies. The objective is not a more complicated forecast. It is a forecast leadership can interrogate, challenge, and manage. A CRM probability percentage is not evidence of customer commitment. FORECAST DISCIPLINE Build confidence from what is actually known. The forecast becomes more useful when evidence quality, revenue motion, classification, and reporting architecture remain visible. EVIDENCE Evidence before probability Use customer behavior, buying milestones, technical qualification, pricing, competition, delivery constraints, and known dependencies to support forecast confidence instead of relying on a percentage alone. REVENUE MOTION Separate what behaves differently Existing-account expansion, new-logo growth, channel revenue, recurring revenue, project-based sales, and one-time transactions do not carry the same predictability and should not be forecast as though they do. CLASSIFICATION Name what the business actually knows Committed, Probable, Developmental, and Speculative revenue should carry different evidence burdens, management actions, and expectations. INFORMATION TRUST Trust the architecture before the report Metric definitions, source systems, manual adjustments, dependencies, and decision use should remain connected so the forecast can be traced back to its underlying assumptions. CONNECTED PLAYBOOKS Reality first. Opportunity proof next. The First 90 Days tests the inherited pipeline, forecast, account baseline, and information architecture. From Market Signal to Revenue carries the qualified opportunity into customer decision, commercial value, route to market, and forecast evidence. Explore the Playbook System","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/commercial-growth-strategic-accounts/revenue-strategy/","title":"Revenue Strategy | SOUBEL","h1":"Growth has to be built from evidence.","description":"SOUBEL revenue strategy connects market demand, commercial economics, competitive position, delivery reality and evidence to prioritized profitable growth.","headings":"Start with what the business can prove. | Four questions before resources are committed. | Where is the economic opportunity? | What is preventing capture today? | What would change the decision? | What can the organization reliably deliver? | From Market Signal to Revenue","text":"Expertise · Commercial Growth REVENUE STRATEGY Growth has to be built from evidence. A credible revenue strategy distinguishes market attractiveness from commercial feasibility, economic value, and the organization’s ability to deliver. ← Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts REVENUE STRATEGY LENS Market truth Customer proof Commercial economics Delivery reality THE STRATEGY QUESTION Start with what the business can prove. SOUBEL evaluates market demand, existing accounts, whitespace, competitive position, pricing, commercial capacity, channel performance, and operational constraints together. The objective is to separate attractive ideas from opportunities the organization can actually win, deliver, and sustain — then tie the priorities to measurable revenue, margin, timing, ownership, and execution requirements. WHAT THE EVIDENCE HAS TO ANSWER Four questions before resources are committed. Each question tests a different source of commercial risk. Together they show whether the opportunity is economically meaningful, winnable, supportable, and executable. ECONOMICS Where is the economic opportunity? Market demand, account potential, pricing, margin, cost-to-serve, working capital, concentration, and delivery requirements shape the value of the revenue, not just its size. CAPTURE What is preventing capture today? Commercial capability, competitive position, technical fit, channel performance, capacity, proposal cycle, qualification, or operating constraints can produce the same revenue symptom for very different reasons. EVIDENCE What would change the decision? Customer action, market proof, commercial economics, technical qualification, operational feasibility, and reporting confidence should become more demanding as the decision becomes more consequential. DELIVERY What can the organization reliably deliver? Revenue quality depends on whether commercial promises can survive engineering, plant, fabrication, field service, inventory, working-capital, and delivery constraints. CONNECTED PLAYBOOK From Market Signal to Revenue The companion playbook carries the opportunity from market signal through customer decision, value, route to market, strategic-account and channel logic, forecast evidence, and the Revenue Opportunity Map. Explore the playbook Download PDF","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/commercial-growth-strategic-accounts/strategic-accounts/","title":"Strategic Accounts | SOUBEL","h1":"Large accounts require more than relationship coverage.","description":"SOUBEL strategic-account strategy connects customer priorities, decision pathways, whitespace, proof requirements and commercial economics to executable growth.","headings":"Build the account around the customer decision. | The account plan should explain how growth can actually happen. | Customer priorities and operating context | Decision pathways and budget ownership | Existing footprint and credible whitespace | Value, proof, and next commitment | The First 90 Days","text":"Expertise · Commercial Growth STRATEGIC ACCOUNTS Large accounts require more than relationship coverage. Strategic-account growth depends on understanding how the customer actually buys, where value is created, who owns the problem, what evidence is required, and where credible whitespace exists across the organization. ← Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts ACCOUNT STRATEGY Build the account around the customer decision. SOUBEL examines account structure, operating priorities, decision pathways, existing footprint, competitive position, budget ownership, technical requirements, and commercial economics to identify where deeper penetration is justified. The goal is not more account activity. It is a focused account strategy that connects customer need to executable revenue opportunities. WHAT HAS TO BE VISIBLE The account plan should explain how growth can actually happen. 01 Customer priorities and operating context Understand the outcomes the customer is trying to protect or improve, the timing around those priorities, and the operational consequences behind the request. 02 Decision pathways and budget ownership Map who owns the problem, who influences the technical decision, who controls funding, what proof is required, and where the buying process can stall. 03 Existing footprint and credible whitespace Separate genuine whitespace from wishful cross-sell by testing technical fit, customer need, incumbent position, internal access, economic value, and the organization’s ability to deliver. 04 Value, proof, and next commitment Define the evidence the customer needs to advance, the operational and economic value at stake, and the next observable commitment that would increase confidence. CONNECTED PLAYBOOK The First 90 Days The account baseline is only useful if inherited assumptions, reporting quality, opportunity reality, and the existing portfolio are tested before they become the strategy. Explore the playbook Download PDF","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/corrosion-cathodic-protection/","title":"Corrosion & Cathodic Protection | SOUBEL","h1":"Corrosion: How Can We Prevent It?","description":"An accessible introduction to corrosion, electrochemistry, cathodic protection, field measurement, and the connected corrosion-control system.","headings":"Explore the system. | CP Systems | Equipment & Components | Testing & Monitoring | Go deeper into the corrosion-control system.","text":"Expertise CORROSION & CATHODIC PROTECTION Corrosion: How Can We Prevent It? Corrosion is an amazing science lesson happening all around us. Explore CP Open the Knowledge Library At its core is electrochemistry — the movement of electrons and ions that can slowly change metal when it interacts with its environment. Rust is one familiar example, but corrosion takes many forms. What you may not have known is that there is an entire global industry built around controlling it. Corrosion can be managed in many ways, including coatings, material selection, chemical treatment, monitoring, inspection, maintenance, and specialized electrical methods. And one of the ways to control it is a method called CATHODIC PROTECTION. EXPLORE CATHODIC PROTECTION Explore the system. 01 CP Systems Galvanic and impressed-current cathodic protection. 02 Equipment & Components The physical equipment and connections that make up a CP system. 03 Testing & Monitoring Field measurement, surveys, and monitoring. EXPLORE THE TECHNICAL DETAIL Go deeper into the corrosion-control system. The Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Knowledge Library provides supporting technical references and deeper detail. Open the Corrosion & CP Knowledge Library →","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/corrosion-cathodic-protection/cp-systems/","title":"Cathodic Protection Systems | SOUBEL","h1":"Cathodic Protection Systems","description":"Galvanic and impressed-current cathodic protection systems.","headings":"Impressed Current Cathodic Protection | ICCP sequence | Galvanic / Sacrificial Cathodic Protection | Cathodic Protection Anodes | Technical Notes | Mixed Metal Oxide (MMO) Anodes","text":"Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Cathodic Protection Systems Two approaches to applying cathodic protection: impressed current and galvanic systems. Impressed Current Cathodic Protection Impressed current cathodic protection uses an external DC power source to drive protective current from an anode system through the electrolyte to the structure being protected. Unlike a galvanic system, the protective current is supplied and controlled by the external power source, allowing ICCP to serve applications with higher or more variable current demand. SOUBEL.com + − 1 2 3 4 5 Field components 1 Rectifier Converts AC to DC. Positive (+) feeds the anode bed; negative (−) connects to the pipeline. 2 Test station Measurement access only: pipeline and reference-electrode leads terminate here. 3 Anode bed Connected to rectifier positive (+); discharges protective current into the soil. 4 Protected pipeline Connected to rectifier negative (−); receives protective current and acts as the cathode. 5 Permanent reference electrode Stable reference used with the pipeline test lead to measure pipe-to-soil potential. Follow the current path ICCP sequence Play sequence ↺ Select a stage or play the full sequence. 01 AC power & rectification 02 Positive (+) to anodes 03 Current through soil 04 Pipeline as cathode 05 Negative (−) return & monitoring System overview Start with the field installation, then follow the electrical path through the system. Galvanic / Sacrificial Cathodic Protection Galvanic cathodic protection uses a more active metal anode — commonly magnesium or zinc — electrically connected to the steel being protected. The natural potential difference between the metals drives protective current through the surrounding electrolyte to the pipeline, making the pipeline the cathode. Unlike an impressed-current system, galvanic protection does not require an external DC power source. The sacrificial anode corrodes preferentially and is gradually consumed while helping protect the steel. Because the available driving voltage and current are limited, galvanic systems are generally used where current demand is comparatively modest and are commonly paired with an external pipeline coating. SOUBEL.com SOUBEL.com Two conceptual galvanic-anode arrangements: a pre-packaged canister bed and a distributed anode bed. Actual spacing, anode material, backfill, sizing, and system design are application-specific. ANODE MATERIALS Cathodic Protection Anodes Technical Notes Galvanic-anode potentials vary with alloy composition, electrolyte, temperature, surface condition, and operating conditions. Reference-electrode basis must be stated when comparing measured potentials. ICCP systems use an external DC power source to drive current. ICCP anode materials are therefore compared by electrochemical performance, current-output capability, consumption, environment, configuration, and design life rather than by position on a galvanic nobility scale. Mixed Metal Oxide (MMO) Anodes MMO anodes typically consist of a titanium substrate coated with an electrocatalytic layer of mixed metal oxides. These coatings commonly incorporate iridium- and/or ruthenium-based oxides , with coating chemistry selected for the intended electrolyte and operating environment. The titanium provides the structural substrate, while the oxide coating provides the electrochemically active surface from which impressed current is discharged into the electrolyte. Unlike galvanic anodes, MMO anodes do not provide the driving voltage for cathodic protection. An external DC power source supplies the driving force. This allows MMO anodes to operate at comparatively high current densities with very low anode consumption when properly selected and operated. MMO-coated titanium is available in configurations including tubular, wire, ribbon, mesh, and discrete anodes. Applications include buried pipelines and groundbeds, tanks, marine structures, and other immersed systems. Coating chemistry and loading , anode configuration, electrolyte, operating current density, and required design life are important considerations in system design. ← Back to Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Next: Equipment & Components →","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/corrosion-cathodic-protection/equipment-components/","title":"CP Equipment & Components | SOUBEL","h1":"CP Equipment & Components","description":"Cathodic protection equipment and system components.","headings":"Components that connect and support the system. | Rectifiers & Power | Test Stations | Cables & Connections | Supporting Components","text":"Corrosion & Cathodic Protection EQUIPMENT & COMPONENTS CP Equipment & Components The physical components that connect, power, access, and support cathodic protection systems. SYSTEM HARDWARE Components that connect and support the system. 01 Rectifiers & Power DC power equipment and supporting power arrangements used with impressed-current systems. 02 Test Stations Field access points for structure leads, reference-electrode leads, bonds, shunts, and related connections. 03 Cables & Connections CP cable, splices, terminations, junctions, continuity, and electrical connections. 04 Supporting Components Isolation devices, reference electrodes, junction boxes, and other system hardware. ← Back to Corrosion & Cathodic Protection","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/corrosion-cathodic-protection/testing-monitoring/","title":"CP Testing & Monitoring | SOUBEL","h1":"CP Testing & Monitoring","description":"Cathodic protection field testing, measurement, and monitoring.","headings":"Measurement shows what the system is doing.","text":"Corrosion & Cathodic Protection TESTING & MONITORING CP Testing & Monitoring Field measurement and monitoring provide the evidence used to understand cathodic protection performance. FIELD EVIDENCE Measurement shows what the system is doing. Test stations, reference electrodes, field surveys, and remote monitoring provide access to the electrical conditions around a protected structure and the evidence used to evaluate system performance. ← Back to Corrosion & Cathodic Protection","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/digital-transformation-ai/","title":"Digital Transformation & AI | SOUBEL","h1":"Digital Transformation & AI","description":"SOUBEL perspective on industrial AI, data provenance, uncertainty, digital twins, sampling bias, workflow integration and decision value.","headings":"Start with the evidence beneath the technology. | What should industrial leaders ask before trusting the output? | Can the data be trusted? | What uncertainty travels with the prediction? | Does the dataset represent the asset, or where people chose to look? | Can a digital twin explain the evidence beneath its output? | What decision changes because the organization knows something sooner? | Technology has to prove itself beyond the initial proof of concept or pilot. | Start with the operating problem | Proof before scale | Context before automation | Human judgment remains accountable | Technology becomes operating capability when the evidence can be trusted, the uncertainty can be understood, the decision can be explained, the work can be executed, and the outcome can be verified with a defensible record.","text":"Expertise Digital Transformation & AI Industrial AI earns value when the organization can trust the evidence, understand the uncertainty, and connect the output to a real operating decision. Explore five operating questions Digital & AI Knowledge RESEARCH-INFORMED PERSPECTIVE Start with the evidence beneath the technology. SOUBEL approaches digital transformation from the operating environment outward. Digital twins, AI, analytics, remote monitoring, connected inspection data, and industrial software can create useful capability. Their value depends on whether the information entering the system carries enough context to be understood, whether uncertainty is visible, whether the dataset itself can distort the conclusion, and whether the output can change a decision that someone is prepared to own. Research basis: This perspective is informed by a targeted SOUBEL review of AMPP/CORROSION literature, PHMSA and pipeline-integrity requirements, digital-twin research, and broader AI/ML literature. The concerns below are established or emerging industry considerations identified in that review; SOUBEL does not present them as newly invented concepts. FIVE OPERATING QUESTIONS What should industrial leaders ask before trusting the output? 01 Can the data be trusted? A value in a database is only the beginning. Source, location, time, instrument condition, calibration, procedure, operating state, environmental conditions, and data-quality flags can change what the information means. When that context is stripped away, the model may still produce an answer, but the organization has less basis for knowing how much confidence the answer deserves. For industrial AI, provenance is part of the evidence. The organization should be able to trace important inputs back to how they were created and understand what may have affected them before those inputs become training data, model inputs, or decision support. Reference foundation: AMPP SP0169-2024; PHMSA 49 CFR Parts 192/195 and Operator Qualification requirements; provenance and metadata themes identified in the SOUBEL research review. Continue reading → 02 What uncertainty travels with the prediction? Industrial models combine measurements, assumptions, parameters, operating conditions, and sometimes sparse or imperfect histories. A precise output can look more certain than the evidence beneath it. Useful decision support therefore has to distinguish what is directly observed from what is estimated, inferred, modeled, or sensitive to assumptions. The research inventory found uncertainty quantification repeatedly identified in digital-twin literature as important to risk-informed decision-making. The practical question is simple: can the user understand not only the prediction, but also the confidence that belongs behind it and what could materially change the conclusion? Reference foundation: Pipeline digital-twin literature reviewed by SOUBEL; uncertainty-quantification research; ISO 23247 and pipeline digital-twin standards/guidance identified for continued review. Continue reading → 03 Does the dataset represent the asset, or where people chose to look? Historical integrity and inspection data are rarely collected at random. Known problem areas may be inspected more often, certain technologies may be used only on selected assets, and older records may contain different levels of completeness. Those collection patterns become part of the dataset whether the model recognizes them or not. That creates an important analytical question: is the model learning the underlying asset condition, or is it also learning the organization’s historical inspection strategy? The SOUBEL review identified this as a subject that deserves explicit scrutiny in industrial AI and integrity analytics. Reference foundation: Corrosion/ML and digital-twin literature reviewed by SOUBEL; broader NIST/IEEE work on ML bias identified in the research inventory; PHMSA integrity-management studies on inspection planning identified for deeper review. Continue reading → 04 Can a digital twin explain the evidence beneath its output? A useful digital twin is more than a visualization layer. Field measurements and sensor data have to be ingested, checked, placed in context, associated with provenance and quality information, and interpreted with uncertainty before model output becomes decision support. The deeper issue is traceability. When a twin indicates condition, risk, remaining life, or another modeled state, the organization should be able to understand the evidence path beneath that result well enough to validate it, challenge it, and decide what action is justified. Reference foundation: Recent pipeline digital-twin reviews; data-quality, interoperability and uncertainty themes documented in the SOUBEL research inventory; ISO 23247 and API RP 1160 identified in the research review. Continue reading → 05 What decision changes because the organization knows something sooner? Prediction is not the operating outcome. Earlier information creates value when it gives people time to gather corroborating evidence, compare options, coordinate resources, adjust operations, plan field work, prioritize capital, or select a better intervention window. SOUBEL therefore evaluates digital capability by the decision opportunity it creates. The question is not simply whether the technology detects, predicts, or visualizes something. It is whether the organization can convert that information into a better decision and then verify what happened after the decision became work. Reference foundation: Risk-based inspection and integrity-management literature reviewed in the SOUBEL research inventory; PHMSA integrity-management emphasis on integrated evidence, prioritization, action and records. Continue reading → FROM CAPABILITY TO OPERATING VALUE Technology has to prove itself beyond the initial proof of concept or pilot. An initial proof of concept or pilot can demonstrate that a technology is technically possible or promising. Operating value begins when it performs with real assets, real data, real users, real procedures, and real accountability at scale. Start with the operating problem Define the problem, the decision owner, the evidence needed, and the outcome the organization is trying to improve before selecting the platform, model, or AI capability. Proof before scale Define what credible success would look like before the pilot begins: technical performance, workflow improvement, adoption, field effort, risk, economics, or another measurable outcome. Context before automation Connected data are not automatically decision-ready data. Asset identity, time, operating state, method, quality and uncertainty remain part of the meaning. Human judgment remains accountable AI can surface patterns and support analysis. Qualified people still have to understand the evidence, limitations and operating context behind consequential decisions. THE SOUBEL VIEW Technology becomes operating capability when the evidence can be trusted, the uncertainty can be understood, the decision can be explained, the work can be executed, and the outcome can be verified with a defensible record. That is the practical connection between Digital Transformation & AI and Operational Trust: the system should not only support the decision. It should preserve enough evidence to show how the decision was made, what was done, and what happened next. Explore Digital & AI Knowledge → Read Enterprise Readiness for AI →","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/expertise/operational-risk/","title":"Operational Risk | SOUBEL","h1":"Operational Risk","description":"SOUBEL perspective on operational risk, governance, decision quality, field execution, compliance, and business consequence in industrial environments.","headings":"Risk has to move from identification to action. | Four dimensions have to stay connected. | What is physically changing? | What happens if the condition progresses? | Who owns the decision, and can the reasoning be explained? | Can the organization actually do the work? | Different settings. The same decision problem. | An indication has to become a defensible response. | An early signal only matters if it creates a better decision window. | A model output becomes one piece of evidence, not the decision itself. | Risk reduction has to connect to an operating or business consequence. | Operational risk does not sit in its own silo. | Can the organization explain the risk, act on it, and verify what happened next?","text":"Expertise Operational Risk Operational risk becomes manageable when the organization can connect evidence, consequence, ownership, decisions, and field action. SOUBEL looks at risk through the operating system around the asset, not as a score that lives by itself. THE OPERATING VIEW Risk has to move from identification to action. A risk model can identify exposure. The harder question is what happens next: whether the evidence is trusted, whether consequence is understood, whether someone owns the decision, whether the work can be executed, and whether the result returns to the record. Evidence → Consequence → Priority → Decision → Action → Verification WHAT SHAPES OPERATIONAL RISK Four dimensions have to stay connected. Asset & Condition What is physically changing? Inspection findings, corrosion, degradation, reliability history, operating conditions, maintenance history, and field observations establish the condition evidence behind a risk decision. Consequence & Exposure What happens if the condition progresses? Safety, environmental impact, regulatory exposure, production, reliability, cost, customer commitments, and business continuity shape the consequence side of the decision. Governance & Decision Who owns the decision, and can the reasoning be explained? Thresholds, assumptions, uncertainty, approvals, escalation paths, traceability, and review conditions determine whether the decision can survive scrutiny and changing circumstances. Execution & Feedback Can the organization actually do the work? People, procedures, contractor capability, permits, materials, access, scheduling, operating windows, verification, and field evidence determine whether a risk decision becomes a controlled outcome. WHERE RISK BECOMES REAL Different settings. The same decision problem. Pipeline Integrity An indication has to become a defensible response. ILI, direct assessment, corrosion data, excavation findings, pressure history, location, consequence, and repair criteria all influence whether an integrity threat is monitored, investigated, mitigated, or repaired. Reliability & Maintenance An early signal only matters if it creates a better decision window. Condition information has value when it helps teams confirm the issue, prepare parts and labor, choose an intervention window, reduce repeat work, or avoid a more consequential failure. Digital & AI A model output becomes one piece of evidence, not the decision itself. Source quality, operating context, validation, uncertainty, ownership, workflow fit, and domain judgment determine whether digital risk insight can support responsible action. COMMERCIAL RELEVANCE Risk reduction has to connect to an operating or business consequence. The value case becomes clearer when risk work can be connected to avoided loss, regulatory exposure, reliability, operating continuity, field effort, maintenance burden, capital allocation, workforce safety, or better decision timing. The commercial question is not simply whether a tool produces a risk score. It is whether the organization can use that information to change an outcome. CONNECTED EXPERTISE Operational risk does not sit in its own silo. Decision & Execution Operational Trust → Integrity Asset Integrity Management → Reliability Asset Performance → Technology Digital Transformation & AI → THE DECISION TEST Can the organization explain the risk, act on it, and verify what happened next? Explore Operational Trust →","type":"Expertise"},{"url":"/industry-intelligence/","title":"SOUBEL Industry Intelligence | Developments, Research & Analysis","h1":"What is happening, and what are we learning from it?","description":"SOUBEL Industry Intelligence brings together consequential industry developments, Oil & Gas AI Lab research, and selected original analysis with clear provenance and context.","headings":"One place to enter the current conversation. | Consequential developments, linked to the source. | Evidence being examined through the Lab. | Original SOUBEL interpretation and commentary. | Curated, not automated noise. | Entries will appear here newest first after source and editorial review. | AI-specific evidence has its own research lane. | SOUBEL Oil & Gas AI Lab | Selected original thinking. | AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It.","text":"SOUBEL INDUSTRY INTELLIGENCE What is happening, and what are we learning from it? Industry Intelligence is the umbrella for consequential external developments, evidence emerging from the Oil & Gas AI Lab, and selected SOUBEL analysis. It is organized to preserve the difference between what happened, what the evidence shows, and what SOUBEL thinks it may mean. THREE INTELLIGENCE LANES One place to enter the current conversation. The purpose is not to create another feed. It is to make current developments easier to understand without blurring source reporting, research findings, and original commentary. 01 · CURRENT DEVELOPMENTS Consequential developments, linked to the source. Regulation, standards, projects, M&A, industrial technology, AI, infrastructure, and other developments that materially affect industrial decision-making. Original source remains primary. SOUBEL adds concise context, not copied reporting. Publication remains curated. Explore Current Developments → 02 · FROM THE OIL & GAS AI LAB Evidence being examined through the Lab. Research notes, public views, observations, and findings that genuinely originate from the Oil & Gas AI Lab research process. Research provenance stays visible. Evidence and uncertainty remain separate from conclusions. Not every Digital & AI article belongs here. Explore Lab Research → 03 · ANALYSIS & PERSPECTIVES Original SOUBEL interpretation and commentary. Selected current writing on commercial growth, regulation, operational trust, industrial operations, integrity, and digital transformation. Original writing stays canonical in Analysis & Perspectives. This page surfaces selected work rather than duplicating the archive. The Integrity Shift remains a signature series. Explore Current Analysis → CURRENT DEVELOPMENTS Curated, not automated noise. SOUBEL tracks developments that may change how industrial organizations operate, invest, comply, adopt technology, or make decisions. Entries should point readers to the original reporting, regulator, standards body, company release, filing, or other primary source and add only the context needed to understand why the development deserves attention. Midstream Pipelines Integrity Corrosion Regulatory M&A AI & Technology Projects PHMSA Energy Infrastructure DEVELOPMENT FEED Entries will appear here newest first after source and editorial review. No copied articles. No indiscriminate press-release stream. No automated publication without review. EDITORIAL STANDARD Discovery can be automated. Publication remains deliberate. SOUBEL separates source facts, research observations, and interpretation so readers can see what is known, what is being examined, and where judgment begins. FROM THE OIL & GAS AI LAB AI-specific evidence has its own research lane. PUBLIC RESEARCH WINDOW SOUBEL Oil & Gas AI Lab The Lab examines how artificial intelligence is moving into oil and gas operations, separates credible developments from hype, identifies where evidence is strong or weak, and supports future SOUBEL research and tools. Open the Oil & Gas AI Lab → 4 Public Research Views currently provide a controlled window into the broader research program. ANALYSIS & PERSPECTIVES Selected original thinking. FEATURED / LATEST AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. Industrial AI can move faster than the operating systems, data structures, and decision processes underneath it. Faster analytics do not automatically create better decisions. Read the article → A&P The complete archive of original SOUBEL interpretation, commentary, essays, and signature series lives in Analysis & Perspectives. Explore Analysis & Perspectives","type":"Industry Intelligence"},{"url":"/industry-intelligence/oil-gas-ai-lab/","title":"SOUBEL Oil & Gas AI Lab | Operational Trust","h1":"SOUBEL Oil & Gas AI Lab","description":"SOUBEL Oil & Gas AI Lab examines industrial AI signals, deployment evidence, operational trust, and governed research in oil and gas.","headings":"Broad sources. Narrow relevance. | Questions worth investigating. | Current signals under review. | AI-enabled drilling operations | Autonomous control-room capability | Where AI investment is moving. | Regulatory Scout","text":"SOUBEL Oil & Gas AI Lab Examined through evidence. The Oil & Gas AI Lab was created by SOUBEL to study how artificial intelligence is moving into oil and gas operations, separate credible developments from hype, and build a clearer view of where the technology is proving useful, where the evidence is still developing, and where important risks or unanswered questions remain. The Lab combines ongoing research with tools such as Regulatory Scout, which tracks meaningful regulatory and standards developments for review. That work helps build a stronger evidence base for SOUBEL analysis, future tools, and practical understanding of how AI may affect real-world operational decisions. 20 Sources monitored 5 Active signals 8 Research priorities 4 Public Research Views WHAT WE'RE WATCHING Broad sources. Narrow relevance. Selected public source families informing the Lab's industrial AI research. NIST · AI risk & assurance DOE · energy-system AI CISA · OT/ICS resilience PHMSA · pipeline safety AMPP & API · integrity evidence Operators & technology providers ON THE RESEARCH DESK Questions worth investigating. What evidence should exist before AI influences a consequential integrity decision? Where is the boundary between recommendation, supervised action, and operational autonomy? What separates a promising pilot from a trustworthy operating deployment? SIGNALS WE'RE WATCHING Current signals under review. PRODUCTION-SCALE CLAIM · INVESTIGATE AI-enabled drilling operations Tracking reported fleet-scale deployment while preserving the distinction between deployment claims and independently validated performance. PILOT / PROOF OF CONCEPT · MONITOR Autonomous control-room capability Watching the evidence boundary between AI recommendations, automated decisions, and demonstrated long-duration operating reliability. AI CAPITAL CONDITIONS Where AI investment is moving. AI investment continues to expand. The Lab is watching where capital is flowing, how that growth is being financed, and whether the power and data-center infrastructure needed to support it is keeping pace. AI infrastructure investment Public-company spending Private credit & debt Power & data-center capacity EVIDENCE BAND · HUMAN-REVIEWED LAB INSTRUMENT 01 Regulatory Scout Regulatory Scout checks selected regulatory and standards websites on a set schedule. Each visit captures the source as it appeared at that moment, creates a digital fingerprint of the content, and verifies expected information. Later visits are compared with the earlier record to identify possible changes for human review. The result is a preserved evidence trail that helps SOUBEL understand where a change could affect its research, knowledge, or tools before anything is updated or published. Public instrument profile · in development Research note: Independent SOUBEL research. Primary sources are preferred. Selected public views do not expose internal research queues, private evidence stores, review mechanics, or unpublished working material.","type":"Industry Intelligence"},{"url":"/insights/","title":"SOUBEL Insights | Industrial Analysis & Perspective","h1":"Perspectives that strengthen industrial decision-making.","description":"SOUBEL analysis on commercial growth, Operational Trust, corrosion, pipeline integrity, regulation, risk, AI, and industrial transformation.","headings":"Three doorways into the SOUBEL point of view. | You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. | Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard | The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life | Explore the work by subject. | Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts | Operational Trust & Integrity | Digital Transformation & AI | Pipeline Integrity & Regulation | The Integrity Shift | From visibility to operational confidence. | Six ideas. One operating thesis. | The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life | Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard | Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions | The Confidence Gap | Enterprise Readiness for AI | When Transformation Meets Operational Reality | The Integrity Shift: A Reflection","text":"SOUBEL INSIGHTS Perspectives that strengthen industrial decision-making. SOUBEL Insights brings together perspectives on commercial growth, Operational Trust, corrosion and integrity, regulation, risk, and industrial digital transformation. Each piece examines a question shaping how industrial organizations make decisions, manage risk, create value, and execute. Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts Operational Trust & Integrity Digital Transformation & AI Pipeline Integrity & Regulation FEATURED INSIGHTS Three doorways into the SOUBEL point of view. Commercial growth, Operational Trust, and asset integrity establish three of the strongest threads running through the current body of work. Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. A commercial-growth perspective on understanding the business, testing the market, and building strategy from evidence, economics, and execution reality. Read insight → Operational Trust & Integrity Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Why industrial transformation has to move beyond visibility toward information, decisions, execution, evidence, and outcomes the organization can rely on. Read insight → Asset Integrity The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life How thousands of ordinary operating decisions can quietly consume long-term asset life under pressure from reliability demands, budgets, regulation, and aging infrastructure. Read insight → EXPLORE BY SUBJECT Explore the work by subject. SOUBEL Insights is organized into four distinct bodies of work, giving each subject room to deepen as new articles, frameworks, regulatory references, and practical resources are published. Editorial series such as The Integrity Shift can span more than one subject area. 01 Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts Profitable growth, strategic accounts, market development, commercial execution, customer value, and the evidence required to build strategy. Current insight You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. Explore Commercial Growth → 02 Operational Trust & Integrity Trusted information, corrosion and asset integrity, reliability, risk, traceability, and the connection between decisions and defensible execution. Current insight Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Explore Operational Trust & Integrity → 03 Digital Transformation & AI AI readiness, data trust, operational maturity, governance, adoption, and the business outcomes technology has to support. Current insight Enterprise Readiness for AI Explore Digital Transformation & AI → 04 Pipeline Integrity & Regulation PHMSA rulemaking, Mega Rule developments, repair criteria, integrity management, engineering judgment, regulatory traceability, and primary-source analysis. Current analysis When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines “Proven”? Explore Pipeline Integrity & Regulation → SIGNATURE SERIES The Integrity Shift Industrial transformation promised visibility. The next standard is Operational Trust: connecting information, decisions, actions, evidence, and outcomes in a way the business can rely on, repeat, and defend. SOUBEL SERIES From visibility to operational confidence. Six connected thought pieces examine asset life, trusted data, operational maturity, AI readiness, and the point where transformation meets field reality. Explore the full series THE SERIES Six ideas. One operating thesis. Read the series in sequence, or enter through the operational question most relevant to you. 01 The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life How thousands of ordinary operating decisions can quietly consume long-term asset life. 02 Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Why industrial transformation has to move beyond dashboards toward trusted, defensible operational intelligence. THE INTEGRITY SHIFT Trusted data drives trusted decisions. Text-led edition 03 Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions The challenge is not data volume. It is whether people can trust the operational context and act on it. 04 The Confidence Gap Why digital advancement can move faster than operational maturity. 05 Enterprise Readiness for AI AI may contribute to the outcome. Accountability, governance, adoption, and business value determine whether it matters. 06 When Transformation Meets Operational Reality Connected systems are not enough when engineering, operations, integrity, and maintenance still see different realities. CLOSING THOUGHT PIECE The Integrity Shift: A Reflection Six thought pieces, one larger question: how do information, decisions, people, and actions remain connected to the outcomes industrial organizations are pursuing? Read the reflection →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/ai-doesnt-fix-bad-data/","title":"AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. | SOUBEL","h1":"AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It.","description":"Industrial AI can move faster than the operating systems, data structures, and decision processes underneath it. Faster analytics do not automatically create better decisions.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Digital Transformation & AI AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. Industrial AI is moving faster than the operating systems, data structures, and decision processes underneath it. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL Originally published September 2, 2026 Industrial AI is moving faster than the operating systems, data structures, and decision processes underneath it. That creates a simple problem: faster analytics do not automatically create better decisions. Data provenance matters. Uncertainty matters. Sampling bias matters. Digital twins can look convincing and still be wrong. And knowing something sooner only creates value when an organization can act on it. SOUBEL has expanded its Digital & AI Knowledge work around five questions that increasingly determine whether industrial technology creates confidence or simply creates more output. The issue is no longer whether AI can produce an answer. The issue is whether the answer deserves to be trusted. Continue exploring SOUBEL Digital Transformation & AI Knowledge Library → Analysis & Perspectives → Digital Transformation & AI expertise →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/commercial-growth-strategic-accounts/","title":"Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts","description":"SOUBEL analysis of profitable growth, strategic accounts, market evidence, value creation, commercialization, and commercial execution.","headings":"This body of work examines how industrial organizations define profitable growth, understand where value is being created or lost, strengthen strategic accounts, and translate market evidence into commercial action. | Define Profitable Growth | Strategic Accounts & Whitespace | Commercial Execution | Published insights | You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One.","text":"← SOUBEL Insights SOUBEL INSIGHTS · COMMERCIAL GROWTH Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts Profitable growth is built from evidence, economics, customer reality, and execution. THE BODY OF WORK This body of work examines how industrial organizations define profitable growth, understand where value is being created or lost, strengthen strategic accounts, and translate market evidence into commercial action. Define Profitable Growth Clarify which markets, customers, offerings, margins, and capabilities create durable enterprise value. Strategic Accounts & Whitespace Understand customer priorities, account potential, buying journeys, and where the organization can create additional value. Commercial Execution Connect market evidence, positioning, proposals, resources, and delivery reality to a strategy the organization can execute. CURRENTLY PUBLISHED Published insights Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. A commercial-growth perspective on understanding the business, testing the market, and building strategy from evidence, economics, and execution reality. Read insight → CONTINUE EXPLORING SOUBEL Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts expertise → experience → All SOUBEL Insights →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/digital-transformation-ai/","title":"Digital Transformation & AI | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"Digital Transformation & AI","description":"Analysis of the operating conditions that determine whether AI, analytics, digital platforms, and connected systems create measurable industrial value.","headings":"This body of work examines the operating conditions that determine whether AI, analytics, digital platforms, and connected systems produce measurable industrial value. | AI Readiness & Accountability | Data Trust & Operational Context | Operational Maturity | Published insights | AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. | Enterprise Readiness for AI | The Confidence Gap | When Transformation Meets Operational Reality","text":"← SOUBEL Insights SOUBEL INSIGHTS · DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION Digital Transformation & AI Technology earns value when it improves operational confidence, adoption, governance, and business outcomes. THE BODY OF WORK This body of work examines the operating conditions that determine whether AI, analytics, digital platforms, and connected systems produce measurable industrial value. AI Readiness & Accountability Establish ownership, governance, risk management, success measures, and the accountability required to move from experimentation to execution. Data Trust & Operational Context Strengthen the quality, context, and usability of information supporting engineering and operational decisions. Operational Maturity Evaluate transformation by how confidently teams can act when conditions become uncertain, critical, or regulated. CURRENTLY PUBLISHED Published insights Digital Transformation & AI AI Doesn’t Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. Data provenance, uncertainty, sampling bias, and operational context determine whether faster analytics create better decisions or simply more output. Read insight → Digital Transformation & AI Enterprise Readiness for AI AI may contribute to the outcome. Accountability, governance, adoption, operational context, and measurable business value determine whether it matters. Read insight → Digital Transformation & AI The Confidence Gap Why digital advancement can move faster than operational maturity, even when the technology itself is working as intended. Read insight → Digital Transformation & AI When Transformation Meets Operational Reality Connected systems are not enough when engineering, operations, integrity, and maintenance still see different versions of operational reality. Read insight → CONTINUE EXPLORING SOUBEL Digital Transformation & AI expertise → Operational Risk expertise → The Integrity Shift → Analysis & Perspectives →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/dont-confuse-altitude-with-influence/","title":"Don’t Confuse Altitude with Influence | SOUBEL","h1":"Don’t Confuse Altitude with Influence","description":"Commercial strategy depends on understanding where the buying event begins and translating between operational reality and enterprise consequence.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Commercial Growth · Strategic Accounts · Operational Trust Don’t Confuse Altitude with Influence The real question is not how high to sell. It is where the buying event begins and how the decision moves through the organization. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL In complex B2B sales, there is a persistent belief that if you can just get high enough in the organization, the deal will move. Sometimes that may be right. If a company is evaluating an acquisition, divestiture, major capital investment, or enterprise risk exposure, the conversation may absolutely belong in the C-suite. Technical insight can become strategic when it helps leadership understand what they are buying, what risks they may be inheriting, and whether the value they see on paper is supported by the condition of the underlying assets. That is not a field-level transaction. That is a business decision. But not every buying event starts there. A CEO may hear a compelling idea and say, “Go talk to Joe.” And Joe may be the person who owns the problem, understands the operational consequence, knows the budget, influences the technical decision, and ultimately has to live with the solution. A Rolodex can open a door. It cannot replace a go-to-market strategy. The real question is not whether to sell high or sell low in an organization. It is: Where does the buying event begin? If the problem is operational, start close to the problem and build the business case upward. If the problem is strategic, start with the enterprise consequence and connect it downward to the technical evidence required to support the decision. The strongest commercial leaders know how to move in both directions. And they know something else: A hypothesis is not a strategy. When entering a new business, market, or revenue stream, leaders should bring ideas. They should see possibilities. They should have a point of view. But prescribing a commercial strategy before understanding the team, the customer base, existing processes, win/loss patterns, buying behavior, internal constraints, and where the current go-to-market model is succeeding or failing is not strategy. It is assumption. Strong commercial leaders arrive with hypotheses. They earn the strategy through diagnosis. That requires listening to the people closest to the work while also understanding the larger business objectives leadership is trying to achieve. Because the field sees things the C-suite may not. And the C-suite sees enterprise implications the field may never encounter. Commercial strategy is not created in the C-suite or in the field. It is created in the translation between them. That translation is where technical capability becomes business value. Where operational risk becomes economic consequence. Where customer problems become strategic priorities. And where credibility becomes something bigger than a sales relationship. It becomes Operational Trust. Because sustainable growth is not simply about reaching the highest-ranking person. It is about understanding the decision, the risk, the people, and the value well enough to become relevant at every level where it matters. Originally published on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Analysis & Perspectives → Operational Trust →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/industrial-operations-operational-understanding/","title":"There’s Something Interesting Happening Across Industrial Operations Right Now | SOUBEL","h1":"There’s Something Interesting Happening Across Industrial Operations Right Now","description":"Industrial transformation is shifting from software-centric conversations toward trusted operational understanding, practical decisions, and field usability.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Industrial Operations · Digital Transformation There’s Something Interesting Happening Across Industrial Operations Right Now The differentiator is shifting away from technology alone and toward operational understanding that people can trust and use. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL There’s something interesting happening across industrial operations right now. For years, digital transformation conversations were centered around software, dashboards, AI, and data lakes. But the companies making real progress are shifting the conversation back to operations. Not “How much data do we have?” But: Can operators trust it? Does it improve decisions? Does it reduce risk? Does it help people do their jobs better? Does it actually support the business? The more time I spend speaking with owner-operators, engineers, integrity teams, and operations leaders, the clearer it becomes: Technology alone is not the differentiator anymore. Operational understanding is. The future belongs to organizations that can connect engineering reality, operational context, and digital capability into something practical, trusted, and usable in the field. That’s where real transformation starts. And honestly, it’s a pretty exciting time to be part of this industry. Originally published on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Analysis & Perspectives → Operational Trust → Digital Transformation & AI →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/","title":"The Integrity Shift | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"The Integrity Shift","description":"The Integrity Shift is a SOUBEL signature series on Operational Trust, asset life, trusted data, digital maturity, AI readiness and industrial execution.","headings":"The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life | Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard | Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions | The Confidence Gap | Enterprise Readiness for AI | When Transformation Meets Operational Reality | The Integrity Shift: A Reflection","text":"SOUBEL SIGNATURE SERIES The Integrity Shift Six thought pieces examine what happens when technology meets operational reality: asset life, Operational Trust, trusted data, operational maturity, AI readiness, and the need for a shared operational picture. 01 The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life Asset life is often consumed quietly through thousands of ordinary operating decisions. 02 Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Trusted, defensible operational intelligence matters more than another dashboard. THE INTEGRITY SHIFT Trusted data drives trusted decisions. Text-led edition 03 Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions Industrial transformation is not a data-volume problem. The real challenge is operational trust. 04 The Confidence Gap Digital capability can advance faster than operational maturity. 05 Enterprise Readiness for AI The shortage is not AI talent. It is accountability for operationalizing AI and proving business value. 06 When Transformation Meets Operational Reality Transformation breaks down when teams cannot align around one contextualized operational reality. CLOSING THOUGHT PIECE The Integrity Shift: A Reflection The series was never meant to be about software, AI, digital twins, or data platforms. It is about what happens when technology meets operational reality. Read the reflection →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/confidence-gap/","title":"The Confidence Gap | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"The Confidence Gap","description":"Digital advancement can outpace operational maturity. The meaningful measure of transformation is how confidently people can act when conditions become uncertain.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Operational Trust & Integrity · The Integrity Shift The Confidence Gap The industry can appear more digitally mature than it operationally behaves. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL One of the more interesting realities emerging across industrial digital transformation is this: The industry appears more digitally mature than it operationally behaves. On the surface, industrial operations look more advanced than ever: AI-driven analytics. Digital twins. Predictive maintenance. Remote operations. Connected infrastructure. Real-time operational visibility. Integrated operational technology environments. And to be clear — real progress has absolutely been made. Many organizations have significantly improved operational awareness, engineering visibility, integrity monitoring, and decision support capabilities over the past decade. But beneath that progress, many companies are still navigating a more difficult operational challenge: How do organizations operationally mature alongside the technology environments they deploy? Because in many industrial environments, operational execution still depends heavily on: manual workarounds, spreadsheet reconciliation, tribal knowledge, field interpretation, and human validation between enterprise systems, operational technology platforms, engineering workflows, and field operations. That is not failure. It is the reality of industrial transformation maturing inside complex operational environments. Because the real measure of industrial digital transformation success may not be how advanced the technology appears. It will be how confidently operations, engineering, and field teams can respond when conditions become uncertain or operationally critical. There is a quiet strength in this shift. Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Operational Trust → Digital Transformation & AI → Operational Risk → Previous Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions Next Enterprise Readiness for AI","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/enterprise-readiness-for-ai/","title":"Enterprise Readiness for AI | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"Enterprise Readiness for AI","description":"The AI challenge is not just technical capability. It is leadership accountability, governance, adoption, operational execution and measurable business value.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Digital Transformation & AI · The Integrity Shift Enterprise Readiness for AI The market does not have an AI talent shortage. It has an accountability shortage. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL The market does not have an AI talent shortage. It has an accountability shortage. In recent years, AI has found its way into nearly every boardroom, strategy session, and investor presentation. Organizations have launched pilots, experimented with new tools, and developed ambitious roadmaps. Yet many are discovering the same reality: Deploying technology is not the same as creating business value. In many ways, this reflects the broader theme behind The Integrity Shift. Organizations have invested heavily in digital technologies designed to improve visibility, increase efficiency, and support better decision-making. Yet technology alone rarely delivers the outcome. The real value emerges when organizations can transform information into trusted operational action. The challenge is rarely finding people who understand AI. The challenge is finding leaders who know how to operationalize it. Leaders who can establish governance, manage risk, align stakeholders, drive adoption, and connect technology investments to business outcomes. In industrial environments, the objective is operational confidence: Safer operations Reliability Reduced risk Compliance Better business decisions AI may contribute to those outcomes, but it is not the outcome itself. So how do organizations move from experimentation to execution? Establish ownership and accountability. Define success before implementation. Align technology to business objectives. Create governance and risk management processes. Invest in data quality and operational context. Measure performance and value realization. Drive organizational adoption. Evaluate every initiative through three lenses: Financial Value: Did it improve revenue, reduce cost, or lower risk? Business Value Creation: Did it improve operations or create competitive advantage? Governance & Trust: Can the outcome be explained, defended, and audited? As organizations move beyond experimentation, the conversation is shifting: From technical capabilities to operational execution From visibility to accountability From activity to measurable business value From technology adoption to operational confidence That may be the real Integrity Shift. Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Digital Transformation & AI → Operational Trust → Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts → Previous The Confidence Gap Next When Transformation Meets Operational Reality","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/operational-trust-new-standard/","title":"Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard","description":"Corrosion management shows why industrial transformation has to move beyond dashboards and static documentation toward trusted, defensible operational intelligence.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Operational Trust & Integrity · The Integrity Shift Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard The real challenge is not a lack of dashboards. It is creating trusted, defensible operational intelligence that supports real decisions. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL Corrosion management may quietly be becoming one of the most important digital transformation conversations in industrial operations. For years, many integrity programs have relied on disconnected workflows: inspection records in one system, process data in another, engineering analysis somewhere else, and operational decisions often buried in emails, spreadsheets, or static reports. The challenge was never simply a lack of dashboards. The real challenge has been creating trusted, defensible operational intelligence that supports: asset integrity reliability risk reduction engineering decision-making As regulatory pressure, operational complexity, and aging infrastructure continue to increase, static documentation alone may no longer be enough. What’s becoming increasingly important is operational context: connecting corrosion data, process conditions, inspection history, risk prioritization, and field operations into workflows that improve visibility, traceability, and confidence in decision-making. One of the more interesting recent industry discussions I’ve seen comes from AMPP around digitalized corrosion control documentation and its role in improving plant reliability and asset integrity. The conversation appears to be moving beyond digitizing records alone — toward creating connected operational intelligence that supports defensible integrity management. That may ultimately become one of the defining shifts in industrial digital transformation. Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Operational Trust → Corrosion & Cathodic Protection → Digital Transformation & AI → Previous The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life Next Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/quiet-consumption-of-asset-life/","title":"The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life","description":"How thousands of small operational decisions can quietly consume asset life under pressure from budgets, reliability demands, regulation and aging infrastructure.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Operational Trust & Integrity · The Integrity Shift The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life Most assets do not suddenly fail. Their lifespan is often consumed quietly through thousands of small operational decisions. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL If you spend enough time around industrial operations, you start noticing something: Most assets don’t suddenly fail. Their lifespan gets consumed quietly over time. Not usually because someone made one terrible decision. But because thousands of small operational decisions slowly add up. “We’ll run it another quarter.” “We’ll deal with it during the next turnaround.” “It’s still operational.” “We can’t justify replacement this year.” And to be fair, most of those decisions make sense in the moment. Teams are dealing with production pressure, shrinking budgets, staffing shortages, delayed parts, aging infrastructure, and nonstop operational demands. But over time, those pressures start shaping asset life itself. Equipment gets pushed harder. Repairs become reactive. Temporary fixes last longer than intended. Older systems stay online because replacement costs are difficult to justify. And sometimes the damage isn’t even visible. Friends of mine in Louisiana who’ve worked around these environments their whole lives will tell you the same thing: Assets age differently down on the Gulf Coast. Heat. Humidity. Salt air. Storm-related power fluctuations. Aging infrastructure operating far beyond what it was originally designed for. Even electrically, unstable power quietly stresses motors, drives, servers, instrumentation, and control systems long before obvious failure appears. And in the pipeline and midstream world especially, integrity teams are still working hard to maintain PHMSA compliance, inspection schedules, repair criteria, and operational continuity at the same time. But compliance does not eliminate operational pressure. It simply defines the boundaries organizations must operate within. Years ago, I worked on a project at a coal-powered generation facility in Nevada. New regulations made it clear the company would never build another new coal facility again. Faced with that regulatory and political reality, the company was forced into a massive overhaul after years of deferred investment. Facility engineers and maintenance teams know the reality: Long-term reliability often loses the budget battle to immediate operational demand. Maybe that’s the real Integrity Shift. Not simply a shift in technology… but a shift in how organizations balance operational continuity, reliability, risk, and long-term infrastructure health under increasing pressure. So maybe the larger question becomes: As industrial complexity continues accelerating… how do organizations preserve long-term operational integrity without quietly consuming the very assets they depend on? Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Operational Trust → Asset Performance → Operational Risk → Series The Integrity Shift Next Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/reflection/","title":"The Integrity Shift: A Reflection | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"The Integrity Shift: A Reflection","description":"A closing reflection on six thought pieces about asset life, Operational Trust, trusted data, digital maturity, AI readiness and operational reality.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Operational Trust & Integrity · The Integrity Shift The Integrity Shift: A Reflection Different topics. Same purpose. Connecting information, decisions, and actions to outcomes that matter. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL Over the past several weeks, I’ve written about: The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions The Confidence Gap Enterprise Readiness for AI When Transformation Meets Operational Reality While each explored a different aspect of industrial transformation, they all seemed to lead back to the same observation: Industrial organizations have more data, more visibility, more analytics, and more computing power than ever before. The progress is real. AI-driven analytics. Digital twins. Predictive maintenance. Connected infrastructure. Real-time operational visibility. These technologies are creating capabilities that would have been difficult to imagine just a decade ago. But as technology continues to advance, a different challenge is emerging: How do organizations adapt their people, processes, and decisions to fully capitalize on the value these technologies create? The questions that matter most remain remarkably human: Can we trust the information? Can we align around priorities? Can we confidently act when conditions become uncertain? Can we transform insight into measurable operational outcomes? The Integrity Shift was never intended to be a discussion about software, AI, digital twins, or data platforms. It is a discussion about what happens when technology meets operational reality. In many ways, The Integrity Shift is not about digital transformation at all. It is about ensuring that information, decisions, and actions remain connected to the outcomes organizations are pursuing—and the men and women responsible for delivering them. Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL The Integrity Shift → Operational Trust → All Insights → Previous When Transformation Meets Operational Reality Continue All SOUBEL Insights","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/transformation-meets-operational-reality/","title":"When Transformation Meets Operational Reality | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"When Transformation Meets Operational Reality","description":"Connected systems create value when operations share contextualized information people can trust, interpret, and use in real decisions.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Operational Trust & Integrity · The Integrity Shift When Transformation Meets Operational Reality Industrial transformation promised visibility. What many organizations got instead were multiple versions of operational reality. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL Industrial transformation promised visibility. What many organizations got instead were multiple versions of the operational reality. More connected systems did not create more connected operations: Engineering sees one picture. Operations sees another. Integrity, maintenance, and leadership each see something different again. I read an article here that included this statement: “AI models built on ambiguous data create risk faster than they create value.” Or… to quote simply, a close friend of mine, “data out is only as good as data in.” That is absolutely true in industrial operations. When operational data still lives on islands, organizations start slowing themselves down. Decisions get revisited. Teams stop trusting each other’s numbers. Priorities shift depending on who is in the room. And critical issues take longer to act on because no one is fully confident in the operational picture. The industry has, however, made real progress connecting assets and systems. But successful transformation requires more than technology. It requires one shared, contextualized version of operational truth across engineering, operations, integrity, and maintenance. When data conflicts, interpretations conflict. And when interpretations conflict, alignment starts to erode. Meetings become stressful…longer. Approvals become slower. Teams become defensive. And eventually, trust in the operational picture itself begins to break down. The organizations making progress aren’t trying to fix everything at once. They focus on mature, operational use cases that improve reliability, response time, and consistency across the business. They know disconnected operational reality isn’t just inefficient anymore. It’s operational risk. And perhaps this integrity shift asks a much larger question: How do organizations maintain operational alignment as industrial complexity continues to accelerate? Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Operational Trust → Operational Risk → Digital Transformation & AI → Previous Enterprise Readiness for AI Next The Integrity Shift: A Reflection","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/integrity-shift/trusted-data-trusted-decisions/","title":"Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions","description":"Industrial organizations already have enormous volumes of data. The differentiator is whether operations and engineering teams can trust and use it.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Operational Trust & Integrity · The Integrity Shift Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions Industrial transformation is not a data-volume problem. The real challenge is operational trust. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL THE INTEGRITY SHIFT · 03 TRUSTED DATA DRIVES TRUSTED DECISIONS. Industrial transformation is not a data-volume problem. The real challenge is operational trust. Industrial transformation is NOT a data problem. Across pipelines, utilities, terminals, power systems, and industrial facilities, organizations already have enormous volumes of operational information flowing through SCADA systems, historians, reliability platforms, integrity programs, and connected infrastructure technologies. The real challenge is operational trust. Operations, engineering, integrity, and reliability teams must trust the data, the operational context, and the workflows supporting critical decisions inside industrial environments. Technology alone does not create adoption. The good news is most companies already have the people, the operational knowledge, and the data they need. The real opportunity is helping field operations and engineering teams make faster, more confident decisions with information they can actually trust and use in the real world. The organizations creating measurable value are the ones transforming operational data into trusted operational intelligence that improves reliability, strengthens asset awareness, reduces uncertainty, and supports better operational decisions. Trusted data drives trusted decisions. Operational trust is what determines whether industrial transformation succeeds. Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Operational Trust → Digital Transformation & AI → Asset Performance → Previous Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Next The Confidence Gap","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/operational-trust-integrity/","title":"Operational Trust & Integrity | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"Operational Trust & Integrity","description":"Analysis connecting information, engineering judgment, integrity, reliability, field execution, risk, and the confidence organizations need to act.","headings":"This body of work examines the connection between information, engineering judgment, integrity, reliability, field execution, risk, and the confidence organizations need to act. | Operational Trust | Asset Integrity & Reliability | Corrosion & Connected Workflows | Published insights | Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard | The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life | Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions","text":"← SOUBEL Insights SOUBEL INSIGHTS · OPERATIONAL TRUST Operational Trust & Integrity Trusted information becomes valuable when it supports trusted decisions and defensible execution. THE BODY OF WORK This body of work examines the connection between information, engineering judgment, integrity, reliability, field execution, risk, and the confidence organizations need to act. Operational Trust Connect information, decisions, actions, evidence, and outcomes in a way the organization can rely on, repeat, and defend. Asset Integrity & Reliability Examine how operational pressure, lifecycle decisions, aging infrastructure, and reliability demands shape long-term asset health. Corrosion & Connected Workflows Bring corrosion data, process conditions, inspection history, risk prioritization, and field activity into a more defensible operational context. CURRENTLY PUBLISHED Published insights Operational Trust & Integrity Operational Trust Is Becoming the New Standard Why industrial transformation has to move beyond visibility toward information, decisions, execution, evidence, and outcomes the organization can rely on. Read insight → Asset Integrity The Quiet Consumption of Asset Life How ordinary operating decisions can quietly consume long-term asset life under pressure from reliability demands, budgets, regulation, and aging infrastructure. Read insight → THE INTEGRITY SHIFT Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions Text-led edition Operational Trust & Integrity Trusted Data Drives Trusted Decisions The challenge is not data volume. It is whether operations, engineering, integrity, and reliability teams can trust the operational context and act on it. Read insight → CONTINUE EXPLORING SOUBEL Operational Trust → Corrosion & Cathodic Protection expertise → Pipeline & Asset Integrity expertise → All SOUBEL Insights →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/phmsa-april-2026-operational-trust-next-standard/","title":"PHMSA’s April 2026 — Operational Trust Is the Next Standard | SOUBEL","h1":"PHMSA’s April 2026 — Operational Trust Is the Next Standard","description":"PHMSA’s April 2026 pipeline safety rulemakings point toward remote sensing, remote monitoring, integrity-management-based compliance, and stronger operational traceability.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Pipeline Regulation · Operational Trust PHMSA’s April 2026 — Operational Trust Is the Next Standard Pipeline safety rulemakings are signaling a more operationally focused digital future built around trusted, defensible information. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL PHMSA’s April 2026 pipeline safety rulemakings suggest the conversation around industrial digital transformation is becoming far more operationally focused. Several proposed updates reference: remote sensing technologies for ROW patrols remote monitoring capabilities integrity-management-based compliance pathways expanded operational traceability Taken together, these changes point toward something bigger than modernization for modernization’s sake. They reflect a growing need for operational intelligence that is trusted, actionable, and aligned with real-world engineering and operational workflows. The conversation is moving beyond dashboards and visualization alone. The real challenge now is whether operators can create trusted, defensible operational intelligence that supports: integrity management risk reduction compliance validation field-level decision making The next phase of industrial digital transformation will likely be defined less by technology hype — and more by operational trust. Originally published on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Analysis & Perspectives → Operational Trust → When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/phmsa-prescriptive-to-predictive/","title":"PHMSA Predictive Models: Who Defines “Proven”? | SOUBEL","h1":"When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines “Proven”?","description":"PHMSA’s 2026 pipeline-repair proposal raises a key question: as modern engineering models gain regulatory weight, what makes a model “proven”?","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Pipeline Integrity & Regulation When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines “Proven”? The technology is not new. What is changing is the regulatory weight engineering models may carry in repair scheduling. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL PHMSA’s July 2026 pipeline-repair proposal leans on “proven, data-driven modern engineering concepts” and “Modern, Proven Engineering Modeling”, signaling a shift toward risk-based scheduling. It starts with two words that stand out: “modern” and “proven.” The NPRM (Notice of Proposed Rulemaking) promises to apply “proven, data-driven modern engineering concepts” and specifically highlights “Modern, Proven Engineering Modeling” to determine where and when repairs are needed. Integrity teams have used advanced inspection data and modeling for years, so what’s changing is how much regulatory weight those models carry. Instead of one-size-fits-all deadlines, PHMSA envisions scheduling fixes by actual threat and remaining safe life. But if an analytical model can set a repair date, its assumptions become crucial. That one word “proven” is key. Does it mean validated by field performance? Independently verified? An AMPP or industry consensus? Who decides… operators, PHMSA, or both? For example, under the proposal, hazardous liquid pipeline operators using an Engineering Critical Assessment (ECA) for dents must now submit their ECA procedures to PHMSA (via a no-objection notification). PHMSA estimates ~710 operators would each spend about 400 hours preparing that documentation. That concrete detail shows models shifting from in-house tools to regulated requirements. The technology isn’t new but giving it regulatory authority is. As we move from prescriptive to predictive safety approaches, defining what makes a model “proven” is the next frontier. What do you think? How should “proven” be defined for integrity models influencing regulations? Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Midstream & Pipelines → Pipeline & Asset Integrity → Operational Risk →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/pipeline-integrity-regulation/","title":"Pipeline Integrity & Regulation | SOUBEL Insights","h1":"Pipeline Integrity & Regulation","description":"SOUBEL analysis of pipeline integrity and PHMSA developments, with focus on engineering judgment, evidence, documentation, and execution.","headings":"This body of work follows pipeline-integrity and PHMSA developments through an operational lens: what is changing, where engineering judgment gains weight, and what operators may need to explain, document, validate, or execute differently. | PHMSA & Mega Rule Developments | Repair Criteria & Engineering Judgment | Integrity Management & Traceability | Current regulatory analysis | When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines “Proven”?","text":"← SOUBEL Insights SOUBEL INSIGHTS · PIPELINE REGULATION Pipeline Integrity & Regulation Regulatory change matters when it changes how operators assess, document, prioritize, and defend decisions. THE BODY OF WORK This body of work follows pipeline-integrity and PHMSA developments through an operational lens: what is changing, where engineering judgment gains weight, and what operators may need to explain, document, validate, or execute differently. PHMSA & Mega Rule Developments Track rulemaking and regulatory changes that influence integrity management, repair decisions, documentation, and operational accountability. Repair Criteria & Engineering Judgment Examine how models, material properties, engineering assessments, and validation influence when and how repairs are prioritized. Integrity Management & Traceability Connect regulatory requirements to the evidence, records, decisions, and workflows operators must be able to explain and defend. CURRENTLY PUBLISHED Current regulatory analysis Pipeline Integrity & Regulation When PHMSA Moves from Prescriptive to Predictive: Who Defines “Proven”? As engineering models gain greater regulatory weight in repair scheduling, PHMSA’s proposal raises a deceptively difficult question: what makes a model “proven”? Read insight → CONTINUE EXPLORING SOUBEL Midstream & Pipelines market → Pipeline & Asset Integrity expertise → Operational Risk expertise → All SOUBEL Insights →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/revenue-strategy-is-built/","title":"You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy | SOUBEL","h1":"You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One.","description":"Revenue strategy is built by listening across the business, testing market evidence, and understanding where value, momentum, and opportunity exist.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts You Don’t Walk In With a Revenue Strategy. You Build One. Revenue strategy is built by listening across the business, testing market evidence, and understanding where value, momentum, and opportunity actually exist. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL Revenue opportunity is rarely sitting in one obvious place. It may be inside an underperforming service line, an existing account with whitespace, a slow proposal process or a technical capability customers do not fully understand. Sometimes the most important commercial signal is not that the business is shrinking. It is that growth has become uneven. One part of the portfolio may be accelerating while another region, market or offering is losing momentum, and its growth is decelerating or falling short of plan. That is when the job stops being “sell harder” and becomes “understand why.” You find those answers by talking to the people who see different parts of the business and you LISTEN. Business development sees emerging markets, unmet needs and where the competition has a stranglehold. Sales understands existing accounts. Technical teams know where the company is differentiated. Finance sees the economics and margins. Proposal teams know what the market will allow and where opportunities become difficult to win. Operations knows what can be delivered successfully, safely and on time. And frankly, listening to cross-functional teams isn’t just about passively meeting the team or being nice. It is the initial part of a rigorous, data-driven diagnostic process to find commercial, competitive and operational gaps. Then you compare that internal picture with what the market is telling you. BCG describes successful growth transformation as requiring “data-driven planning, stress testing, smart sequencing, and persistence.” Before looking for the next big growth engine, you need to understand the one you already have. Where are we winning? Where are we losing? Which capabilities create measurable value? Where are margins strongest? What are customers asking for that we are not providing? Where has the market moved faster than we have? McKinsey senior partner Jill Zucker put a number behind that thinking after research involving 4,000 companies: “Around 80 percent of growth comes from maximizing the value of the core, but 20 percent of growth comes from other pathways.” The answer may be a different buying journey, value proposition, market or commercial approach. But you cannot prescribe the answer before you understand the whole picture. You don’t walk in with a revenue strategy. YOU BUILD ONE. Originally published in an earlier form on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only minor web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts → experience → All Insights →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/insights/transform-revenue-yesterdays-job/","title":"You Don’t Transform Revenue by Hiring for Yesterday’s Job | SOUBEL","h1":"You Don’t Transform Revenue by Hiring for Yesterday’s Job","description":"Commercial transformation requires roles, resources, expectations, and measures of success built for the future revenue model.","headings":"Continue exploring SOUBEL","text":"Commercial Growth & Organizational Transformation You Don’t Transform Revenue by Hiring for Yesterday’s Job Commercial transformation requires roles, resources, expectations, and measures of success built for the future revenue model. By Helen M. Dupree · Founder / Principal, SOUBEL Organizations often say they want growth when what they really need is transformation. Those are not the same thing. When the revenue model is changing, the commercial role responsible for delivering it may need to change with it. Yet companies often start with a traditional account-management job description and then expect the person they hire to become a market strategist, new-business hunter, commercialization leader, relationship builder, cross-functional connector, and change agent. That is not an expanded version of the same job. It is a different job. A future-facing commercial role should be built around the outcome the business actually wants to create. If the goal is expansion, the person in that role has to be able to find whitespace inside existing accounts, recognize new buying triggers, uncover under-commercialized capabilities, and develop opportunities the organization may not be pursuing today. If the goal is transformation, that person also needs room to examine how the commercial model works and where it may need to evolve. Not because everything that came before was wrong, but because tomorrow’s revenue will not automatically come from yesterday’s structure. Leadership has a role in this too. Someone hired to create a different result needs access to information, visibility across functions, support from technical and operational teams, and a clear understanding of how success will be measured. Most importantly, the organization has to decide what it is actually hiring for. Is the expectation to protect existing relationships, expand strategic accounts, or build a new market? Is it to commercialize technical capabilities, build relevance with executive buyers, or develop a different route to revenue? Those objectives may overlap, but they call for different behaviors, different skills, and often different measures of success. The mistake is assuming one traditional role can quietly absorb all of them without changing the environment around it. That is how an organization ends up hiring for transformation while continuing to manage for maintenance. And that is an expensive contradiction. Commercial roles should be designed around the future revenue model, not simply inherited responsibilities. Sometimes the greatest commercial mistake is not hiring the wrong person… it is hiring the right person for the wrong job. If the mandate is transformation, then the role, resources, expectations, and measures of success have to be built for transformation too. Tomorrow’s revenue cannot be created inside yesterday’s job description. Originally published on LinkedIn. This SOUBEL website edition preserves the original article text with only web-formatting cleanup. Continue exploring SOUBEL Analysis & Perspectives → Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts →","type":"Analysis & Perspectives"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/","title":"SOUBEL Knowledge Library | Oil & Gas Technical Knowledge","h1":"Oil & gas technical knowledge, organized by subject.","description":"Browse SOUBEL technical knowledge for oil & gas integrity, corrosion, asset performance, regulation, digital capability, and decision support.","headings":"Start with the subject closest to the question. | Pipeline Integrity | Corrosion & Cathodic Protection | Tank & Terminal Integrity | Asset Performance | Digital & AI Knowledge | Asset Integrity Management | AC Mitigation | Internal Corrosion | Coatings & Linings | Leak Detection & Monitoring | Reliability & Maintenance | Regulatory & Standards | Go beyond reference knowledge.","text":"SOUBEL KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY Oil & gas technical knowledge, organized by subject. Browse practical reference material across integrity, corrosion, asset performance, regulation, digital capability, and related technical disciplines. CORE KNOWLEDGE AREAS Start with the subject closest to the question. Each area leads to deeper technical reference material. The library will expand as substantive governed content is added. Pipeline Integrity Assessment, ILI, direct assessment, field validation, remediation, integrity evidence, and decision continuity. Explore pipeline integrity &rarr; Corrosion & Cathodic Protection CP fundamentals, interference, monitoring, coatings, materials, field evidence, and data confidence. Explore corrosion & CP &rarr; Tank & Terminal Integrity Storage tanks, corrosion, inspection, transfer systems, overfill prevention, containment, and lifecycle records. Explore tank & terminal integrity &rarr; Asset Performance Condition, reliability, maintenance strategy, lifecycle economics, operating context, digital twins, and AI. Explore asset performance &rarr; Digital & AI Knowledge Data provenance, sampling bias, model confidence, digital twins, uncertainty, and data-to-decision continuity. Explore digital & AI knowledge &rarr; Asset Integrity Management Governance, lifecycle thinking, evidence, execution, verification, decision continuity, and organizational learning. Explore Asset Integrity Management &rarr; AC Mitigation AC interference, induced voltage, mitigation systems, monitoring, and field verification. Developing Internal Corrosion Threat mechanisms, monitoring, mitigation, assessment, evidence, and operating context. Developing Coatings & Linings Coating systems, condition, shielding, defects, inspection, compatibility, and lifecycle performance. Developing Leak Detection & Monitoring Detection methods, monitoring systems, thresholds, operating context, and response evidence. Developing Reliability & Maintenance Failure modes, maintenance strategy, reliability, condition monitoring, and lifecycle decisions. Developing Regulatory & Standards PHMSA, API, ASME, AMPP, regulatory context, standards relationships, and verification needs. Developing RELATED SOUBEL WORK Go beyond reference knowledge. Analysis & Perspectives Original articles and commentary on oil & gas, growth, integrity, regulation, AI, and transformation. Read published analysis &rarr; Practical Resources Decision guides, workbooks, and reference tools designed for practical use. Explore resources &rarr; Oil & Gas AI Lab The public view into SOUBEL&rsquo;s developing research environment for AI, technology, and industry signals. Explore the AI Lab &rarr;","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/asset-integrity-management/","title":"Asset Integrity Management Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"Connected technical knowledge across the asset integrity lifecycle.","description":"SOUBEL Knowledge Library gateway connecting Asset Integrity Management to pipeline integrity, tank and terminal integrity, corrosion and cathodic protection, asset performance, and digital decision support.","headings":"Move from the AIM framework into the domain that matches the question. | Threats, assessment, direct assessment, field validation, and the next decision | Storage, transfer, corrosion, controls, containment, and lifecycle context | Materials, protection systems, field evidence, interference, and monitoring | Condition, reliability, maintenance, economics, and operational context | Data provenance, uncertainty, digital twins, AI, and decision support | Return to the Asset Integrity Management perspective","text":"Knowledge Library · Asset Integrity Management ASSET INTEGRITY MANAGEMENT Connected technical knowledge across the asset integrity lifecycle. Asset Integrity Management depends on more than one method, discipline, or asset class. This knowledge gateway connects the technical branches that inform condition, integrity, performance, risk, digital continuity, and the decisions carried through execution. KNOWLEDGE BRANCHES Move from the AIM framework into the domain that matches the question. Each branch goes deeper into the evidence, methods, operating context, and decision logic relevant to that subject while remaining connected to the broader Asset Integrity Management system. PIPELINE INTEGRITY Threats, assessment, direct assessment, field validation, and the next decision Follow evidence through pipeline integrity assessment and verification. TANK & TERMINAL INTEGRITY Storage, transfer, corrosion, controls, containment, and lifecycle context Follow integrity across tanks, terminal piping, transfer systems, and operating interfaces. CORROSION & CATHODIC PROTECTION Materials, protection systems, field evidence, interference, and monitoring Connect corrosion-control evidence to integrity and operating decisions. ASSET PERFORMANCE Condition, reliability, maintenance, economics, and operational context Connect technical condition to performance and lifecycle value. DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION & AI Data provenance, uncertainty, digital twins, AI, and decision support Explore the evidence and governance beneath industrial digital capability. AIM FRAMEWORK Return to the Asset Integrity Management perspective See governance, lifecycle, methods, evidence-to-execution, application domains, and digital enablement in one framework.","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/asset-performance/","title":"Asset Performance Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"What is asset performance?","description":"SOUBEL Asset Performance knowledge explains how physical assets fulfill their intended purpose over time and how condition, reliability, maintenance and lifecycle economics shape performance.","headings":"Running is not the same as performing. | Three knowledge paths explain why performance changes and what to do next. | Is the asset's ability to perform changing, and what does the evidence mean? | What should be done to preserve or restore required performance? | When does continued maintenance stop being the strongest operating value? | Asset performance is the relationship between what the asset is expected to do and what it is actually delivering over time.","text":"Knowledge Library ASSET PERFORMANCE What is asset performance? Asset performance is how well a physical asset fulfills the job it is expected to do over time — safely, reliably, efficiently, and economically, within the operating conditions and constraints that matter to the business. Explore what shapes performance Return to Expertise THE BASIC IDEA Running is not the same as performing. An asset can be operating and still be underperforming. It may be consuming more energy, requiring more maintenance, carrying more risk, losing capacity, or using up remaining life faster than expected. Performance is judged against intended function. The question is not simply whether the asset is on. It is whether the asset is continuing to deliver what the operation requires at an acceptable level of reliability, risk, cost, and useful life. WHAT SHAPES PERFORMANCE Three knowledge paths explain why performance changes and what to do next. Each path answers a different question. Each also includes a practical tool for the moments when the question needs a more structured review. 01 · CONDITION & CONTEXT Is the asset's ability to perform changing, and what does the evidence mean? Interpret condition signals in operating context, challenge the evidence, and determine whether the change affects the function the asset is expected to deliver. Explore Condition & Context Practical tool: When Asset Condition Changes 02 · RELIABILITY & MAINTENANCE What should be done to preserve or restore required performance? Use reliability insight, maintenance history, field reality, and intervention timing to determine what should be monitored, planned, repaired, or changed. Explore Reliability & Maintenance Practical tool: Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook 03 · LIFECYCLE ECONOMICS When does continued maintenance stop being the strongest operating value? Compare cost, downtime, consequence exposure, remaining life, reversibility, and the value of repair, replacement, continued operation, or waiting. Explore Lifecycle Economics Practical tool: Lifecycle Decision Comparison Digital support: SCADA, historians, CMMS/EAM, inspection systems, engineering models, digital twins, and AI can strengthen these three views when the underlying data and context are trustworthy. Explore Digital & AI Knowledge . THE POINT Asset performance is the relationship between what the asset is expected to do and what it is actually delivering over time. Condition, reliability, maintenance, economics, risk, and digital evidence matter because they help explain that performance and what should change when the gap becomes meaningful. Asset Performance Expertise Operational Risk","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/asset-performance/condition-context/","title":"Condition & Context | Asset Performance Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"Before acting on a condition change, establish what the evidence actually means.","description":"SOUBEL Condition & Context helps leaders determine whether a condition change is real, what it means in operating context, and whether it changes an asset","headings":"Four questions keep condition evidence tied to action. | Is the change real? | What was happening when it changed? | Does the change affect required performance? | What should happen next, and what would change that response? | When Asset Condition Changes | Established principles, organized for practical use. | Could someone later understand what changed, why the response was chosen, and what happened next?","text":"Knowledge Library · Asset Performance PATH 1 OF 3 · CONDITION & CONTEXT Before acting on a condition change, establish what the evidence actually means. This is the first Asset Performance knowledge path. It focuses on whether a change is real, what was happening when it occurred, whether it affects required performance, and what response makes sense next. Explore the questions Back to Asset Performance Knowledge FROM SIGNAL TO RESPONSE Four questions keep condition evidence tied to action. Condition information is useful only when the organization can test the evidence, understand the operating context, judge whether performance is actually affected, and choose a response that can be explained and verified. 01 Is the change real? Check the source, quality, completeness, calibration, timing, lineage, assumptions, and whether the apparent change could reflect noise, instrumentation error, or a data-quality problem. 02 What was happening when it changed? Interpret the signal against operating mode, load, pressure, temperature, throughput, environment, recent work, process changes, intended function, criticality, and other relevant conditions. 03 Does the change affect required performance? Determine whether the condition changes the asset's ability to deliver its intended function, reliability, capacity, safety margin, useful life, or other operating requirement. 04 What should happen next, and what would change that response? Compare legitimate options such as continued monitoring, additional evidence, inspection, operating change, mitigation, repair, deferral, or replacement. Preserve the key assumptions, ownership, review triggers, and how the result will be verified. SOUBEL perspective: more data does not automatically create a better understanding of asset condition. The value comes from preserving enough context to determine whether the evidence reflects a meaningful change in performance and what action, if any, should follow. PRACTICAL RESOURCE When Asset Condition Changes The downloadable guide expands these four questions into 10 questions for a defensible response, with space to document evidence, context, options, reasoning, review triggers, and follow-through. Open the PDF REFERENCE BASIS Established principles, organized for practical use. The guide is informed by asset-management, integrity-management, reliability, condition-monitoring, and risk practice. It is a leadership aid, not a substitute for applicable regulations, engineering standards, company procedures, or qualified technical judgment. ISO 55013:2024 · Asset management — Guidance on the management of data assets 49 CFR 192.917 · Threat identification, data gathering and integration PHMSA · Hazardous Liquid Integrity Management SAE JA1011:2024 · Reliability-Centered Maintenance criteria THE TEST Could someone later understand what changed, why the response was chosen, and what happened next? Operational Trust depends on keeping the evidence, context, response basis, and verification connected well enough to survive review.","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/asset-performance/lifecycle-economics/","title":"Lifecycle Economics | Asset Performance Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"Repair, replace, monitor, or wait. Compare what each choice really costs over time.","description":"SOUBEL Lifecycle Economics helps leaders compare realistic asset options across cost, operating impact, consequence exposure, remaining life, uncertainty, and timing.","headings":"Which option creates the strongest operating value over the period being considered? | What options are realistically available? | What does each option require now? | What does each option preserve or expose over time? | What uncertainty could change the comparison? | Lifecycle Decision Comparison | Established asset-management and life-cycle-value principles, organized for practical use. | Can the organization explain why this option creates stronger value than the alternatives?","text":"Knowledge Library · Asset Performance PATH 3 OF 3 · LIFECYCLE ECONOMICS Repair, replace, monitor, or wait. Compare what each choice really costs over time. This is the third Asset Performance knowledge path. It compares realistic courses of action on the same basis so cost, operating impact, consequence exposure, remaining life, uncertainty, and future flexibility can be considered together. Explore the lifecycle questions Back to Asset Performance Knowledge THE THIRD QUESTION Which option creates the strongest operating value over the period being considered? The comparison is stronger when every credible option is evaluated on the same economic and operating basis rather than judging one choice by its invoice and another by its risk or remaining life. 01 What options are realistically available? Define the legitimate choices before comparing them. Depending on the condition and operating need, those may include continued monitoring, additional evidence, operating changes, planned intervention, repair, mitigation, deferral, or replacement. 02 What does each option require now? Compare immediate expenditure, labor, materials, specialist or contractor support, outage requirements, downtime, production or throughput effects, operating constraints, and the effect on other planned work. 03 What does each option preserve or expose over time? Consider remaining useful life, reliability, capacity, maintenance burden, future flexibility, cost of deferral, and credible safety, environmental, regulatory, production, financial, customer, or reputation consequences. 04 What uncertainty could change the comparison? Record the assumptions that carry the most weight, what is still unknown, what evidence could change the economics, how reversible each option is, and when the comparison should be revisited. SOUBEL perspective: lifecycle economics is not simply about choosing the lowest-cost option. It is about making the trade-offs visible enough to explain what the organization expects to spend, preserve, expose, defer, and keep possible. PRACTICAL RESOURCE Lifecycle Decision Comparison The worksheet places available courses of action side by side so immediate cost, operating impact, remaining life, consequence exposure, uncertainty, reversibility, and timing can be compared on the same basis. Open the PDF REFERENCE BASIS Established asset-management and life-cycle-value principles, organized for practical use. This page and worksheet draw on established asset-management, life-cycle-cost, integrity-management, reliability, and risk principles. They support leadership comparison and documentation and are intended to complement engineering analysis, formal life-cycle costing, company procedures, qualified technical judgment, and applicable regulation. Selected basis: ISO 55001:2024 ; ISO 15686-5:2017 ; Institute of Asset Management · Life Cycle Value Realisation ; and PHMSA integrity-management guidance for pipeline-specific risk and consequence context. THE TEST Can the organization explain why this option creates stronger value than the alternatives? A defensible lifecycle comparison keeps the evidence, assumptions, cost, operating impact, consequence exposure, remaining life, and review conditions connected well enough for someone else to understand the choice later. Return to Asset Performance Knowledge →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/asset-performance/reliability-maintenance-strategy/","title":"Reliability & Maintenance | Asset Performance Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"Once the condition is understood, decide what work will preserve or restore performance.","description":"SOUBEL Reliability & Maintenance helps teams decide what work, if any, is needed to preserve or restore required asset performance and what the result teaches next.","headings":"What should be done to preserve or restore required performance? | What condition are we actually managing? | What intervention is justified now? | Is the work ready to succeed? | What did the work reveal? | Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook | Established reliability and asset-management practice, organized for practical use. | Did the intervention improve the asset's ability to perform?","text":"Knowledge Library · Asset Performance PATH 2 OF 3 · RELIABILITY & MAINTENANCE Once the condition is understood, decide what work will preserve or restore performance. This is the second Asset Performance knowledge path. It focuses on whether intervention is actually needed, what work makes sense now, whether the organization is ready to execute it, and what the result teaches about the asset. Explore the maintenance questions Back to Asset Performance Knowledge THE SECOND QUESTION What should be done to preserve or restore required performance? The goal is not more maintenance. It is the right action, at the right time, with enough preparation and feedback to improve the asset's ability to perform. 01 What condition are we actually managing? Carry forward the evidence and context from Path 1. Clarify the observed condition, likely failure or degradation mechanism, uncertainty, criticality, and how the issue affects required performance. 02 What intervention is justified now? Compare legitimate choices such as continued monitoring, inspection, operating adjustment, planned maintenance, repair, deferral, or replacement. Use consequence, timing, decision window, and reversibility to determine what action is proportionate. 03 Is the work ready to succeed? Confirm people, qualifications, parts, tools, procedures, access, permits, operating windows, contractor support, responsibilities, and the reasoning that needs to travel with the work. 04 What did the work reveal? Compare expected versus actual condition, capture field findings, verify whether performance improved, update assumptions, and carry useful learning into the next maintenance or reliability decision. SOUBEL perspective: reliability insight creates value when it improves the quality or timing of action. Prediction by itself is not the outcome; the better maintenance decision is. PRACTICAL RESOURCE Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook The workbook carries these four questions into a working decision record for technical, operational, planning, and field teams. It preserves the evidence, options, execution readiness, review triggers, field findings, and expected-versus-actual results behind the decision so the reasoning does not disappear with the people who made it. Years later, another qualified person should still be able to understand what was known, why the action was chosen, what uncertainty remained, and what happened afterward. Decision Continuity: preserve enough of the reasoning behind an operational decision that the next person can understand it, challenge it, and learn from what happened. Open the PDF REFERENCE BASIS Established reliability and asset-management practice, organized for practical use. This page and workbook draw on established asset-management, reliability, maintenance, condition-monitoring, and production-assurance practices. They are intended to complement an organization's engineering, operating, safety, work-control, CMMS, EAM, and APM systems. Selected basis: ISO 55001:2024; ISO 17359:2018; ISO 14224:2016; ISO 20815:2026; SAE JA1011:2024; and the GFMAM Maintenance Framework. THE TEST Did the intervention improve the asset's ability to perform? If the result differs from expectation, the difference is new evidence. Carry it forward before comparing repair, replacement, continued operation, or other lifecycle choices. Continue to Path 3 · Lifecycle Economics →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/corrosion-cathodic-protection/","title":"Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"Understand the protection system. Follow the evidence.","description":"SOUBEL Knowledge Library guide to cathodic protection fundamentals, materials and equipment, field evidence, interference, monitoring, integrity context, and data confidence.","headings":"How do we tell if the metal is actually being protected? | What can change the meaning of those electrical readings? | What makes the record defensible? | Each component has a job in the same protection system. | Anodes & CP Systems | Testing & Field Measurement | Cables, Connections & Remote Monitoring | How do we know what is happening in the field? | CIPS / CIS and routine monitoring | DCVG and coating surveys | DC and AC interference | Remote monitoring & digital visibility | CP is one control inside a larger integrity system. | Coatings + CP | ECDA | Direct examination & excavation evidence | Context is part of the measurement. | Preserve the context | Software & AI | Use the right source for the decision.","text":"SOUBEL KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY · CORROSION & CATHODIC PROTECTION Understand the protection system. Follow the evidence. A reference guide to the systems, components, field measurements, monitoring, and evidence behind cathodic protection. Explore the CP system Back to Expertise EXPLORE CATHODIC PROTECTION 01 Anodes & CP Systems Galvanic and impressed-current protection 02 Rectifiers & Power DC power, controls, and field power systems 03 Test Stations & Connections Field access, bonds, shunts, and coupons 04 Testing & Measurement Reference electrodes, potentials, surveys, instruments 05 Cables & Components Continuity, cable, splices, isolation, junctions 06 Remote Monitoring & Digital CP RMUs, communications, alarms, and histories Integrity context and data confidence remain part of the guide below, connecting CP evidence to the larger asset-integrity system. 01 FOUNDATION How do we tell if the metal is actually being protected? Cathodic protection changes the electrochemical condition of the metal. Technicians take electrical readings, including the voltage difference between the structure and a reference electrode , and in some cases the current flowing through parts of the system. What can change the meaning of those electrical readings? Reference-electrode condition and placement, soil or electrolyte condition, current distribution, coating condition, interruption method, electrical continuity , interference , and operating state can all affect what a structure-to-electrolyte potential represents. What makes the record defensible? The record should preserve what was measured, how it was measured, the criterion or engineering basis used, the field conditions present, and the action that followed. Authoritative starting point · AMPP → 02 MATERIALS & EQUIPMENT Each component has a job in the same protection system. The useful question is what role each component plays, what can compromise that role, and what evidence tells the operator whether it is still doing its job. Anodes & CP Systems Anodes provide protective current through galvanic or impressed-current systems. Rectifiers & power systems provide and control DC power in impressed-current systems. Environment, current demand, geometry, output, connections, maintenance, and system condition all affect performance. Testing & Field Measurement Reference electrodes establish the comparison point for potential readings. Test stations & field connections provide access to structure leads, bonds, casings, coupons , and measurement points. Cables, Connections & Remote Monitoring Junction boxes organize circuits and monitoring points. RMUs provide remote visibility into selected conditions. Sensors extend condition visibility, while cables and connections preserve electrical continuity across the system. 03 FIELD EVIDENCE How do we know what is happening in the field? Surveys, electrical readings, monitoring systems, and field observations help show whether the corrosion-control system is performing as intended. Their value depends on how, where, and when the evidence was collected and whether the surrounding conditions are understood. CIPS / CIS and routine monitoring Close-interval surveys increase spatial resolution along the structure. Interpretation depends on reference-electrode control, stationing, interruption where appropriate, timing, repeatability, and comparison with other evidence. AMPP reference · Close-interval potential surveys → DCVG and coating surveys Coating surveys help locate and characterize coating discontinuities and become more useful when results carry into prioritization, excavation planning, and comparison with observed condition. AMPP reference · Aboveground coating-condition surveys → DC and AC interference Foreign CP systems, bonds, crossings, grounding systems, power corridors, and other current paths can change potentials and current distribution. Evaluation considers where current enters and leaves and how mitigation affects connected structures. AMPP reference · Cathodic-protection interference → Remote monitoring & digital visibility Remote monitoring can shorten the time between a change in system condition and organizational awareness when alarms, validation, ownership, and response procedures are clear. AMPP reference · Remote cathodic-protection monitoring → Field standard: the procedure, the work, and the record should tell the same story. 04 INTEGRITY CONTEXT CP is one control inside a larger integrity system. Coatings, CP, interference, direct assessment, excavation findings, repairs, inspection history, operating conditions, records, and regulatory requirements describe different parts of the same asset condition. Coatings + CP Coatings reduce the area exposed to the environment. Cathodic protection addresses electrochemical activity where protection is needed. Coating deterioration changes current demand and system performance over time. ECDA Pre-assessment, indirect inspection, direct examination, and post-assessment connect CP and coating evidence to prioritized field validation and the learning that follows. Direct examination & excavation evidence Coating condition, corrosion morphology, measurements, photographs, repair decisions, and field notes test what above-ground evidence predicted and strengthen the next assessment cycle. Federal pipeline-safety context · PHMSA Part 192 → 05 DATA CONFIDENCE Context is part of the measurement. Survey files, test-station readings, rectifier histories, RMU data, coating surveys, excavation findings, GIS, inspection records, and integrity software become stronger evidence when identity, location, time, units, method, operating condition, and source remain intact. Preserve the context A number without location, time, instrument, method, units, operating condition, and asset identity loses decision value quickly. Data confidence starts before analytics and before AI. Software & AI Digital tools can preserve history, compare evidence, identify change, manage exceptions, and support prioritization. AI can surface patterns across large datasets, but qualified judgment still owns the decision and must understand source quality, validation, uncertainty, and operating context. SOURCE DISCIPLINE Use the right source for the decision. SOUBEL adds practical context and connects the pieces. Asset-specific requirements and technical decisions remain grounded in current standards, regulations, company procedures, original technical literature, and qualified engineering judgment. Back to Corrosion & CP Expertise Open SOUBEL Resources","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/digital-transformation-ai/","title":"Digital Transformation & AI Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"AI Doesn't Fix Bad Data. It Scales It.","description":"SOUBEL Knowledge Library hub for industrial AI, data provenance, uncertainty, digital twins, sampling bias and data-to-decision practice.","headings":"This Isn't a Technology Problem. It's an Evidence Problem. | Questions that deserve more than a slogan. | Data Provenance & Industrial AI | Uncertainty & Model Confidence | Sampling Bias in Integrity Data | Trustworthy Digital Twins | From Data to Operating Decision | Industrial AI becomes useful when the evidence path remains understandable.","text":"Knowledge Library · Digital Transformation & AI THE EVIDENCE BENEATH INDUSTRIAL AI AI Doesn't Fix Bad Data. It Scales It. Industrial AI and digital twins don't correct for gaps, blind spots, or inconsistent records. They inherit them. A model trained on incomplete or unevenly collected data doesn't know what is missing. It simply produces answers from whatever it was given, at a speed and scale no human reviewer worked at before. This library exists to ask five practical questions before that inheritance becomes a problem: What does the data actually represent? Where did it come from? How was it collected? What is missing? And what conclusions can it honestly support? Explore the five topics Digital & AI Expertise HOW TO USE THIS LIBRARY This Isn't a Technology Problem. It's an Evidence Problem. These pages don't introduce a new SOUBEL theory. They organize what's already established in technical literature, standards, and regulatory guidance, drawing from AMPP/CORROSION research, PHMSA requirements, digital-twin studies, integrity-management practice, and AI/ML literature. The goal is practical: help readers ask better questions about where their data came from, what it's missing, and what it can honestly support before they trust what a model does with it. Where the research points to an open question rather than a settled answer, these pages say so directly. FIVE DEEPER TOPICS Questions that deserve more than a slogan. 01 Data Provenance & Industrial AI What has to travel with a measurement so a person, model, or digital twin can understand where it came from and how much confidence it deserves? Continue reading → 02 Uncertainty & Model Confidence How should industrial decision support distinguish observed evidence from estimates, assumptions, modeled values, and uncertainty? Continue reading → 03 Sampling Bias in Integrity Data When historical inspection is concentrated around known concerns, can a model confuse where the organization looked with where degradation exists? Continue reading → 04 Trustworthy Digital Twins How do quality checks, provenance, context, uncertainty, models, and human review connect before a digital-twin output becomes decision support? Continue reading → 05 From Data to Operating Decision What changes because the organization knows something sooner, and can that additional decision time become prepared action and a verified result? Continue reading → OPERATIONAL TRUST Industrial AI becomes useful when the evidence path remains understandable. The model is one part of the decision system. Source quality, context, uncertainty, ownership, execution, and verification determine whether the organization can rely on what happens next. Explore Operational Trust → Read Enterprise Readiness for AI →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/digital-transformation-ai/data-provenance/","title":"Data Provenance & Industrial AI | SOUBEL","h1":"The Value Made the Trip. The Meaning Got Left Behind.","description":"Why industrial data needs its source, method, operating context, quality and history to survive the trip into AI models, dashboards and digital twins.","headings":"Context is part of the evidence. | What should travel with important industrial data? | Established requirements and an emerging digital trust problem. | Can someone explain where the critical input came from and what could have affected it?","text":"Knowledge Library · Digital Transformation & AI DATA PROVENANCE The Value Made the Trip. The Meaning Got Left Behind. A cathodic-protection reading, an inspection result, a sensor value — each one can pass through several systems before it ever reaches a model or a dashboard. Along the way, the number usually survives. The context around it often doesn't. Back to Digital & AI Knowledge WHY IT MATTERS Context is part of the evidence. A timestamp might remain while the procedure version disappears. A value might keep its units while the calibration status of the instrument is gone. A location might stay attached while the operating conditions that shaped the reading get separated from it entirely. That's a problem because a model can't recover context it was never given. It processes whatever value it receives, whether or not anyone can still explain where that value came from or what conditions produced it. Provenance doesn't make data correct. It gives the people relying on it enough to judge how much confidence it's earned — instead of borrowing confidence the data never had. PRACTICAL QUESTIONS What should travel with important industrial data? Source: What system, instrument, inspection, survey, document, or person created the information? Identity: Is the correct asset, location, component, test point, or inspection segment unambiguous? Time and operating condition: When was the information collected, and what was the asset doing at the time? Method: What procedure, configuration, technique, or model version produced the result? Quality: Was calibration, validation, completeness, or another quality check documented? Change history: Can the organization tell whether the data, procedure, asset configuration, or interpretation changed later? SOUBEL practical interpretation: These questions organize provenance themes identified in the research inventory. They are not presented as a new standard or proprietary framework. RESEARCH FOUNDATION Established requirements and an emerging digital trust problem. The SOUBEL research inventory reviewed AMPP SP0169-2024, PHMSA requirements in 49 CFR Parts 192 and 195, Operator Qualification expectations, recent pipeline digital-twin literature, and related work on uncertainty and interoperability. The review found strong existing expectations around qualified personnel, procedures, records, and technical criteria, while also identifying continued industry attention to the problem of preserving sufficient context and uncertainty through digital systems. The research exercise originally explored whether a formal provenance framework for pipeline digital twins could become a novel academic contribution. Novelty was not established. The useful outcome for practice is the clearer recognition that digital systems should preserve enough evidence about important inputs for people to evaluate their trustworthiness later. Reference foundation: AMPP SP0169-2024 · PHMSA 49 CFR Parts 192/195 · PHMSA Operator Qualification requirements · pipeline digital-twin literature and data-confidence themes inventoried in SOUBEL research. THE DECISION TEST Can someone explain where the critical input came from and what could have affected it? If not, the model may still produce an answer, but the organization has less evidence for deciding how much trust to place in that answer. Next: Uncertainty & Model Confidence → Digital Transformation & AI Expertise →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/digital-transformation-ai/data-to-decision/","title":"From Data to Operating Decision | SOUBEL","h1":"Knowing Sooner Only Matters If You Move Sooner.","description":"SOUBEL explains why knowing sooner only creates value when industrial data, analytics and AI lead to timely, owned, executable decisions and measurable outcomes.","headings":"The model output is not the finish line. | What turns insight into an operating decision? | Integrity and risk practice already depend on evidence integration and prioritization. | What changes because the organization knows something sooner?","text":"Knowledge Library · Digital Transformation & AI DATA TO DECISION Knowing Sooner Only Matters If You Move Sooner. Prediction creates value when it gives the organization enough time to change what happens next — in priority, preparation, cost, timing, or execution. Back to Digital & AI Knowledge FROM INFORMATION TO ACTION The model output is not the finish line. Evidence → Interpretation → Priority → Decision → Prepared Work → Outcome → Learning Integrity programs, risk-based inspection, and digital systems can assemble enormous amounts of information. But that information only creates value once the organization turns it into a decision that's timely, owned, executable, and reviewable. Earlier visibility creates options — time to gather corroborating evidence, coordinate operations, compare repair alternatives, or choose a better intervention window. But extra time has no value on its own unless someone actually uses it. That's why prediction should be treated as decision support, not an outcome in itself. The real question isn't what the model found. It's what changed in priority, preparation, cost, or timing because the organization knew something sooner. If nothing changes, the technology may be informative without ever becoming operationally valuable. PRACTICAL QUESTIONS What turns insight into an operating decision? Decision owner: Who is accountable for deciding what happens next? Decision timing: How much useful time exists between the signal and the point when options narrow? Corroboration: What additional evidence is needed before action is justified? Options: What legitimate responses exist, and what tradeoffs separate them? Execution readiness: Are people, procedures, access, materials, permits, systems, and operating windows aligned with the selected action? Verification: What evidence will show whether the decision produced the intended result? Learning: Will the outcome update the data, assumptions, thresholds, or model used for the next decision? SOUBEL practical interpretation: These questions organize the data-to-decision themes identified in the research inventory and connect them to Operational Trust. RESEARCH FOUNDATION Integrity and risk practice already depend on evidence integration and prioritization. The research inventory noted that risk-based inspection and integrity-management literature already provides methods for prioritizing assets and threats, while often treating inspection and monitoring data as inputs to those methods. The SOUBEL review focused on an additional practical question: how clearly does data credibility and uncertainty remain visible as information moves into prioritization and executive or operating decisions? The academic exercise did not establish a novel data-to-decision framework. It did reinforce an established operating truth: technical evidence creates more value when the organization can explain how it influenced the decision, what uncertainty remained, why one option was selected, and what evidence will determine whether the choice worked. Reference foundation: risk-based inspection and integrity-management literature inventoried in the SOUBEL research review · PHMSA integrity-management requirements and records expectations · data-confidence themes from digital-twin and corrosion literature. THE DECISION TEST What changes because the organization knows something sooner? The useful measure of prediction is the decision opportunity it creates and whether that opportunity becomes prepared work, a measurable outcome, and better evidence for what comes next. Explore Operational Trust → Digital Transformation & AI Expertise →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/digital-transformation-ai/sampling-bias/","title":"You Get What You Checked For | SOUBEL","h1":"You Get What You Checked For","description":"SOUBEL explains how inspection history, uneven coverage, legacy records and sampling patterns can shape the data used to train industrial AI models.","headings":"The dataset inherits the history behind it. | Here is why that is easy to miss. | Sometimes the model learns the inspection pattern too. | How was this data collected, and does that shape the answer we are getting?","text":"Knowledge Library · Digital Transformation & AI SAMPLING BIAS You Get What You Checked For Inspection data does not come from a random sample. It comes from decisions about where inspectors had access, which areas were flagged as risky, what regulations required, and what equipment was available at the time. All of that shapes the dataset long before anyone trains a model on it. Why sampling matters Back to Digital & AI Knowledge WHY IT MATTERS The dataset inherits the history behind it. A lot of that history is not sitting in one clean system. Plenty of integrity data still lives in binders in a field office, or in spreadsheets saved across 30-plus different folders, drives, and formats, each with its own habits for what got recorded, how consistently, and by whom. Consolidating that into a single dataset does not erase those old habits. It inherits them. Here is why that is easy to miss. “High findings” can mean “looked at closely,” not necessarily “worse condition.” An area inspected repeatedly may produce more recorded findings simply because it received more scrutiny than an area inspected once. A quiet record is not the same as a safe asset. Few or no findings can mean limited inspection or limited evidence, not necessarily better underlying condition. Older data was often collected differently, sometimes by hand. What was measured, how often, and with what tools has changed over time, and years of records may have started as paper before ever becoming digital. Scattered spreadsheets carry scattered standards. When integrity data has lived in dozens of separate files over the years, those files may not use the same fields, units, naming conventions, or judgment calls even before a model sees the data. Some locations get extra attention because something already looked off. That is normal, sensible engineering judgment, but it also means those records may represent targeted investigation rather than typical asset conditions. WHAT THE MODEL CAN LEARN Sometimes the model learns the inspection pattern too. A model trained on this kind of data can end up learning the inspection pattern itself: where people tended to look, what they tended to record, and how they recorded it, instead of learning only what is true about the equipment. This does not mean the data is bad or the model is wrong. It means the first question worth asking is not “What does the model predict?” It is “How was this data collected, and does that shape the answer we are getting?” THE QUESTION BEFORE THE PREDICTION How was this data collected, and does that shape the answer we are getting? That question belongs in model validation whenever historical inspection patterns, uneven coverage, legacy records, or changing inspection methods shape the training data. Next: Trustworthy Digital Twins → Digital Transformation & AI Expertise →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/digital-transformation-ai/trustworthy-digital-twins/","title":"Trustworthy Digital Twins | SOUBEL","h1":"Confidence Isn’t the Same as Accuracy. Even in 3D.","description":"Why digital-twin confidence depends on source quality, context, uncertainty and an evidence path that qualified users can challenge.","headings":"Data quality, context and uncertainty belong upstream of the decision. | What should the organization be able to explain? | The literature already points to interoperability, data quality and uncertainty as core challenges. | If the twin changes the decision, can the organization explain why?","text":"Knowledge Library · Digital Transformation & AI TRUSTWORTHY DIGITAL TWINS Confidence Isn’t the Same as Accuracy. Even in 3D. Raw data doesn’t become trustworthy because it entered a sophisticated model. A digital twin still needs enough visibility into source quality, context, and uncertainty to keep a modeled result from looking more certain than the evidence supports. Back to Digital & AI Knowledge THE EVIDENCE PATH Data quality, context and uncertainty belong upstream of the decision. Field Sensors & Measurements → Data Ingestion → Quality Check → Context & Provenance → Uncertainty → Digital Twin / AI → Output → Decision That becomes especially important when data from different systems are combined. Inspection records, SCADA, historians, GIS, maintenance logs, and engineering models may all describe the same asset from different angles. Integration does not erase differences in timing, method, accuracy, or purpose. A trustworthy twin should support challenge, not just visualization. Qualified users should be able to ask where a result came from, what evidence supports it, what is missing, and whether the output is even suitable for the decision being made. PRACTICAL QUESTIONS What should the organization be able to explain? Input lineage: Which measurements, records, models, and systems materially influence the output? Quality controls: What validation, cleaning, calibration checks, or data-quality rules occur before the model consumes the data? Context: Are asset identity, operating state, time, environment, procedure, and other decision-relevant conditions preserved? Uncertainty: Can the system communicate limitations, missingness, confidence, or sensitivity rather than only a point estimate? Model governance: Is the model version, change history, validation basis, and ownership understandable to the people relying on it? Decision connection: What action can the output influence, who owns that action, and what evidence will verify the result afterward? SOUBEL practical interpretation: These questions translate the research inventory into operating review points. They are not a certification standard for digital twins. RESEARCH FOUNDATION The literature already points to interoperability, data quality and uncertainty as core challenges. The research inventory reviewed recent pipeline digital-twin literature describing the integration of IoT sensors, physics models and machine learning for applications such as leak detection and corrosion prediction. Those reviews also identified uncertainty quantification and data interoperability as continuing research and implementation challenges. The academic exercise explored whether a pipeline-specific provenance framework could become a novel paper. Novelty was not established. The useful industry takeaway is more practical: a digital twin should preserve enough evidence about its inputs and assumptions for people to understand why an output deserves confidence and where review is still needed. Reference foundation: recent pipeline digital-twin reviews inventoried by SOUBEL · uncertainty-quantification literature · ISO 23247 and API RP 1160 identified in the research review for continued standards comparison. THE DECISION TEST If the twin changes the decision, can the organization explain why? The most useful digital twin is not the one with the most impressive visualization. It is the one whose evidence path remains understandable when the operating decision matters. Next: From Data to Operating Decision → Digital Transformation & AI Expertise →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/digital-transformation-ai/uncertainty-model-confidence/","title":"Uncertainty & Model Confidence | SOUBEL","h1":"Certainty Is Not a Default Setting.","description":"SOUBEL explains why precise industrial model outputs can still rest on uncertain measurements, assumptions and model choices, and why confidence belongs beside the answer.","headings":"Confidence belongs beside the answer. | What should a user understand before acting on a model result? | Digital-twin research already treats uncertainty as a decision problem. | Would the decision change if the uncertainty were understood differently?","text":"Knowledge Library · Digital Transformation & AI UNCERTAINTY & MODEL CONFIDENCE Certainty Is Not a Default Setting. A precise output can still rest on uncertain measurements, assumptions, and model choices. Good decision support makes that uncertainty visible before someone acts on the answer. Back to Digital & AI Knowledge WHY IT MATTERS Confidence belongs beside the answer. Industrial models combine sensor measurements, inspection findings, physics, assumptions, operating histories, and statistical relationships. Every one of those layers can introduce its own uncertainty, from instrumentation and field conditions, to simplifications in how the model represents the asset, to parameters that were estimated rather than directly measured. Recent pipeline digital-twin literature identifies uncertainty quantification as important to risk-informed decision-making, while still raising open questions about how fidelity and acceptable uncertainty should be defined in safety-critical applications. That's not a reason to distrust modeling. It's a reason to make confidence visible enough for qualified people to use the output responsibly. The real risk is false precision. A model can output a single clean number even when several assumptions underneath it are uncertain. If the interface only shows the result, a user may read more certainty into it than the evidence actually supports. Good decision support puts uncertainty in the room, not just the answer. PRACTICAL QUESTIONS What should a user understand before acting on a model result? Observed or inferred? Which parts of the result come from direct evidence, and which come from estimation or modeling? What is missing? Are there data gaps, stale inputs, sparse coverage, or operating conditions that the model does not represent well? What assumptions matter most? Which inputs or model choices could materially change the conclusion? How stable is the result? Does reasonable variation in the inputs produce the same decision, or a different one? How is confidence communicated? Does the user see a range, quality flag, scenario, confidence measure, or another indication of uncertainty? What would trigger review? Is there a clear condition under which the model output should be challenged, recalculated, or escalated? SOUBEL practical interpretation: These questions translate uncertainty themes from the research inventory into operational review questions. They are not a substitute for engineering or statistical uncertainty analysis. RESEARCH FOUNDATION Digital-twin research already treats uncertainty as a decision problem. The research inventory reviewed recent pipeline digital-twin literature that discusses measurement, model, and parameter uncertainty and the need to propagate uncertainty into risk-informed decisions. The review also identified the absence of a single unified standard for acceptable digital-twin fidelity or uncertainty thresholds in safety-critical settings. The original academic exercise considered whether a pipeline-specific trust framework could become a novel research contribution. Novelty was not established. What remains useful for industrial practice is the documented principle that uncertainty should not disappear between raw evidence and a decision-support output. Reference foundation: pipeline digital-twin reviews inventoried by SOUBEL · uncertainty-quantification literature · ISO 23247 and related digital-twin standards identified in the research review. THE DECISION TEST Would the decision change if the uncertainty were understood differently? If the answer could be yes, confidence and assumptions belong in the decision record, not only inside the model. Next: Sampling Bias in Integrity Data → Digital Transformation & AI Expertise →","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/pipeline-asset-integrity/","title":"Pipeline Integrity Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"Follow the evidence through the integrity cycle.","description":"A practical SOUBEL knowledge branch covering pipeline integrity fundamentals, threat families, assessment selection, direct assessment, field validation, integrity evidence, and decision continuity across energy and water infrastructure.","headings":"Know the condition. Understand the threats. | More than one threat may be present at the same time. | It is ongoing work, not a one-time inspection. | Different threats require different evidence. | Use the tool that can characterize the threat. | A pressure test answers a different integrity question. | Use a threat-specific pathway when the method is appropriate. | The letters aren’t the point. The mechanism is. | Where is external corrosion most likely, and what should be excavated? | Where can electrolyte collect inside the pipe, and where should the operator look first? | Do the operating history and environment support SCC susceptibility, and where should direct examination be concentrated? | From indication to observed condition. | Carry the assessment logic into the field. | Document what is actually there. | Reconcile prediction with reality. | The next decision depends on what the organization remembers. | Keep the reasoning attached to the evidence. | Software should make evidence easier to compare, not harder to understand. | Did the assessment lead to better knowledge of the asset? | Different pipeline systems operate under different requirements. | U.S. DOT Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration | U.S. Environmental Protection Agency","text":"Knowledge Library PIPELINE INTEGRITY Follow the evidence through the integrity cycle. Pipeline integrity is the ability of a pipeline system to remain fit for its intended service and perform safely, reliably, and effectively under the operating and environmental conditions it is expected to encounter. Explore this page 00 · Foundation 01 · Assessment Selection 02 · Direct Assessment 03 · Field Validation 04 · Integrity Evidence & Decision Continuity Decision Test Reference Framework Open the Knowledge Library Explore Asset Integrity Management 00 · FOUNDATION Know the condition. Understand the threats. Integrity is maintained over time by understanding what can threaten the pipeline, evaluating what is known about its condition, choosing assessment methods that fit the threat, acting on the evidence, and reassessing as conditions and information change. WHAT CAN THREATEN INTEGRITY More than one threat may be present at the same time. Corrosion and material degradation. External corrosion, internal corrosion, coating deterioration, stress corrosion cracking, and other forms of material loss or degradation. Material, manufacturing, and construction-related conditions. Defects associated with pipe materials, seams, welds, joints, fabrication, installation, or construction. Third-party and outside-force damage. Excavation damage, dents, gouges, impact, ground movement, geohazards, flooding, erosion, and other external loads or environmental forces. Operational and equipment-related conditions. Equipment or component failure, abnormal pressure or flow conditions, control-system problems, valve or regulator malfunction, incorrect operation, and operating changes that place the pipeline outside its normal or intended service conditions. Aging, service, and performance conditions. Leakage, loss of capacity, structural deterioration, declining service performance, and other age-related conditions that reduce the pipeline’s ability to perform as intended. HOW INTEGRITY IS MAINTAINED It is ongoing work, not a one-time inspection. Maintaining pipeline integrity includes preventing or controlling threats where possible, assessing condition using methods appropriate to the threat, analyzing the results with other relevant information, addressing conditions that may reduce integrity, applying preventive or mitigative measures, and reassessing over time. Pipeline integrity describes the condition and capability of the pipeline. Integrity management is the ongoing process used to preserve it. Explore AIM Technical Knowledge 01 · ASSESSMENT SELECTION Different threats require different evidence. ILI, pressure testing, direct assessment, direct examination, NDE, and other methods answer different questions. The first discipline is matching the assessment method to the threat, the pipeline configuration, the information required, and the limitations of the method. IN-LINE INSPECTION Use the tool that can characterize the threat. Tool technology, sizing capability, detection limits, inspection conditions, prior run history, validation results, and data quality determine what confidence can reasonably be placed in an ILI result. PRESSURE TESTING A pressure test answers a different integrity question. Hydrostatic and other pressure-test approaches can demonstrate pressure resistance and expose certain defects, but they do not provide the same location-specific condition information as inspection or direct examination. DIRECT ASSESSMENT Use a threat-specific pathway when the method is appropriate. ECDA, ICDA, and SCCDA are structured processes that integrate pre-assessment, indirect evidence, targeted examination, and post-assessment learning for specific threats. 02 · DIRECT ASSESSMENT The letters aren’t the point. The mechanism is. ECDA, ICDA, and SCCDA use a similar structure: pre-assessment, indirect evaluation, direct examination, and post-assessment. That similarity can make them look interchangeable. They are not. Each exists because a different damage mechanism behaves differently, develops under different conditions, and leaves different evidence. Same framework. Different physics. Use the wrong lens, and a technically complete assessment can still focus attention in the wrong place. ECDA · EXTERNAL CORROSION Where is external corrosion most likely, and what should be excavated? ECDA integrates pipeline history, corrosion-control information, coating and environmental context, complementary above-ground inspection methods, excavation findings, and post-assessment learning. Under 49 CFR 192.925, ECDA is a four-step process: pre-assessment, indirect inspection, direct examination, and post-assessment. Decision focus: identify and prioritize indications, determine excavation urgency, compare indirect evidence with actual pipe and coating condition, and use the findings to improve reassessment and corrosion-control decisions. Why cathodic protection (CP) matters here: CP performance, coating condition, interference, electrical continuity, and field measurement quality can materially affect the evidence used in an ECDA program. 49 CFR 192.925 · ECDA Corrosion & CP context ICDA · INTERNAL CORROSION Where can electrolyte collect inside the pipe, and where should the operator look first? ICDA uses pipeline-specific operating, elevation, flow, geometry, and other data to identify locations where liquid or electrolyte may reside and internal corrosion is most likely. For dry-gas ICDA under 49 CFR 192.927, the process includes pre-assessment, indirect inspection, detailed examination, and post-assessment monitoring. Decision focus: determine whether ICDA is feasible, define ICDA regions, predict likely liquid-holdup locations, examine the pipe at the locations most likely to contain corrosion, and validate the model against what is actually found. Important boundary: the federal dry-gas ICDA process is limited to normally dry natural-gas segments; the regulation contains additional requirements when electrolytes are normally present. 49 CFR 192.927 · ICDA SCCDA · STRESS CORROSION CRACKING Do the operating history and environment support SCC susceptibility, and where should direct examination be concentrated? SCCDA systematically integrates data about pipe, coating, cathodic protection, environment, operating stress, temperature, cyclic loading, and other susceptibility factors, then uses indirect surveys and direct examination to test whether SCC is present. Decision focus: identify susceptible segments, prioritize locations for examination, characterize discovered cracking, determine remediation, and establish reassessment based on the mechanism and conditions found. Important boundary: SCCDA is complementary to other SCC assessment methods such as ILI or hydrostatic testing with a spike test; it is not automatically a substitute for them. 49 CFR 192.929 · SCCDA The practical distinction: ECDA follows evidence of external corrosion. ICDA follows the conditions that can create internal corrosion. SCCDA follows susceptibility and evidence associated with stress-corrosion cracking. The common discipline is to predict, examine, compare, learn, and decide. PHMSA Direct Assessment overview 03 · FIELD VALIDATION From indication to observed condition. Integrity excavations and direct examination are where an assessment prediction meets the physical asset. NDE, coating observations, measurements, photographs, samples, metallurgical or laboratory work, and repair records can confirm, refine, or contradict what the assessment suggested. BEFORE THE DIG Carry the assessment logic into the field. The crew and technical team need to know what indication is being investigated, where it is expected, what measurements are required, and what evidence must be preserved. AT THE PIPE Document what is actually there. Observed coating condition, corrosion morphology, crack characteristics, dimensions, NDE results, environmental observations, and photographs become part of the decision record. AFTER THE DIG Reconcile prediction with reality. The value of direct examination is not only the repair. The finding should feed back into tool performance, prioritization logic, threat models, future assessment, and the asset record. 04 · INTEGRITY EVIDENCE & DECISION CONTINUITY The next decision depends on what the organization remembers. Assessment results, field findings, repairs, operating history, corrosion-control information, material properties, and prior decisions become more useful when they remain traceable over time. Integrity software can support that continuity; risk models and AI can extend analysis, but neither substitutes for reliable source data, context, validation, and qualified judgment. The useful record keeps assessment results, field findings, repair or mitigation, and the next integrity decision connected. TRACEABILITY Keep the reasoning attached to the evidence. Asset identity, location, date, method, tool or instrument, operating condition, units, assumptions, uncertainty, and disposition are what make a record useful later. DECISION SUPPORT Software should make evidence easier to compare, not harder to understand. Useful systems connect history, inspection, field evidence, remediation, risk, and follow-up so the next reviewer can see what changed and why the prior decision was made. THE DECISION TEST Did the assessment lead to better knowledge of the asset? A credible integrity process should leave the organization with more than a completed inspection, test, excavation, or repair. It should leave a stronger understanding of the threat, better evidence about asset condition, a clear record of what was decided and why, and better information for the next assessment. Asset Integrity Management Open SOUBEL Resources REFERENCE FRAMEWORK Different pipeline systems operate under different requirements. This page uses broad pipeline-integrity principles. Regulatory and technical requirements vary by pipeline service, material, jurisdiction, and operator. ENERGY PIPELINES U.S. DOT Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration PHMSA integrity-management requirements and guidance for regulated gas and hazardous-liquid pipelines, including 49 CFR Parts 192 and 195. Gas Transmission Integrity Management 49 CFR Part 195 WATER SYSTEMS U.S. Environmental Protection Agency EPA resources on drinking-water distribution system condition, integrity, asset management, maintenance, rehabilitation, and replacement. Drinking Water Distribution System Tools and Resources","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/knowledge-library/tank-terminal-integrity/","title":"Tank & Terminal Integrity Knowledge | SOUBEL","h1":"Terminal integrity is built through Operational Trust at every interface.","description":"SOUBEL connects storage-tank condition, corrosion, transfer systems, inspection, overfill prevention, containment, operating context, digital continuity and Asset Integrity Management across terminal facilities.","headings":"Integrity follows the product, the equipment, and the decision path. | A tank carries several integrity stories at once. | Condition below the product line can control the decision. | Geometry, corrosion, and service history matter together. | The tank boundary includes the places where systems connect. | Corrosion changes as the exposure changes. | Soil-side and internal exposure can tell different stories. | External environment and coating condition remain visible. | Buried and aboveground sections face different environments. | Protection systems need evidence that they are performing as intended. | The method should answer the integrity question. | Establish what can be observed and measured. | Floor condition requires appropriate coverage and interpretation. | Shape and support conditions can change the integrity question. | Inspection becomes decision support through qualified interpretation. | Integrity continues beyond the tank nozzle. | Configuration and service history shape the threat picture. | Mechanical condition affects both integrity and operability. | Transfer interfaces bring equipment, controls, and people together. | Prevention depends on equipment, controls, response time, and ownership working together. | Reliable level information has to lead to prepared action. | Containment condition is part of facility integrity. | The response begins before the event. | Lifecycle & Decision Continuity | Different parts of the system are governed by different technical and regulatory sources. | Terminal integrity crosses disciplines by design. | Protection, field evidence, and corrosion-control context | The management framework | Reliability, maintenance, and lifecycle value | Carry the decision basis into the work | Connected records, models, and decision support | Practical decision tools | Can the organization trace condition and decision basis across the tank, transfer system, controls, containment, and the work that follows?","text":"Knowledge Library · Tank & Terminal Integrity TANK & TERMINAL INTEGRITY Terminal integrity is built through Operational Trust at every interface. A terminal links storage, transfer, containment, corrosion control, inspection, instrumentation, maintenance, and people into one operating system. SOUBEL follows the interfaces where condition, evidence, ownership, and operating consequence meet. Explore this page The Terminal System Storage Tank Integrity Corrosion Across the Facility Inspection & Condition Evidence Transfer Systems Overfill, Containment & Release Prevention Lifecycle & Decision Continuity Authoritative Context Open the Knowledge Library AIM Application Domains THE TERMINAL SYSTEM Integrity follows the product, the equipment, and the decision path. Receipt, storage, transfer, loading, containment, and response depend on assets that share operating conditions and consequences. A useful integrity view keeps those relationships visible as condition changes and work moves across disciplines. Storage interface Tank bottom, shell, roof, foundation, product, water, coatings, corrosion control, nozzles, seals, and appurtenances. Transfer interface Piping, pumps, valves, manifolds, meters, loading systems, buried connections, supports, and mechanical equipment. Control interface Level measurement, alarms, overfill prevention, interlocks, shutdown functions, monitoring, and operating procedures. Execution interface Inspection, engineering, operations, maintenance, contractors, repair, verification, records, and return-to-service decisions. STORAGE TANK INTEGRITY A tank carries several integrity stories at once. Tank integrity is shaped by geometry, materials, service, environment, inspection history, corrosion, settlement, repairs, operating changes, and the condition of connected systems. Each part of the tank contributes different evidence. BOTTOM & FOUNDATION Condition below the product line can control the decision. Bottom-plate thickness, soil-side exposure, water and product-side corrosion, foundation condition, drainage, settlement, prior repairs, lining history, and cathodic-protection performance can all affect remaining integrity. SHELL & ROOF Geometry, corrosion, and service history matter together. Shell thickness, welds, nozzles, roof condition, floating-roof systems, seals, drainage, atmospheric exposure, coating condition, distortion, and operating history contribute to the condition picture. APPURTENANCES & CONNECTIONS The tank boundary includes the places where systems connect. Nozzles, piping interfaces, mixers, gauges, vents, ladders, platforms, roof penetrations, grounding, instrumentation, and other attachments create their own inspection and maintenance questions. API 653 context CORROSION ACROSS THE FACILITY Corrosion changes as the exposure changes. A terminal can place steel against soil, atmosphere, product, water, coatings, linings, insulation, concrete, and electrical systems within the same operating facility. Corrosion management depends on recognizing the environment and mechanism behind the evidence before selecting the response. TANK BOTTOM & FOUNDATION Soil-side and internal exposure can tell different stories. Foundation moisture, soil chemistry, drainage, bottom configuration, coatings, cathodic protection, internal water accumulation, product chemistry, lining condition, and prior thickness history can shape bottom-corrosion risk. SHELL, ROOF & ATMOSPHERE External environment and coating condition remain visible. Atmospheric exposure, water traps, roof drainage, seals, coastal or industrial environments, coating breakdown, crevices, and local geometry can create different corrosion patterns across the same tank. TRANSFER PIPING Buried and aboveground sections face different environments. Internal service, dead legs, low points, external coatings, cathodic protection where applicable, atmospheric exposure, insulation, supports, drainage, and changing operating service all influence the evidence. PROTECTION & VERIFICATION Protection systems need evidence that they are performing as intended. Coatings, linings, cathodic protection, monitoring, inspection, thickness history, field measurements, and repair verification become more useful when the operating and environmental context remains attached. Environment → Mechanism → Protection → Evidence → Decision Explore Corrosion & CP Knowledge API RP 651 / 652 source INSPECTION & CONDITION EVIDENCE The method should answer the integrity question. Visual inspection, thickness measurement, floor scanning, weld and leak testing, dimensional surveys, settlement evaluation, remote inspection, and engineering assessment each contribute different evidence. The decision depends on capability, coverage, limitations, uncertainty, and how the result compares with operating and historical context. VISUAL & THICKNESS EVIDENCE Establish what can be observed and measured. Visual condition, UT thickness, corrosion patterns, coating or lining condition, appurtenances, leaks, deformation, and service history help establish where additional evaluation may be needed. BOTTOM ASSESSMENT Floor condition requires appropriate coverage and interpretation. MFL and other bottom-inspection techniques can support broad floor screening and characterization when capability, calibration, accessibility, confirmation methods, and limitations remain understood. GEOMETRY & SETTLEMENT Shape and support conditions can change the integrity question. Settlement surveys, verticality, roundness, dimensional measurement, laser scanning, foundation observations, and historical comparison can help identify movement or distortion that warrants evaluation. ENGINEERING ASSESSMENT Inspection becomes decision support through qualified interpretation. Fitness-for-service, remaining-life assessment, risk-based inspection, repair planning, and return-to-service decisions combine condition evidence with materials, loads, service, consequence, uncertainty, and governing criteria. Integrity Methods & Decision Inputs TRANSFER SYSTEMS Integrity continues beyond the tank nozzle. Terminal piping and transfer equipment carry product between storage and receipt or delivery points. Their condition affects containment, throughput, environmental exposure, maintenance planning, and the ability to safely isolate or respond when conditions change. PIPING & MANIFOLDS Configuration and service history shape the threat picture. Aboveground and buried piping, dead legs, low points, supports, flanges, drains, vents, manifolds, previous modifications, and service changes all contribute to inspection and corrosion decisions. PUMPS, VALVES & METERS Mechanical condition affects both integrity and operability. Leakage, vibration, sealing, isolation capability, pressure and flow behavior, maintenance history, materials, and connected instrumentation influence reliability and response. LOADING & UNLOADING Transfer interfaces bring equipment, controls, and people together. Loading racks, arms, hoses, couplings, valves, grounding or bonding, metering, containment, alarms, procedures, and operator actions form a connected transfer boundary. OVERFILL, CONTAINMENT & RELEASE PREVENTION Prevention depends on equipment, controls, response time, and ownership working together. Level information, alarms, independent protection layers, transfer procedures, containment, inspections, and emergency response each address a different part of release prevention. Their effectiveness depends on clear operating limits and a response path that survives the handoff. OVERFILL PREVENTION Reliable level information has to lead to prepared action. Tank capacity, operating limits, level measurement, alarms, shutdown actions, transfer rates, response time, testing, maintenance, and accountability form the overfill-prevention system. SECONDARY CONTAINMENT Containment condition is part of facility integrity. Dikes, berms, walls, floors, drainage controls, penetrations, valves, accumulated water, inspection findings, and maintenance affect how a facility limits the consequence of a release. RELEASE RESPONSE The response begins before the event. Isolation capability, emergency shutdown, procedures, access, communications, spill planning, maintenance condition, and field readiness determine how quickly a facility can control and learn from a release. API 2350 · Overfill Prevention EPA · Aboveground Storage Tanks / SPCC LIFECYCLE & DECISION CONTINUITY Lifecycle & Decision Continuity Design basis, construction and repair records, inspection history, tank geometry, corrosion rates, cathodic-protection and coating history, operating service, alarms, modifications, transfer-system changes, field findings, and verification become more valuable when the facility can connect them over time. Integrity platforms, inspection databases, connected asset records, 3D models, monitoring systems, analytics, and digital twins can help preserve that continuity when source, asset identity, context, assumptions, changes, and verification remain traceable. AIM Across the Asset Lifecycle Digital & AI Knowledge AUTHORITATIVE CONTEXT Different parts of the system are governed by different technical and regulatory sources. SOUBEL provides orientation and connects the decision context. Current standards, regulations, company procedures, qualified technical authority, and asset-specific engineering govern requirements, calculations, acceptance criteria, inspection intervals, repair decisions, and compliance. API 653 Tank Inspection, Repair, Alteration & Reconstruction Core in-service aboveground storage-tank inspection and repair context. API RP 651 / RP 652 Tank-bottom corrosion control Cathodic protection and linings for aboveground petroleum storage tank bottoms. API 2350 Overfill Prevention Management-system and protection considerations for petroleum storage-tank transfers. API 2610 Terminal & Tank Facilities Design, construction, operation, maintenance, and inspection context for terminal and tank facilities. EPA · 40 CFR PART 112 SPCC context Aboveground oil storage may be subject to Spill Prevention, Control, and Countermeasure requirements. SOUBEL · AIM Governance & Assurance Connect roles, decision authority, procedures, traceability, verification, assurance, and learning. Regulatory applicability and standard requirements depend on facility, service, jurisdiction, asset configuration, and other conditions. Current primary sources should be verified for each specific decision. CONNECTED KNOWLEDGE Terminal integrity crosses disciplines by design. CORROSION & CP Protection, field evidence, and corrosion-control context Connect coatings, cathodic protection, monitoring, field measurements, interference, and decision context. ASSET INTEGRITY MANAGEMENT The management framework Keep condition, evidence, decisions, execution, verification, governance, and learning connected across the lifecycle. ASSET PERFORMANCE Reliability, maintenance, and lifecycle value Connect condition and operating context to reliability, maintenance strategy, economics, and performance decisions. EVIDENCE TO EXECUTION Carry the decision basis into the work Preserve technical meaning across handoffs, field execution, verification, and learning. DIGITAL & AI Connected records, models, and decision support Explore provenance, uncertainty, digital twins, analytics, and trustworthy data-to-decision continuity. SOUBEL RESOURCES Practical decision tools Use asset-condition, lifecycle, reliability, maintenance, metallurgy, and integrity decision resources. THE TERMINAL INTEGRITY TEST Can the organization trace condition and decision basis across the tank, transfer system, controls, containment, and the work that follows? Operational Trust grows when evidence remains connected across the interfaces where terminal integrity is actually managed. Asset Integrity Management Return to the Knowledge Library","type":"Knowledge Library"},{"url":"/markets/","title":"Markets | SOUBEL","h1":"Oil and Gas: How It Moves from the Ground to Everyday Use","description":"A plain-language view of how oil and gas moves from the ground to everyday use, and SOUBEL","headings":"Understanding Upstream, Midstream, and Downstream | Where SOUBEL’s market experience is concentrated. | Midstream & Pipelines | Downstream & Refining | Utilities & Industrial Infrastructure | Shared questions across markets.","text":"MARKETS Oil and Gas: How It Moves from the Ground to Everyday Use Oil and gas move through a series of connected steps before reaching the fuels, products, and services people use every day. Upstream brings oil and natural gas out of the ground. Midstream moves, processes, and stores it. Downstream refines and distributes it. Alongside those activities, electric power and grid infrastructure, water systems, and industrial facilities support the broader network that keeps industry and communities running. SOUBEL focuses on the parts of this landscape where asset condition, integrity, reliability, field operations, technology, regulation, and commercial decisions come together. THE ENERGY VALUE CHAIN Understanding Upstream, Midstream, and Downstream The oil and gas industry is commonly described in three parts. Upstream is where oil and natural gas are found and produced. Midstream connects production to the rest of the market through gathering systems, processing, pipelines, transportation, terminals, and storage. Downstream turns those raw materials into fuels and other useful products through refining, processing, distribution, and delivery. SOUBEL MARKET FOCUS Where SOUBEL’s market experience is concentrated. The strongest body of work spans three primary market areas where technical knowledge, operating conditions, and business decisions are closely connected. 01 · MIDSTREAM & PIPELINES Midstream & Pipelines Pipelines and midstream systems move energy across long distances and through complex networks of gathering lines, transmission pipelines, compressor and pump stations, processing facilities, terminals, tanks, and storage. SOUBEL’s experience in this market includes corrosion and cathodic protection, pipeline integrity, asset performance, field data, integrity management, and the commercial decisions surrounding the technologies and services used to support these systems. Explore Midstream & Pipelines → 02 · DOWNSTREAM & REFINING Downstream & Refining Refineries and downstream facilities turn crude oil and other feedstocks into fuels and products used across transportation, industry, and everyday life. These environments bring together process equipment, piping, tanks, terminals, inspection, maintenance, reliability, turnarounds, corrosion management, and the constant need to balance operating performance with risk, cost, and production demands. Explore Downstream & Refining → 03 · UTILITIES & INDUSTRIAL INFRASTRUCTURE Utilities & Industrial Infrastructure Electric power and grid infrastructure, water systems, industrial facilities, and other critical infrastructure rely on many of the same disciplines that support oil and gas operations: asset condition, maintenance, reliability, monitoring, corrosion control, field execution, and trustworthy operational information. SOUBEL’s experience in this area includes the systems and decisions that help infrastructure owners understand what is happening, what requires attention, and where technology can improve visibility and performance. Explore Utilities & Industrial Infrastructure → ACROSS THE SYSTEM Shared questions across markets. A pipeline, refinery, electric grid, water system, and industrial facility may look very different, but decisions about inspection, maintenance, repair, risk, technology, and investment often begin with the same basic questions. What is the asset telling us? Can the information be trusted? What changed? What happens if action is delayed? Which option makes sense technically, operationally, and financially? Those questions connect integrity, reliability, digital technology, regulation, field execution, and commercial strategy across the industries SOUBEL studies. Understanding the market is only the beginning. The deeper work is understanding how the asset, the evidence, the people, and the decision fit together. EXPLORE THE MARKETS Midstream & Pipelines → Downstream & Refining → Utilities & Industrial Infrastructure →","type":"Market"},{"url":"/markets/downstream-refining/","title":"Downstream & Refining | SOUBEL","h1":"Downstream & Refining","description":"A plain-language view of refinery operating systems, asset integrity, corrosion, reliability, maintenance, operational risk, technology, and lifecycle economics.","headings":"A refinery is a connected system, not a collection of isolated assets. | Crude Receipt | Separation | Conversion & Treating | Blending | Storage & Transfer | Separation, conversion, treating, and blending | Shared systems that keep the facility operating | Tankage, loading, unloading, and movement | The equipment carrying the operating burden | Keep the facility available while managing condition, risk, work, and consequence. | Keep interconnected systems performing. | Understand degradation before it becomes loss of containment. | Convert condition and risk into executable work. | Consider consequence across the connected facility. | Use technology where it improves a decision. | Technical need still competes for resources. | Earlier insight matters when the organization can turn it into better work. | Technical context connected to operating and commercial decisions. | Asset Integrity Management | Corrosion & Cathodic Protection | Asset Performance | Operational Risk | Digital Transformation & AI | Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts","text":"Markets Downstream & Refining Refineries are interconnected operating environments where process units, utilities, storage, transfer systems, and supporting assets have to perform together. Reliability depends on understanding condition, consequence, maintenance timing, operating risk, production requirements, and economics in the same decision. Explore the refinery system WHAT THIS MARKET INCLUDES A refinery is a connected system, not a collection of isolated assets. Crude receipt, separation, conversion, treating, blending, storage, and transfer are linked by piping, rotating equipment, vessels, exchangers, instrumentation, utilities, and supporting infrastructure. A change in one part of the facility can change risk, maintenance priorities, throughput, or economics somewhere else. 01 Crude Receipt Feedstock enters the facility through pipeline, terminal, storage, and transfer systems. → 02 Separation Distillation separates crude into fractions that move to additional processing. → 03 Conversion & Treating Process units change, remove, or upgrade components to meet product requirements. → 04 Blending Streams are combined and controlled to produce finished-product specifications. → 05 Storage & Transfer Finished products are stored, measured, loaded, and transferred to the next market connection. Utilities & Offsites Steam, power, water, flare and relief systems, cooling, treatment, and other shared services support the process from end to end. Simplified refinery system overview. Actual refinery configurations, process units, feedstocks, products, and operating arrangements vary by facility. PROCESS UNITS Separation, conversion, treating, and blending Interconnected process equipment where throughput, condition, operating limits, and product requirements meet. UTILITIES & OFFSITES Shared systems that keep the facility operating Power, steam, water, cooling, flare and relief systems, treatment, and supporting infrastructure. STORAGE & TRANSFER Tankage, loading, unloading, and movement Systems that receive feedstocks, stage intermediate streams, store products, and connect the refinery to transportation networks. FACILITY ASSETS The equipment carrying the operating burden Piping, vessels, exchangers, rotating equipment, instrumentation, electrical systems, structures, and related assets. WHAT OWNERS HAVE TO MANAGE Keep the facility available while managing condition, risk, work, and consequence. RELIABILITY & AVAILABILITY Keep interconnected systems performing. Process equipment, utilities, storage, transfer systems, and supporting assets have to remain available while operating rates, maintenance requirements, and outage exposure continually change. ASSET INTEGRITY & CORROSION Understand degradation before it becomes loss of containment. Inspection evidence, corrosion mechanisms, operating history, materials, process conditions, and engineering assessment support decisions about monitoring, mitigation, repair, replacement, and remaining life. MAINTENANCE & TURNAROUNDS Convert condition and risk into executable work. Work has to be prioritized, scoped, resourced, and sequenced around operating constraints, planned outages, turnaround windows, materials, labor, and the cost of delay. PROCESS & OPERATIONAL RISK Consider consequence across the connected facility. Equipment degradation, operating change, process interactions, loss of containment, and production impacts can move risk beyond the boundary of a single asset. DATA & TECHNOLOGY Use technology where it improves a decision. Monitoring, analytics, integrated data, digital twins, and AI create value when they improve visibility, timing, prioritization, traceability, or execution. ECONOMICS & CAPITAL Technical need still competes for resources. Maintenance burden, downtime exposure, production impact, remaining life, capital alternatives, and cost of delay shape what an economically defensible response looks like. FROM CONDITION TO ACTION Earlier insight matters when the organization can turn it into better work. The operating value is not another dashboard. It is the ability to connect evidence to a decision, the decision to executable work, and the completed work to better evidence for what comes next. Evidence Condition, inspection, monitoring, process, and operating context → Decision Risk, consequence, priority, timing, and economics → Work Scope, plan, resources, execution, and verification → Result Operating outcome, asset response, and lessons learned → Better Evidence What the next inspection, maintenance, technology, or capital decision can use WHERE SOUBEL CONNECTS Technical context connected to operating and commercial decisions. SOUBEL connects asset condition, integrity, reliability, operational risk, digital capability, and commercial value so technical evidence can move through the organization without losing the context required for action. INTEGRITY Asset Integrity Management Inspection, degradation, engineering evidence, prioritization, execution, verification, and defensible response. CORROSION Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Corrosion mechanisms, mitigation, monitoring, field evidence, and integrity context. PERFORMANCE Asset Performance Condition, reliability, maintenance, operating context, economics, and lifecycle value. RISK Operational Risk Consequence, governance, traceability, uncertainty, and decision confidence. DIGITAL Digital Transformation & AI Operational data, monitoring, analytics, digital twins, AI, workflow fit, and governance. GROWTH Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts Value cases, strategic accounts, commercialization, market development, and adoption pathways. EXPLORE RELATED WORK Markets Overview Asset Integrity Management Asset Performance Knowledge Resources","type":"Market"},{"url":"/markets/midstream-pipelines/","title":"Midstream & Pipelines | SOUBEL","h1":"Midstream & Pipelines","description":"A plain-language view of natural gas and liquids midstream infrastructure, pipeline integrity, reliability, field execution, digital capability, and commercial decisions.","headings":"Infrastructure that moves, conditions, stores, and delivers energy. | Keep product moving while protecting the assets that make movement possible. | Maintain flow and availability. | Understand condition and act on credible evidence. | Connect the decision to the work and the record. | Use technology where it improves an operating decision. | Technical context connected to operating and commercial decisions. | Asset Integrity Management | Corrosion & Cathodic Protection | Asset Performance | Digital Transformation & AI | Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts","text":"Markets Midstream & Pipelines Midstream connects production to processing, storage, transportation, and delivery. Natural gas and liquids move through different systems, but both depend on reliable infrastructure, sound field execution, credible operating information, and disciplined lifecycle decisions. Explore Technical Context WHAT THIS MARKET INCLUDES Infrastructure that moves, conditions, stores, and delivers energy. Gathering systems, processing facilities, compressor and pump stations, transmission pipelines, measurement, storage, terminals, and delivery connections form the physical network between production and the markets that use the product. Gas Well Pads / Gathering Collects raw natural gas from production sites and moves it into the gathering network. Processing Separates and conditions raw natural gas so it can enter transmission and market systems. Compression Raises gas pressure so natural gas can continue moving through the pipeline network. Gas Metering Measures natural gas flow for balancing, custody transfer, and operational control. Gas Transmission Pipeline Moves natural gas over long distances between processing and delivery points. Utility Users Receive natural gas for electric generation or local utility service. Industrial Users Receive natural gas for plant operations, manufacturing, or other industrial use. Oil Wells / Gathering Collects crude oil and liquids from production sites and moves them into the gathering network. Pump Station Provides the pressure needed to move crude oil and liquids through the pipeline system. Liquids Metering Measures crude or liquids flow for custody transfer, balancing, and operational control. Liquids Transmission Pipeline Moves crude oil or other liquids over long distances between gathering, storage, and terminal facilities. Storage Tank Farm Holds crude oil or liquids temporarily for balancing, staging, and delivery. Terminal Receives, stores, and transfers product to the next transportation or delivery mode. Hover or tap a marker for a quick asset definition. WHAT OWNERS HAVE TO MANAGE Keep product moving while protecting the assets that make movement possible. RELIABILITY Maintain flow and availability. Pipeline, compression, pumping, storage, and terminal assets have to perform under changing operating conditions without losing sight of maintenance burden, capacity, and service commitments. INTEGRITY Understand condition and act on credible evidence. Inspection, corrosion data, operating history, engineering analysis, and field findings support decisions about monitoring, excavation, repair, mitigation, and future assessment. FIELD & REGULATORY EXECUTION Connect the decision to the work and the record. Planning, qualifications, field execution, verification, and documentation have to preserve enough context for the response to be understood, completed, and supported later. DATA & TECHNOLOGY Use technology where it improves an operating decision. Remote monitoring, integrated data, analytics, digital twins, and AI are useful when they improve visibility, traceability, prioritization, or the timing of action. WHERE SOUBEL CONNECTS Technical context connected to operating and commercial decisions. SOUBEL’s experience spans the physical asset, the integrity problem, the operational data layer, and the commercial decision. That combination helps connect field conditions to decisions about maintenance, technology, investment, and growth. INTEGRITY Asset Integrity Management Inspection, threats, engineering evidence, prioritization, field execution, verification, and defensible response. CORROSION Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Corrosion control, cathodic protection, field evidence, mitigation, monitoring, and integrity context. PERFORMANCE Asset Performance Condition, reliability, maintenance, operating context, economics, and lifecycle value. DIGITAL Digital Transformation & AI Operational data, remote monitoring, digital twins, AI, workflow fit, and governance. GROWTH Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts Value cases, market development, strategic accounts, commercialization, and adoption pathways. EXPLORE RELATED WORK Markets Overview Pipeline Integrity Knowledge Resources","type":"Market"},{"url":"/markets/utilities-industrial-infrastructure/","title":"Utilities & Industrial Infrastructure | SOUBEL","h1":"Utilities & Industrial Infrastructure","description":"A plain-language view of power generation, electric grid infrastructure, water and wastewater systems, and industrial facilities through asset condition, reliability, field execution, technology, and lifecycle decisions.","headings":"Four operating environments. | Power Generation | Transmission & Distribution | Water & Wastewater | Industrial Facilities | The infrastructure behind everyday service and industrial operations. | Keep critical systems available, reliable, and maintainable. | Understand asset condition. | Protect reliability and continuity. | Prioritize maintenance and capital. | Apply technology to a specific operating need. | Technical context connected to operating and commercial decisions.","text":"MARKETS Utilities & Industrial Infrastructure Power generation, the electric grid, water and wastewater systems, and industrial facilities support the services people and businesses depend on every day. These environments operate differently, but each relies on physical assets, field execution, reliable information, maintenance, and investment decisions made over long asset lives. WHAT THIS MARKET INCLUDES Four operating environments. Different systems, different assets, and different operating demands, but the same expectation: keep essential services available and dependable. 01 Power Generation Natural gas and nuclear generation, along with the plant systems required to keep electricity available. 02 Transmission & Distribution Transmission lines, substations, and distribution networks that move electricity from generation to end users. 03 Water & Wastewater Treatment, pumping, storage, distribution, collection, and wastewater systems that keep water moving reliably. 04 Industrial Facilities Process equipment, utilities, piping, electrical systems, and facilities that support continuous industrial operations. SYSTEM OVERVIEW The infrastructure behind everyday service and industrial operations. These are distinct operating environments, not one value chain. What connects them is the need to understand asset condition, protect reliability, support field execution, and make sound lifecycle decisions. WHAT OWNERS HAVE TO MANAGE Keep critical systems available, reliable, and maintainable. Understand asset condition. Inspection, monitoring, maintenance history, corrosion, failures, repairs, and field observations build the picture of what needs attention and when. Protect reliability and continuity. Maintenance and capital work have to fit service requirements, operating constraints, outage windows, redundancy, resilience, and the consequences of interruption. Prioritize maintenance and capital. Owners continually decide what to repair, replace, upgrade, monitor, or defer while balancing risk, lifecycle cost, remaining life, capacity, and available resources. Apply technology to a specific operating need. Remote monitoring, connected sensors, analytics, digital twins, and AI create value when they improve visibility, reduce decision friction, support field execution, or create a better window for action. WHERE SOUBEL CONNECTS Technical context connected to operating and commercial decisions. SOUBEL’s experience spans the physical asset, the integrity problem, the operational data layer, and the commercial decision. That combination helps connect field conditions to the decisions organizations make about maintenance, technology, investment, and growth. Asset Performance Condition, reliability, maintenance, operating context, and lifecycle value. Corrosion & Cathodic Protection Corrosion control, field evidence, mitigation, monitoring, and integrity context. Digital Transformation & AI Operational data, remote monitoring, digital twins, AI, workflow fit, and governance. Commercial Growth & Strategic Accounts Value cases, market development, strategic accounts, commercialization, and adoption pathways. EXPLORE RELATED WORK Markets Overview → Asset Performance Knowledge → Resources →","type":"Market"},{"url":"/resources/","title":"Resources | SOUBEL Decision Tools & Guides","h1":"From technical understanding to verified execution.","description":"SOUBEL practical guides and decision tools organized as a connected system: understand the technical landscape, challenge changed conditions, compare credible options, and carry decisions through execution and learning.","headings":"Understand → Challenge → Compare → Execute & Learn | Pipeline Integrity, Metallurgy & PHMSA | When Asset Condition Changes | Lifecycle Decision Comparison | Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook | Use the Library when the tool raises a deeper question. | Assessment, validation, and integrity evidence | Protection systems and field evidence | Condition, reliability, maintenance, and lifecycle value | Provenance, uncertainty, models, and decision support | Decision support, not substituted authority.","text":"SOUBEL · Resources PRACTICAL DECISION TOOLS From technical understanding to verified execution. Four SOUBEL resources work together as one decision sequence: understand the technical landscape, challenge what changed, compare credible choices, and carry the decision through execution and learning. THE SOUBEL DECISION SYSTEM Understand → Challenge → Compare → Execute & Learn The tools are designed for different moments in the same decision. Each named resource below leads first to its web framework page. The PDF is the downloadable working version. 01 · Understand Pipeline Integrity, Metallurgy & PHMSA Executive Technical Fluency Guide. Organized around conversations, not calculations. A plain-language guide to integrity architecture, materials, ILI and NDE evidence, engineering assessment, and PHMSA context. Purpose: Build enough technical fluency to participate responsibly in the conversation without crossing the boundary into engineering responsibility. Explore the guide Download PDF → 02 · Challenge When Asset Condition Changes 10 Questions for a Defensible Decision. A concise sequence for testing the signal, evidence quality, operating context, interpretation, consequence, options, and reconsideration triggers. Purpose: Test whether the evidence and interpretation can support the decision before the organization commits to action. Explore the framework Download PDF → 03 · Compare Lifecycle Decision Comparison Practical Decision Tool. Compare immediate expenditure, OPEX, downtime, remaining life, deferral exposure, emergency response, uncertainty, reversibility, and organizational capacity. Purpose: Make the economic and operational consequences visible before the organization chooses among credible asset options. Explore the comparison Download PDF → 04 · Execute & Learn Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook Working Decision Record. See It, Challenge It, Plan It, Hand It Off, Do the Work, Verify It, and Learn From It. Purpose: Keep the reasoning with the work so evidence, assumptions, operating context, field findings, verification, and learning survive the handoff. Explore the workbook Download PDF → CONNECTED KNOWLEDGE Use the Library when the tool raises a deeper question. Resources support the working decision. The Knowledge Library supplies deeper context around the technical evidence, asset condition, digital information, and integrity questions behind that decision. Asset Integrity Management provides the wider management framework. PIPELINE INTEGRITY Assessment, validation, and integrity evidence Go deeper into ILI, direct assessment, field validation, remediation, data, risk, and regulatory context. CORROSION & CP Protection systems and field evidence Explore CP fundamentals, materials, surveys, interference, monitoring, corrosion-control evidence, and integrity context. ASSET PERFORMANCE Condition, reliability, maintenance, and lifecycle value Connect asset health and operating context to maintenance strategy, economics, digital systems, and decisions. DIGITAL & AI Provenance, uncertainty, models, and decision support Explore the evidence beneath industrial AI, digital twins, data quality, sampling bias, and model confidence. Open the Knowledge Library Asset Integrity Management USE & RESPONSIBILITY Decision support, not substituted authority. These materials support better questions, clearer handoffs, and more defensible decisions. They do not replace applicable standards, regulations, procedures, licensed engineering judgment, qualified technical authority, safety requirements, or legal advice for a specific asset or decision. Regulatory requirements can change; current primary-source requirements should always be verified.","type":"Resource"},{"url":"/resources/lifecycle-decision-comparison/","title":"Lifecycle Decision Comparison | SOUBEL","h1":"Lifecycle Decision Comparison","description":"A practical SOUBEL decision tool for comparing credible asset options across cost, risk, timing, remaining life, reversibility, deferral exposure, and organizational consequence.","headings":"The cheapest option today is not always the lowest-cost decision. | What the comparison is designed to make visible. | Immediate expenditure | Ongoing OPEX | Downtime and production impact | Remaining life | Cost of deferral | Emergency response exposure | Uncertainty | Reversibility / future flexibility | Organizational capacity | What happens to the organization if the condition becomes an event? | Once the option is chosen, the reasoning has to survive the handoff.","text":"SOUBEL · Resources · Asset Performance COMPARE Lifecycle Decision Comparison Compare the decision, not just the repair cost. This practical tool helps teams compare credible asset options across cost, risk, operating impact, remaining life, uncertainty, reversibility, deferral exposure, and the organizational capacity consumed when a condition becomes an event. Download the comparison tool View the full decision system WHY IT EXISTS The cheapest option today is not always the lowest-cost decision. Asset decisions often get reduced to direct expenditure: repair now, monitor, defer, replace, or redesign. But the real business consequence can extend far beyond the purchase order. This tool widens the comparison so that timing, downtime, remaining life, emergency exposure, future flexibility, uncertainty, and the organizational cost of an event are visible in the same decision. DECISION CRITERIA What the comparison is designed to make visible. Immediate expenditure The direct capital or maintenance spend required by each option. Ongoing OPEX Recurring operating, inspection, monitoring, maintenance, or support cost. Downtime and production impact The operational consequence of taking the asset out of service or constraining performance. Remaining life How each option changes useful life, replacement timing, or future intervention needs. Cost of deferral The economic and operational consequence of waiting rather than acting now. Emergency response exposure The potential cost and disruption if a manageable condition becomes an event. Uncertainty What is known, what remains uncertain, and how uncertainty differs across options. Reversibility / future flexibility Whether a choice preserves future options or locks the organization into a path that is difficult to change. Organizational capacity The people, attention, and normal work displaced if the condition escalates into an event. THE HIDDEN COST What happens to the organization if the condition becomes an event? A failure or major operational event does not consume only repair dollars. It can pull operations, maintenance, safety, environmental, engineering, procurement, leadership, legal, communications, and other functions away from normal work. That displaced capacity is part of the economic consequence, even when it never appears as a line item on the repair estimate. NEXT IN THE DECISION Once the option is chosen, the reasoning has to survive the handoff. The Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook carries evidence, assumptions, execution context, field findings, verification, and learning through the work. Continue to Execute & Learn","type":"Resource"},{"url":"/resources/pipeline-integrity-metallurgy-phmsa/","title":"Pipeline Integrity, Metallurgy & PHMSA | Executive Technical Fluency | SOUBEL","h1":"Pipeline Integrity, Metallurgy & PHMSA","description":"A plain-language SOUBEL guide to pipeline integrity, metallurgy, ILI and NDE evidence, engineering assessment, and PHMSA context for leaders who need technical fluency without performing engineering calculations.","headings":"Technical fluency without pretending to be the engineer. | From technical evidence to informed conversation. | Pipeline integrity architecture | Metallurgy and material behavior | ILI, NDE, and engineering evidence | PHMSA and regulatory context | When the condition changes, challenge the evidence before choosing the response.","text":"SOUBEL · Resources · Pipeline Integrity UNDERSTAND Pipeline Integrity, Metallurgy & PHMSA Executive Technical Fluency Guide. Organized around conversations, not calculations, this guide helps leaders understand enough of the technical landscape to ask better questions, follow the evidence, and participate responsibly in integrity and regulatory discussions. Download the guide View the full decision system PURPOSE Technical fluency without pretending to be the engineer. Senior commercial, operational, and business leaders are often expected to discuss integrity evidence, material behavior, repair criteria, regulatory requirements, and engineering recommendations without performing the calculations themselves. The guide creates a practical bridge between specialist language and executive decision-making. It focuses on what the evidence means, what questions should be asked, and where qualified technical authority remains essential. Regulatory context: 2026 PHMSA repair-criteria rulemaking Last reviewed: August 2026 WHAT THE GUIDE HELPS TRANSLATE From technical evidence to informed conversation. Pipeline integrity architecture How inspection, assessment, repair, monitoring, and integrity-management activities relate to one another. Metallurgy and material behavior Why material properties, degradation mechanisms, fracture behavior, and defect context matter to decision quality. ILI, NDE, and engineering evidence What different forms of evidence can tell a decision-maker, and where uncertainty or limitations still remain. PHMSA and regulatory context How changing regulatory expectations affect the questions leaders need to ask before relying on technical conclusions. A USEFUL RULE Speak the conversation, not the calculation. Technical fluency is not a substitute for engineering responsibility. Its value is helping leaders understand the issue well enough to challenge assumptions, recognize uncertainty, connect evidence to consequence, and know when specialist authority is required. NEXT IN THE DECISION When the condition changes, challenge the evidence before choosing the response. The next SOUBEL tool turns technical understanding into a practical decision sequence. Continue to When Asset Condition Changes","type":"Resource"},{"url":"/resources/reliability-maintenance-decision-workbook/","title":"Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook | SOUBEL","h1":"Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook","description":"A practical SOUBEL workbook for carrying evidence, assumptions, operating context, decision rationale, execution readiness, field findings, verification, and learning through maintenance work.","headings":"A work order can say what to do. It does not always carry why. | From first signal to learning. | What would be lost if the next person received only the work instruction? | Context that should survive execution. | The workbook is the execution layer, not the starting point.","text":"SOUBEL · Resources · Reliability & Maintenance EXECUTE & LEARN Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook Carry the decision through the work. A practical working record for preserving evidence, assumptions, operating context, decision rationale, execution readiness, field findings, verification, and learning from the first signal through completed work. Download the workbook View the full decision system THE HANDOFF PROBLEM A work order can say what to do. It does not always carry why. Execution becomes fragile when the physical work is separated from the evidence and reasoning that produced it. Assumptions disappear. Operating context gets simplified. Field findings never make it back into the record. The workbook is designed to keep that context moving with the work, so the decision can be understood, executed, verified, and improved rather than merely completed. SEVEN STAGES From first signal to learning. 01 See It 02 Challenge It 03 Plan It 04 Hand It Off 05 Do the Work 06 Verify It 07 Learn From It A QUESTION WORTH KEEPING What would be lost if the next person received only the work instruction? That question gets to the heart of the workbook. The goal is not more paperwork. It is preserving enough context for the next person to understand what evidence mattered, what assumptions were made, what the plan depends on, and what should be learned from the result. WHAT THE RECORD PRESERVES Context that should survive execution. Evidence & assumptions What was observed, what was believed, and where uncertainty remained. Operating context & rationale Why the decision made sense under the conditions that existed at the time. Execution readiness & field findings What the work required and what the field actually revealed. Verification & learning Whether the result worked, what changed, and what should inform the next decision. CONNECTED DECISION SYSTEM The workbook is the execution layer, not the starting point. Use it after the technical issue is understood, the changed condition is challenged, and credible options have been compared. Understand Challenge Compare","type":"Resource"},{"url":"/resources/when-asset-condition-changes/","title":"When Asset Condition Changes | 10 Questions for a Defensible Decision | SOUBEL","h1":"When Asset Condition Changes","description":"A practical SOUBEL leadership guide for testing evidence, challenging interpretation, comparing options, and defining what would change a decision when asset condition or performance shifts.","headings":"Better decisions start with better questions. | Ten questions to carry into the decision. | What changed? | Can we trust the evidence? | What was happening when it changed? | What other evidence agrees — or disagrees? | What does it mean for intended function? | What happens if our interpretation is wrong? | What legitimate options exist? | Why is one option preferable now? | What would cause us to change the decision? | How will we know the decision worked? | From leadership decision to working record. | When Asset Condition Changes | Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook | Condition means more when context travels with it.","text":"SOUBEL · Resources · Asset Performance PRACTICAL LEADERSHIP GUIDE When Asset Condition Changes 10 Questions for a Defensible Decision. A practical framework for moments when asset condition, performance, or evidence changes and a decision has to be made before certainty is available. Download the 3-page guide All SOUBEL resources WHY IT EXISTS Better decisions start with better questions. A changed signal does not automatically mean the asset changed. A technically plausible interpretation is not automatically the right business decision. And a decision that was reasonable yesterday may need to change when new evidence arrives. This guide provides a concise sequence for testing the signal, challenging the interpretation, understanding consequence, comparing legitimate options, and defining what evidence should trigger reconsideration. THE FRAMEWORK Ten questions to carry into the decision. The web version makes the framework visible and scannable. The downloadable guide provides the printable working format. What changed? Define the observation precisely: signal, trend, inspection finding, field observation, test result, model output, or performance shift. Can we trust the evidence? Check source, quality, completeness, calibration, timing, lineage, assumptions, and whether the apparent change may reflect the data rather than the asset. What was happening when it changed? Establish operating mode, load, pressure, temperature, throughput, environment, recent work, process changes, and other relevant context. What other evidence agrees — or disagrees? Integrate inspection, integrity, maintenance, process, field, laboratory or metallurgical, historical, and comparable-asset evidence where applicable. What does it mean for intended function? Connect condition to asset duty, criticality, degradation or failure context, reliability, availability, and performance. What happens if our interpretation is wrong? Challenge potential safety, environmental, regulatory, production, reliability, financial, reputational, and other material consequences. What legitimate options exist? Consider continued operation, monitoring, additional evidence, inspection, operating changes, escalation, mitigation, repair, deferral, replacement, redesign, or specialist analysis. Why is one option preferable now? Compare risk, cost, performance, timing, uncertainty, downtime, resource constraints, remaining life, and reversibility. What would cause us to change the decision? State assumptions, contradictory evidence, information gaps, thresholds, triggers, and escalation conditions. How will we know the decision worked? Define ownership, follow-up evidence, effectiveness measures, verification, record updates, and learning. USE IT WITH From leadership decision to working record. The guide and workbook address different parts of the same problem: making the decision, then carrying evidence and learning through execution. EXECUTIVE DECISION GUIDE When Asset Condition Changes Use the ten questions to challenge assumptions, understand consequence, compare options, and define what new evidence would change the decision. Download guide WORKING DECISION RECORD Reliability & Maintenance Decision Workbook Carry asset evidence, professional judgment, uncertainty, execution context, field findings, verification, and learning from the first signal through completed work. Download workbook Purpose: These tools support better questions and more defensible decisions. They do not replace applicable standards, regulations, procedures, licensed engineering judgment, qualified technical authority, safety requirements, or legal advice for a specific asset or decision. CONNECTED KNOWLEDGE Condition means more when context travels with it. Explore the SOUBEL Knowledge Library for the broader asset-performance framework behind condition, context, reliability, lifecycle economics, evidence quality, and decision confidence. Condition & Context → Reliability & Maintenance → Lifecycle Economics →","type":"Resource"}];
